      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD ELECTION.-TO THE BURGESSES OF THE WARD.</p>
        <p>LADIES &amp; GENTLEMEN,</p>
        <p>A vacancy has occurred in the representation of your Ward owing to the well-deserved elevation to the Aldermanic Bench of my friend Mr. James Evans.</p>
        <p>On many' occasions I have been requested by Burgesses of all shades of political and religious opinion to offer myself as a Candidate for a seat in the Town Council. Up to now I have declined these invitations, but so strong has been the pressure put upon me in regard to the present vacancy, that 1 feel it is my duty to offer my ser- vices to my old constituents, in the belief that such ability and experience as I possess may yet be of use in the Municipal administration of my native Town.</p>
        <p>It is impossible to place before you in detail in this address, my views upon all the important questions associated with the control of the affairs of the Borough, but I feel strongly that the enormous amount of the Ratepayers' Capital which is invested in the various undertakings of the Corporation and the further large expenditure which is contemplated in various di rections,dernands the careful and efficient oversight of those accus- tomed to large commercial concerns. If you elect me, I shall do my utmost to secure for the Town, economical and efficient management in each department while at the same time I shall support such a policy as will offer every reasonable inducement to manufacturers to extend their operations and so provide remunerative aud addi- tional employment for our rapidly increasing population. The last Report of the Medical Officer of Health discloses such a condition of affairs in regard to the general health of the town and especially as to the excessively high infantile death rate, as calls for prompt and effective treatment,</p>
        <p>ALL DISEASE WHICH IS PREVENTIBLE OUQIHT TO BE PREVENTED.</p>
        <p>CHILD LIFE appeals to all that is best in us for its protection. To questions of this nature I would give my most serious and earnest consideration. To WORKING MEN—With so many of whom I ana proud to claim intimacy of personal friendship— the question of HEALTH IS OF THE IlicilrEsT IMPORTANCE. HEALTH IS THE WORKMAN'S CHIEF—IF NOT HIS ONLY ASSET and therefore it is imperative that those to whom is entrusted the administration of the Health Department of ths Town. should be supported in every logiilmate whia they may make for the improvement of the general conditions under which the great mass of our industrial popula- tion are compelled to live.</p>
        <p>As you are aware, I have been actively engaged for many years in the protection of the rights of the Traders of Warrington in regard to the Naviga- tion of the River Mersey and the litigation connected with which, having so satisfactorily terminated, has made it possible for Warrington to becor4e in a few years' time a most important centre for the receiving and distribution of sea-borne traffic. _</p>
        <p>I regret:that the short time at my disposal will prevent me from calling personally upon you, hitt STANDING AS I DO AS AN ABSOLUTELY INDEPEZ- DENT CANDIDATE I appeal with confidence for your support as your fellow citizen and as a large Ratepayer and Employer of labour, both in the Ward and in its immediate vicinity, and as one who is deeply interested in all that makes for your happiness and p rosperity. In conclusion I promise that if elected, I will do the best I can to justify a renewal of your confi- dence and as far as lies in my power, to promote the wellbeing of the Burgesses of the Whitecross Ward in particular and of the Town at large. I am Ladies and Gentlemen, Yours faith fully, HENRY ROBERrS. •</p>
        <p>" Kinsley " Hill Cliffe, Warrington. 24th June, 1909.</p>
      </div>
