      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>COUNTY PALATINE OF CHE°TER. XTOTICE IS HEREBY GIVEN, that the next 11 GENERAL QUARTER SESSIONS of the Peace for the COUNTY PALATINE OF CHESTER will be holden at the CASTLE OF CHESTER, in and for the said County, on MONDAY, the 28th day of JUNE,. 1909, at Half-past Eleven o'clock in the Forenoon, when the Grand Jury will be empannelled. The hearing of all Appeals arising within the Petty Sessional Divisions of Broxton, Chester, Castle, Dares-- bury, Eddisbury, Nantwich, Runcorn, Winsford, and Wirral, forming the Chester District, and the trials of all prisoners committed front the last-mentioned Divisions, will take place at such Sessions. The Grand and Petty Juries for the Chester District must be in attendance at the Castle of Chester, at the Opening of the Court on MONDAY, the 28th day of June, 1909, at Half-past Eleven o'clock in the Forenoon. THE COURT will be Adjourned to the COURT HOUSE, KNUTSFORD, on WEDNESDAY, the 30th DAY OF JUNE, 1909, at Eleven o'clock in the Forenoon.</p>
        <p>The bearill of all Appeals arising within the Petty Sessional Divisions of Altrincham, Bucklow, Dukinfield, Middlewich, Sandbach, Congleton, Northwich, Prestbury and Stockport, forming the Knutsford District, and the trials of all prisoners committed from such Divisions will take place at such Adjourned Sessions. The Grand and Petty Juries for the Knutsford District must be in attendance at the Court House, Knutsford, at the Opening of the Court on WEDNESDAY, the 30th day of June, 1909, at Eleven o'clock in the Forenoon. REGINALD POTTS, Clerk of the Peace for the County of Cheater.- Chester, 14th June, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE DISMISSAL OF MARRIED FEMALE TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>Threatened Chancery Action.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday even- ing, Councillor J. Fulton-Smith presiding. There were also present :—Mr. H. Roberts (deputy-chairman), Aldermen Bennett and Smethurst; Councillors Dirties, Dickenson, Lord, Pemberton and Sankey; Revs. James Davies, Canon Stevenson, N. Wilson and ;1. Yong° ; Messrs. T. P. Bradshaw, A. J. Jolley, C. E, Parker, E. Plinston, C. H. Unsworth and G. Walker; J. Moore Murray (director), R. B. Roberts (assistant director).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LATE ALDERMAN ELLTSON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE ALDERMAN ELLTSON.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Ellison wrote tbanking Oa Committee for the vote of condolence passed with Mrs. Ellison and family on their bereavement. CONFERENCE EXPENSES SURCHARGED.</p>
        <p>The Finance Sub-committee reported having received the report of the District Anditor of the Local Govern. raent Board upon his audit of the committee's accounts -ior the year ended on the 31st March last, and it was -noted that he had taken exception to certain payments, and had surcharged the expenses incurred by the Head- master of the School of Art in attending, on behalf of the committee, the International Art Congress in London. It was noted that an appeal was being made by the Law Clerk to the committee to the Local Government Board against this surcharge, and the Director was instructed to write to the Board of Education calling their attention to the items objected to and to seek their intervention with the Local -Government Board with a view to such expenditure being allowed in the future. Mr. JoLLEY moved the confirmation of the minutes of the committee, and said that, although be held strong opinions on one or two aspects of the question, as it was partly subjudice at the present moment.it would be inexpedient to embark on any discussion upon it. 'there were questions of principle and detail involved of con- siderable importance. THE ASSISI'ANT DIRECTOR'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Finance Sub-committee resolved that the salary of Mr. Robert B. Roberts, assistant director of education, be increased from £2OO to £220 per annum as from the Ist July, 1909. Mr. JOLLEY said it gave him great pleasure to recommend that the minute be passed. and he hoped it would commend itself to the whole of the members of the Education Committee as it did to the Finance Committee. (Hear, hear). The minute was a recognition of very good services rendered by an efficient officer. (Hear, hear). Councillor SANKEY seconded, and the minutes were agreed to. HEAD MISTRESSES' QUALIFICATIONS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon STRVP.NSON, alluding to the School Management Committee's decision that from July Ist, 3912, no teacher shall be appointed headmistress of an infants' department or school unless she holds the certificate of the National Froebel Union, said he hoped the regulation would add very materially to the efficiency of their infant school. The teachers of these schools did not always receive quite as much profes- sienal Iraining in the special direction necessary as other teachers did, and the excellent examination held by the Froetel Union necessitated such a course of study on the part of those who presented themselves for it that it could not fail to render them very much more efficient in the discharge of their duties as mistresses of infants schools. The Committee had given a reason- able notice that they would require any teacher who aspired to the position of head mistress of an infant school to bold the certificate of the Union. In answer to the Rev. John Yonge, Canon STEvEssoN ,fitnid the resolution woul4 ii9t apply to headmistresses ,already appointed. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SIZE OF CLASSES.</head>
        <p>THE SIZE OF CLASSES.</p>
        <p>The School Management Sub-Committee considered the Board of Education's communication as to the intended revision by the Board in the code for 1900 of the regulations affecting the staffing of the public elementary schools and the size of the classes therein, ancla report upon the subject which had been prepared by the Director of Education having betn received, it was resolved (a) that this committee, whilst recogniz- ing the educational advantage which will accrue from these changed regulations, desire to approach the Board of Education with a view to the regulation Tequiring that the •nun.ber of scholars on the register of any class shall not exceed 60, being so modified as to provide that at no time during the school year shall the average attendance in any class for any one week exceed 60; and (b) that the Association of Education Committees be asoed to support the Committee's action in this matter.</p>
        <p>Canon STZVENSON said tlat with the recommendations of the circular everyone who had the good of education at heart would be in cordial agreement, but if all that it required was fully carried ont,i t would add materially to the cost of education in the town, and the resolu- tion asked that they might be let down a little easily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BEAMONT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>BEAMONT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON informed the Committee that the most excellent and satisfactory reports had bren received from H.M. Inspector, after a visit to this school. (hear, hear). MISS Frtosrs SERVICES.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY, alluding to the resignation of Miss Elizabeth Frost, senior assistant mistress 2.t the Secondary School and Technical institute, said she had performed her duties so well that the minute ought not to be allowed to pass without comment. It would be difficult to find a lady so qualified tor the position. Canon STEVENSON said Miss Frost had done her work well, faithfolly, and conscientiously, and had gained the affection of the pupils. She had exercised a good influence, especially over the girls in the school, and they all regretted the loss of her services. They -wished her all success in her married life. (Hear, hear)</p>
        <p>MARRIED LADY TEACHERS. THREATENED LEGAL PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Management Committee, the Director reported that he had received a letter from the Law Clerk to the committee, forwarding copies of communications received by him from a firm of solicitors, intimating their intention to commence pro- ceedings in the Chancery Division.of the High Court of Justice, on behalf of Mrs. Lydia Mary Stansfield and Dirs. Catherine. Grounds, against the Local Education Authority in respect of the termination of the engage- ments of those teachers. The sub-committee passed the following resolution:—"That the Law Clerk to the Com- mittee he. instructed to take the necessary step to defend the Local Education Authority in the proceed- ings referred to."</p>
        <p>The School Management Sub-committee also -reported that in connection with the permission given for the appointment of a head mistress lor the girls' department of the Fairfield School, the Director as instructed to inform the man- nsers of the committee's intention to advertise the position and to submit the applications received to the managers for their consideration. It was also noted that applications would also be submitted to the managers of the other schools mentioned with a view to the filling of the positions named.</p>
        <p>Capon STEVENSON, alluding to this question, said the Education Committee knew his views on the matter, and he would offer no comment at that stage. Alderman BICNNETT said he did not intend to open a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>iVi,•THE.P2</head>
        <p>iVi,•THE.P2</p>
        <p>tg McDOUGALL'S Pewit SELF-RAISING FLOUB IS Tl-11-17-ST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>BUILDING INDUSTRIES, HOUSE RENTS, AND FREE TinDE.</head>
        <p>THE BUILDING INDUSTRIES, HOUSE RENTS, AND FREE TinDE.</p>
        <p>There are no importations into this country from abroad of ready-made houses or other buildings. Consequently no possible tariff duties upon importa- tions from abroad could benefit the hding trade by way of making employment more regular or raising wages. _ .</p>
        <p>All the same, however, under the Tariff Reform policy, • workers in the building trade would have to pay at least 5 per cent. more than at present for their food, and an uncertain percentage more for their clothing and other necessaries. The building industry is one of the largest in the kingdom; and the population employed in it increases more rapidly than the total population of the king- 4m. The 1901 Census shewed 1,336,000 persons employed in " building and IN% rks of construction." In 1801 the number was only 956.000. That is, .the trade grew about 40 per cent. in ten years. In this year, 1909, therefore, it will probably be employing very considerably over 1,500,000 workers. All the British textile industries put together do - not give occupation to nearly so large a number. Further, we must note that whilst in the ‘textile industries female workers predominate, the building trade is a!most entirely a masculine occupation. Thus the number of persons dependent upon it for sustenance—when wives and children of workers are included—is even larger, proportionately, than is indiCated by the num- ber engaged in it. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>DRAWBACK OF THE TRADE</head>
        <p>A DRAWBACK OF THE TRADE</p>
        <p>As most people are aware, the quantity of unemploy- ment, or irregular employment, in the building trade is considerably larger than the average for all trades. One obvious reAson is that solnuch depends on the weather. This drawback is, doubtlesss, mote or less recognised, on the average, and provided for in the scales of wages. Again, the industry, distributed as it is all over the kingdom, is peculiarly liable to local variations—briskness in one district at the same time as depression in another. As the workers can *move about these variations are partly compensatory—but only in part ; and moving from one district to another means, of course, an unemployed interregnum. More seriously and exceptionally, however, the building trade is liable to larger alternations, of " boom" and.of deep depression than other trades are. These are due to the,average state of the other indus- tries of the country, and the consequent mood, for the time being, of speculative capitalists. If the trade in general of the country seems promisipg, then the speculative building trade " booms," and there is full employment If the general trade outlook is dark, then new building enterprise is almost stopped. and ordinary work of maintenance ;and repair is reduced to the utmost.</p>
        <p>As a result of these causes, continuous expeKience has shown that, at any time when there are general complaints of unemployment, statistical inquiries prove that the building trade workers are a dispropor- tionately large percentage of the unemployed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IS TARIFF REFORM A REMEDY?</head>
        <p>IS TARIFF REFORM A REMEDY?</p>
        <p>The most sanguinely promising Tariff Reformers tight rather shy of the problem of unemployment in the building industries. They'may maintain with some show of plausibility that employment in the home textile industries will be " steadied " by shutting out foreign textiles ;, and employment in the metal trades by checking imports of foreign metal manufactures, though even in these instances the experience of Protectionist countries is against them. But as regards the building industries —inasmuch as these are necessarily carried on wholly within the country---the Tariff Reform cure for unem- ployment has not even the plausible show of a case. It has .been argued traly, indeed, that if Tariff Reform policy " steadies" employment in other industries the building trade must gain a sort of reflected steadiness. Workers in the building trade, it maybe' safely predicted, will be little geateful for this highly problematical and second-hand promise of benefit to themselves, as compared with the overflow- ing gains that Tariff Reformers allege they can guarantee in other industries. Tariff Reformers have nothing at all useful to Fay upon the main drawback of the building trade as before mentioned—the larger eras of over-activity and of depression due to varied money market conditions and the passing temper of speculative capitalists. The worst extremes oethese trades tides originate in the United States, the chosen land of Protectionism, where the building trade fluctuations are notoriously even more violent than in this country. The subsidiary effects of the great American trade waves spread to this country. and others. No Tariff devices could check them. On the contrary, probability and experience alike -point to the conclusion that -Tariff barriers aggravate the extremes, whilst Free Trade ameliorates them, precisely because it erects no barrieis to narrow the channels of trade. but leaves all the space open for the free flow of the trade tides.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>ALL LOSS AND NO GAIN,</head>
        <p>ALL LOSS AND NO GAIN,</p>
        <p>Thus, so far as regards the million and a half of building trade workers, the Tariff Reform programme is all taxation, with no compensatory chance of profit to builders by the taxation of other industries. The threatened losses to the building trade are : I. The food and other related Tariff taxes. 2. A special Tariff tax on an important raw material of the building trade-7-namely, foreign timber. The chief reason for this proposal so far as can be gathered is to give" preference" to the Canadian timber trade—a trade which is surely prosperous enough already, without the compara- tively trifling gain that could accrue to it at the expense of working folk in the Mother Country. 3. Subsidiary Tariff taxes on such imported materials as glass, tiles, tools, metal-work of the varied kinds used in building, paper, paints, and varnishes.</p>
        <p>'lite single pretence of a contra-item proffered by Tariff Reform on the one side is—more employment for joiners, by the taxation of imported ready-made doors, window-frames, and the like. It is perhaps sufficient answer to remark that, without any artificial hindrance, the imports of these commodities into this country have gone down by more than one-half in the last twenty years; namely, from :U48,000 per annum to 3:223,000 per annum. Compared, with the vast amount Of carpentry and joinery work in the Kingdom, this trifle of importation, if stopped altogether, would make no difference perceptible to the wood-working industries—which, of , course, are but a moderate section of the total of building trades.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>HIGHER HOUSE RENTS.</head>
        <p>HIGHER HOUSE RENTS.</p>
        <p>The only way in which the building trade workers might be practically compensated is a way which Tariff Reformers do not care to enlarge upon. The workers might possibly insist upon higher wages.* In this matter the absence of foreign competition would be their strong point. Textile workers_ or metal workers have to keep their wage demands down, in some degree, Under the threat that their trade may go abroad. The building trade cannot be appropriated by the foreigner; and there could, therefore, be no such answer to the building trade workers' demands.</p>
        <p>Their rise in wages, of course, if obtained, would have to be paid, ultimately, by the consumers who, in this case, are the tenants of the houses and other buildings. The result would be higher rents. Higher rents, .of course, would have the effect of checking building operations, and so reducing employment. And here we have the short but sufficient reason why Tariff Reformers do not publicly point out to the building trade workers where their ultimate com- pensation must be found for their losses by Tariff Taxation.</p>
        <p>*There is no reason why they should not do this at any favourable moment now under Free Trade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Lancashire Cup Draw.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Lancashire County Union was held at the Albion Hotel, Lancashire, OD Tuesday evening. The statement of accounts showed a balance in band of £328, and was adopted. The British Playing Fields Society's scheme of purchasing and taking fields an lease for the purpose of fostering junior foolLall was approved, and it was decided to give it the necessary support. The draw for the first round of the Lancashire Senior Cup, to be played on October 16, was made as follows: Leigh v. Barrow, Widnes v. St. Helens, Rochdale Hornets v. Oldham Wigan v. Warrington. Byes: Broughton Rangers, Salford, Swinton, and Runcorn. The County Chnrnoionship match with Cumberland was fixed to be played in Lancashire on October 4, and the Yorkshire match in Yorkshire on November 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
