        <p>BROUGHTON RANGERS CLUB.</p>
        <p>The talsnce-sheet of the Broughton Rangers Northern Union Football Club shows a loss of almost £3CO on laq season's working. fhe total receipts were .£3.327 15s. sd, and the expenditure amounted to £3,628 Se. 4d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ROCHDALE HORNETS.</head>
        <p>ROCHDALE HORNETS.</p>
        <p>Tbe directors of the Rochdale Hornets Football Club report a profit of ,£BO 'on last season's working deepite losses of £3O on the Australian match, £4O on the Ebbw Vale match, end 1-'8 on the baud concerts. The adverse balance now steads at £3.11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>discussion on tbe question—(hear,bear)—but it struck him that the resolution with reference to the appoint- ment of teachers in place of the married female teachers who had been given notice of dismissal was rather" too previous." One war familiar with the law's delay and it was conceivable that the decision of the Court might not be given until the appointments had been made. If the Court decided they had no power to dismiss the teachers, the committee might be placed in an invidious positon—a position they ought to endeavour to avoid.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : I think it is a purely legal matter and that it would be unfair to onrselyes to discuss it. Alderman BENNETT said be had no desire to argue the merits of the case, and if the Chairman thoupht there w4s the slightest danger of the matter being mis- construed he would not say another word.</p>
        <p>Mr. LNswoitTli said he quite agreed with Alderman Ecnnett that the resolution ought not to be passed, but deferred, because if the Court granted an injunction the Education Committee would be unable to curry out the arrangement with reference to the new mistresses.</p>
        <p>The CHATRMAN : The schools must go on. That is where the difficulty arises. I think it would be much better not to discuss the matter to•night. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>'Mr. Usswownt : We ore not discussing the legal question. We are discus.ing the question of appoint- ing someone in Mrs. Stanstield's place which is quite a separate matter.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. ROBERTS said he wished to say, with all deference to one or two of the speakers, that the reso- luticn with reference to the new teachers was part and parcel of the step that had to be taken to carry out the decision of the committee from a legal standpoint. The DIRECTOR: It does not follow from the resolu- tion that the appointments will be made. You are putting yourselves in a proper position with the managers.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERTS said he thought that, in justice to that authority, it ought to be pointed out that that there VCRS another course which might have been adopted—a simple, inexpensive course —which would have cost the persons concerned a penny postage stamp and possibly two or three sheets of writing paper, but that had been absolutely discarded with the object of bluffing the authority into a position of funk. The people who had taken that course bad made a great mistake. As far as he understood there was no desire on the part of the authority to do anything but what was legally and morally right,and, seeing that the Board of Education was constituted the Court of Appeal in matters of this kind, and as it cost nothing to go to that Court, it was little short of a scandal that the persons concerned on the other side had not in the first instance availed themseßes of the opportunity that as thus afforded them all. (Hear, hear). Alderman BENNETT said be was under the impression that the Board of Education had declined to give any opinion. _ _</p>
        <p>-Mr. ROBERTS: No. - They have not been asked. Councillor PEMBERTON said the School Management Committee had intimated their desire ro act in a friendly way, and be felt with Mr. Roberts that the step which had been taken was little tab ortof a scandal. If an appeal had been made to the Board it would have cost the authority nothing. It seemed to him from what be had heard, that the local people were not Go much reeponsible for the proceedings as the London people, The minutes of the sub-committee were confirmed' MR. PLINSTON'S NEW OFFICE.</p>
        <p>The confirmntion of the minutes of the School Attendance Committee WAS propose i by Nit% PIANsro N. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. BRADSHAW FPconded and said Mr. Plinston's modesty would not sllow him to state tbat he had been elected Chairman of the School Attendance Committee. Ile was one of the senior members and one of the most reaular :menders. He had all the necessqry details at his finger ends and would make a most excellent chair- man. (Hear, heir). _ _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN Sdid Mr. Plinston's apprenticeship fully qualified him for the appointment.</p>
        <p>Tfieininutes were carried: s DECORATION OF SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON moved, and Mr. WALKER seconded the adoption of the Sites and Buildings Committee minutos.</p>
        <p>lir. UPSON said he would like to take that opportunity of putting a question to Councillor Yember,ton. (I.aughter.) Could he tell thin h6-er.lll:equently schOols were decorated ? It seemed to him that some names appeared on the list year by year and in other cases two or three years went by during which no money, was spent. _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. BRADSHAW also asked forsome information with regard to the following resolution :—" A letter was read from the managers of the Hamilton-street School asking the Committee to re-consider their regulation under which the proportion of the cost of the annual cleaning and decorating of the school is claimed by the committee from the managers, hut the Committee could not see tbeir way to take any action in the matter. lite Director was insttncted to suggest to the managers that they should appeal to the Board of Education upon the subject." He wished, as a manager of the school, to know the reason for their action, and why the managers of Hamilton-street school should be charged one-seventh of the cost of painting and decorating, seeing that they did not use the school at all on Sundays. Councillor PEMBERTON said the Borough Surveyor went round the schools and it was, of course, the duty of the Education Committee to clean them when required. In answer to Mr. Bradshaw, the DIRECTOR said Harnilton-street school was certainly not used on Sunda!-s, but it was there and could be used by thr managers if they wished. The school bad to be kept in proper condition, and whether it was used or not, a certain propottion of the cost must be contributed to the managers. _</p>
        <p>The miiiutes were carried. NEW SCHOOL MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee the consideration of the appointment of a selected member of the Education Committee in place of Miss Williams was deferred. Councillor Dickenson was appointed representative manager of the Heathside School, in place of Miss Williams, resigned. Coun- cillor Pemberton was appointed representative Manager of the Latchford St. James' C.E. School in place of the late Alderman Luke Ellison.</p>
        <p>The minutes were paqeed, on the motion of the CHAIRMAN, seconded by the DEPUTY-CHAIRMAN.</p>
      </div>
