      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>[By Redcap.]</p>
        <p>THE TURKEY,</p>
        <p>The breeding Season is now too far advanced for me to recommend my readers to make a start with turkeys. But those who have already done so and others who are thinking of purchasing young stock may find much useful information in this column. It is however, no use making a start with turkeys at all unless you occupy a-warm and sheltered position. I would therefore first of all point out that the rearing of turkeys for profit can only be recommended to farmers whose conditions and accommodation are right. Of course where. there are no conveniences for rearing, a few turkey poults could be bought and fatted up for market purposes or home consumption. • Ido not wish to go into a close description of the turkey; but rather to show and to assist in making it plain that this much neglected branch of poultry farming can be made to pay. A few statistics will therefore help my readers to realise this fact. In one of the London markets during the season of 1898-9 no less than 1,070,000 foreign turkeys had passed therein; 100,000 from Canada, 20,000 from America; in one month alone, 200,000 from Italy; Russia (from November to February) 50,000; Hungary (December to April) 200,000; France (during the full season) 500,000. This only goes to show that much of this foreign " dumping " might be reduced if a more exten- sive culture in our own borders were prevalent. A glimpse into any of our local markets and poulterers' windows at Christmas time, proves much foreign pro- duction and the scarcity of home produce. lam ready to admit that the rearing of young turkeys is somewhat difficult and that everything depends on very careful management. But at the same time when proper knowledge is acquired difficulties seem to die. Suitable</p>
        <p>ACCOMMODATION</p>
        <p>then is the first road to success and if you have not got this I would advise you not to entertain the id4a at all. Damp runs and confined areas dre both very detrimental to turkeys and they become victims to such much sooner than any other class of poultry. For a turkey chicken to get a thorough wetting spells disaster and unless you Can keep a batch of chickens warm and dry for the first two months of their exist- ence they will do very little good. The critical period is, as I have pointed out, the first two months or until the nipples on the head and neck develop by swelling, then changing in colour to a dark, a light purple, and afterwards a very bright red. During this period at least the young ones should have ample sheltering accommodation from the hot sun, cold winds and rain. Given plenty of invigorating food together with careful attention until they, have developed into young turkeys or poults you. will experience little difficulty afterwards. Because, after this stage, if supplied with artificial shelter they will 'make good use of it during inclement weather.</p>
        <p>!lOUS [MG</p>
        <p>turkeys is not an expensive item. In fact if you could be sure that no foxes would molest your farm in the early morning when the birds were foraging, then they might roost in the trees. On the other hand a large open fronted shed (providing the opening faced the south) would make ample housing accommodation with just a wire-netting door to keep out foxes or dogs. Just allow me to remind you that- while the turkey chicken is one that needs care during the first period, it is fairly hardy when fully developed, as you will derive froth the fact 'that open roosting quarters are recommended. Keep them always free from damp runs and confined areas.</p>
        <p>FEEDING</p>
        <p>the young birds is a matter fO; care during the first two months. Many different foods and methods of feeding are recommended. But in these days of scieutific research we are very much assisted by having placed within our reach specially prepared- food-staffs which are well suited to feed and rear young turkeys with very little difficulty. I have no hesitation in advising my readers to take full advan- tage of such foods, the results of which are decidedly satisfactory. Take a good sample of biscuit-meal and add sparingly some bruised hemp seed; also add green food which should consist of lettuce, dandelion leaves, and young onions chopped .very fine. Nettles and parsley are also a wholesome green food but as you cannot always get these the diet recommended will be found to be a good substitute. Give the drinking water in shallow pans and remove when they have had sufficient. When about three to four months old they will require less- food, as the young turkeys are rare foragers and if neara wood or on a free range will find rcost of their own food,—at any rate sufficient to keep them growing until the</p>
        <p>FATTEN' [NG process begins, which should be when about six months old: At this stage more nutritious food should be given to assist in final growth and forma- tion of flesh. If you have a large flock 1 would recommend the separation of the sexes. By so doing you will find that they fatten much more quickly. Unlike other classes of poultry, turkeys do not feed in too close confinement. The best method then to adopt is by utilising any disused stable, out-house, or shed into which has been placed a good depth of dry straw litter and provide them also with a perch. The following is a useful feeding diet : Boiled potatoes mixed with maize-meal one part, barley-meal one part, and a quantity of whole beans or bean-meal should be given twice daily. always removing what is left over. An iron feeding trough should be used as it is easily washed out after each meal. If in the least the food is sour or tainted, growth is retarded, because the birds will eat less readily. Vary the food as often as is convenient by leaving off, say the maize-meal and giving extra of one of the other ingredients and so on, thus keeping them on the move. When neat ing the time for killing, a system of "hand-cramming' should be resorted to. Balls of barley meal well moistened with skimmed milk should be given just before the birds go to roost. These they should be made to swallow, and it is not so diffi- cult as at first it might A seem. After preparing the meal balls, take the bird under your left arm, the legs resting on your knee and just open the mouth, place the ball in, then just close the beak for a second and you' will find how readily it will be "gobbled" down. After such a process of feeding they become exceedingly fat and plump, putting on flesh of a delicious flavour. Naturally the skin also is tender so that when</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DRESSING</head>
        <p>DRESSING</p>
        <p>-.„.. them for the market, care should be taken not to tear the skin as the flesh of a turkey looks very unsightly if the skin has been either tom or bruised. In con- clusion may I appeal to those of my readers who are blest with ample accommodation to assist as a matter of duty in reducing the foreign import of our Christmas turkey.</p>
        <p>- Next week I ,shall write on "The Sussex System of Fattening Poultry, etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LONGEST DAY.</head>
        <p>THE LONGEST DAY.</p>
        <p>Since shortest day and longest night Have we been circling to the While through the darkness, fog and rain, The Spring was coming back again.</p>
        <p>Though rough the winter's storms might blow, And deeply drift the falling snow; • Yet did the ever lengthening day Bring summer nearer on her way.</p>
        <p>If warmer days and gentle showers Unfolded soon the early flowers, And they were fated in their birth, Still others graced the budding earth.</p>
        <p>The grass that fills the meadows green And dancing in the wind is seen, Through chilly morning frost has passed With lovely flowers that bloom at last.</p>
        <p>The trees that hide the noontide glare With leaves that fan the sunny air, Both tall and spreading, young and old, Have felt the winter's bitter cold.</p>
        <p>But now the green old earth is blest And weleame,Summer is her guest, The shortest night, and longest day, Have brought her for a while to stay</p>
        <p>Oh summer, beautiful art thou, - Rich garlands grace thy stumy brew, The fairest we shall ever see, For naught on earth compares with thee,</p>
        <p>S. KENYON.</p>
        <p>Pabbleton. June, 1908.</p>
        <p>Long Live The King i,</p>
        <p>'an or Black,</p>
        <p>Kid .Bar Shoe 111+.</p>
        <p>'What shouts of Exultation greet this Exclamation! Of course, you will join the throng of loyal subjects to see your King and Queen. and, naturally, will desire to look your best. A pair of "BENEFIT" BOOTS or SHOES will give just that desir- able completeness to the Toilet of any Lady or Gentleman. Get a pair, to-day.</p>
