      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire League tofirney has reached a keenly, interesting stage and a great deal depends upon Warrington's match at Prescot to-day. At preSent Prescot hold the top position, having lost one •match out of seven, and Warrington who come next, have not lost one of their four matches. if Warring- ton art) victorious to-day their chances of the championship will be considered of the rosiest, but should Prescot come out triumphant they will be first favourities. The Warringtomans have given an excellent account of themselves so far and their supporters will wish them well in • to-day's important engagement.</p>
        <p>The following ,are the League tables up-to-date : FIRST DIVISION. Cub. P. W. L. D. Pts. Prescot Warrington St. Helens Recs. Earlestown St. Helens 4 ... 1 ... 2 ... 1 3 *Newton *Two points deducted for playing ineligible player.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Roes Warrington Prescot Earlestown St. Helens Newton</p>
        <p>5</p>
        <p>Warrington triumphed over Earlestown at Arpley on iturday, and that after taking a very unusual course. Although Mr. Silcock, the Warrington captain; was favoured; by the spin of the coin, he boldly allowed his opponents to take first turn at the wicket, and events funk justified this departure, Earlestown being dismissed for 113, while the Warringtonians piled on 191. In justice to the visitors, it must be mentioned that they were without the services of Marsh,.Unsworth and Alderson, whilst Warrington had a strong side doing duty. The most successful batstnen for Earlestown were i. Crawley (29) and F. M. Clare (25). Cresswell's deliveries caused the visiting players much trouble and he captured 6 wickets for 34 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington began their innings in aggressive style and fifty was hoisted as the result of twenty minutes play, boundary strokes being the order. J. Callon hit out in dashing 'style and gave Harrison a lively time, getting two 4's in succession. He was at length magnificently caught low down by Ltigh, after a splendidly compiled 30. W. Richardson was also in brilliant form, his 31 including some pretty late cuts, while his whole innings was characterised by sound judgment. The Rev. 11. Gray distinguished himself with a faultless 22, B. Silcock made a useful 20, and J. W. Pearson carried out his bat for a nicely compiled 18. •</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd are also going strongly aud are only a single point behind St. Helens Recs. Who head the League table. On Saturday they followed up a succession of victories by defeating Earlestown 2nd on their own ground by .26. Batting first Earlestown made 72, R. Allen being the only player to do any- thing out of the ordinary. His 34' Was knocked up in fine style. W. Bennett's bowling Was particularly effective, accounting for eight wickets for 36 runs. The feature of the Warrington innings was a fine stand by T. Houghton and H. Dolan who played splendid forcing cricket and scored 31 and 29 respectively, while W. A. Turton was-responsible for 16.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District, League champions, St. James's, are still unconquerable and on Saturday they trounced Wilderspool Brewery to the tune of 67 Fester and Holt were in grand form with the bat and each made .28, while Hallam and Holt howled with deadly effect, their respective analyses reading five wickets for eleven runs and two wickets for nine runs. For the Brewery, Hallows took four wickets for 21 rims and Lloyd three for 18.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists disposed of Sankey Wire Mills 2nd, by 29, the top scorer being 11. Gifford who knocked up 24. For the Wire Mills, F. Dooley gave a capital' display for his 17. Bladen and Bell each took four wickets for 24 runs, while E. Cliffe and Joseph Cox captured four for 23 and three for 25 respectively.</p>
        <p>There was an exciting contest I)etween R3land's and the" Guardian" office, the printers rUnning out winners by the narrow margin of three. Layfield (Rylands') made the highest score of 22, and J. Newton (12)was the only player who contributed double figures for the victors.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Lines Engineers triumphed over Walker's Brewery in a low-scoring match, W. Hill (12) being the solitary batsman to place double figures to his credit. For Walker's, Spicer took seven wickets for ten runs.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Junior League, Appleton and Strettoii entertained Rylands' 2nd and defeated them by 16 runs, "John Lloyd (12) and W. Fletcher (11) being top .scorers.</p>
        <p>Warrington St. Paul's had hard luck in not winning their friendly match with Ditton, the adverse margin being only four. The bowlers had decidedly the best of matters, E. Harrison for Ditton securing six wickets for 6 runs and Royle 4 wickets for 9.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills retain their unbeaten record, and on Saturday they inflicted a severe drubbing on Barnton. Going in first, Barnton were all dismissed for 79 and the Wire Mills ran up the substantial total of 105 with only 1.! wickets down. H. Brough gave a brilliant display, carrying out his bat for 54, and H. Hawthorn knocked up a splendid 29. T. Whitfield, who did good work with the ball for the Wire Mills, is ii Warringtonian at present on a visit \home from South Africa.</p>
        <p>The Municipal Officers' Guild suffered a reverse on Saturday, Hough Green being their vanquishers to the extent of 47 runs. R. Price did the best service with the bat for the Guild, his innings realising 19.</p>
        <p>Sankey defeated Bewseyi by 10 runs, Stevens (12) and Williams (10 not out) batting well.</p>
        <p>Lynnu made no mistake about their victory over Arley, the most striking feature of the match being a brilliant innings by J. Hampson, who retired after compiling 64. He has done signal service for the Lymm eleven this geason, and bids fair to run up a remarkable record. T. Marsh also gave a notable performance which realized 27. Oughtrington Park trounced Broughton 2nd in decisive style, N. Wood heading the scores with the line figure of 93. Oughtrington Park 2nd, however, were not equal to the task of disposing of Altrincham Wesleyans 2nd, who won by 21. Taylor captured five wickets for eight runs. •</p>
        <p>Warrington Adult School inflicted defeat on Bold- street Wesleyans, H. Humphries (21) and J. Mairs (20 not out) doing good service with the bat. The bowling of Potter (Adult School) and Bell and Utile (Bold-street) was particularly effective.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>r.fro scratch teams—Whitecross and L. and N.-W. —met last Saturday in the Works Bowling Competi- tition. Whitecross bad the best of the match, winning by. 39 points.</p>
        <p>A keenly contested game was witnessed between AV. Gleave (L. and N.-W.) and J. Appeton (Whitecross). They peeled at 3, but Appleton gained another 4 before his opponent could add a single. When Appleton was 10 his contestant was only 5, but Gleave increased his score to 8 while Appleton remained at 10. The Whitecross representative kept in front until he reached• 14, when with three successive woods, Gleave won. •</p>
        <p>The play between T. Horrabin (L. and N.-W.) and G. Warburton was of a very even character. .They were equal at 4 and 7, but Horrabin obtained a slight lead, keeping it until 12 was reached, when they both peeled. Warburton carried off the "colours" by the narrow' margin of 1.</p>
        <p>That J. Hutchinson (L. and N.-W.) and W. Gleave were unevenly matched was evident from the result of the game--2-15.</p>
        <p>Something, of a setisation was caused when J. Slavin (Whitecross) beat T. Appleby by 3 points. The latter had 9 to his credit whilst the former had only 2, and it looked as if Appleby were going to owin with plenty of room to spare. However, Slavin was determined to turn the tables and he steadily pulled</p>
        <p>up until they %ere equal at 11. Then came the critical moments. Appleay gained another one, which was followed up by two doubles from his opponent who, of course, registered a victory.</p>
        <p>It is not often that two brothers are found to be opposed to each other in the final of a bowling corn- petition,but such a coincidence Was witnessed in the final for the Greenall Challenge Cup, in connection with the Warrington District of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds, at Walton on Saturday. The brothers Twiss happened to be in this position and it is rather a pity that the two could not win, for their play right through the competition was of the best. Herbert carried off the trophy, after a keenly-fought contest, and he was loudly applauded for the skill with which he handled the woods.</p>
        <p>An interesting experiment was made on Saturday last by the Warrington and District Bowling Associa- tion, when for the first time an individual merit com- petition was held on llylands' green. The entries were •very gratifying •to the promoters. The most interesting game, of' the afternoon was between B. Jolley, of. Runcorn, and G. Hatton, of Whitecross Recreation, which after an exciting finish, resulted in a victory for Jolley by two points.</p>
        <p>A. Gleave, a well-known Warrington bowler, figured in the final of the Liverpool Merit Competition held last Tuesday evening. He made a plucky light, but his opponent Raiack beat him by 7 points.</p>
        <p>Bylands' No. I played and beat Longford No. 2 by 72 points. It will be seen, when it mentioned that Longford had an advantage over their opponents by .30, that Bylands team is by far the better one. - All the winning team's representatives achieved a victory except Miller who only lost to Bromley by 1.</p>
        <p>That the most exciting match of the contest was between J. Miller (Rylands') and H. Bromley (Long- ford), is a fact demonstrated by the result. Both players equalised at 4 and 5: Miller obtained a lead ,of .one, only to be beaten by his opponent who reversed the margin. However Miller played well Making his figures 14, whilst Bromley had to be content with S. Miller could get no further and Bromley steadily increased his figures, witming.by 1.</p>
        <p>T. Humphries (Rylands') had an easy task with Shaw, whom Ile beat •by 14.</p>
        <p>Mersey White Lead (25) lost to Fletcher's No. 1 (5) by 9 points. Most of the games were exciting. Fletcher's were well represente4, only three of their men failing to carry off the desired number-15.</p>
        <p>A good game was witnessed between J. Carter (Fletcher's) and A. Atkinson. Both peeled at 3 and later at 6. Atkinson reached 10 before his opponent could stir. However, Carter then ." had a little bit on his own," getting up to 9, whilst his contestant remained at 10. The Lead Work's man increased his total to 13, with Carter still standing at .9. With some more fine play they equalised at 13 and again at 14.1 t was a close slave, but Carter won and received well merited applause.</p>
        <p>Another well played game was that' between J. Vernon. (Fletcher's) and J. Webb, the former only coining off victor by one point. Nine swere recorded for the former, whilst the latter was only I,but with a fine effort Webb was able to peel at ins opponent's figures-9. Then excitement began to be aroused, and they again equalled at 12 and 13. Webb gained another one, but Vernon added two, thus winning by only a point.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LIVERPOOL BOTANISTS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL BOTANISTS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Visits to'the Museum and Thelwall.</p>
        <p>The second field meeting of the present session of the Liverpool Botanical Society was held on Saturday at Thelwal/ Roughs and the Warrington Museum. From Warrington the party, who were under the leadership of Mr. G. A. Dunlop, of the Warrington Museum, and included a number of members of the Warrington Field Club, journeyed to Thelwall. In a hedge near Thelwall Station an example of the true hawthorn, Cratwgus Oxyacanthoides—a rare shrub in South Lancashire—was encountered. This differs from the prevailing form, C. monogyna, in having a glabrous peduncle and calyx tube and two or three carpels. The Roughs yielded an interesting flora, the more noteworthy plants encountered including Saxifraga grantilata, 'tabus. • Lindleyantis, Cards mine amara, Equisetum maximum, and several Micro fungi. The Liverpool botanists were interested in the abundance of hornbeam and guelder rose, two species which are generally regarded as rarities in the neighbourhood of Liverpool. Two American oliens,Claytonia perfoliata and Claytonia sibiricit, were found near the station. Returning to Warrington for tea, a visit was then paid to the War- rington ,Museum, %there the members wore welcomed by Mr. Linumus Greening, P.L.S., chairman of the Museum Sub-committee.</p>
        <p>Many of the botanical treasures for which the Museum is noted were specially exhibited on this occasion in the lecture-room. Of particular interest were the collections and relics of William Wilson, the greatest of btrologists' and a native of Warrington. The original manuscript of his Bryologia Britaunica, a classic work on British mosses,was exhibited,as also the Wilson herbarium of flowering plants, and a selection of the Wilson collection of British mosses at the British Museum. An address on some of the features of - botanical interest in the Warrington Museum was delivered by Mr. G. A. Dunlop, assistant curator, who said the ,management were firm believers in the educational value of specimens, local objects and collections in particular. A special effort was made to secure the sympathy aud co-operation of local teachers and in this direction they had met with a ready response. Special object lessons for children, an ingeniously contrived topical wild flower table, a scheme og prizes, &amp;c., in conjunction with the active sympathy and co-operation of the teachers and Museum authorities, had done much to arouse a love of Nature in the children of the Warrington district. Nor were the interests .of older students and Nature lovers neglected, as evidenced by the operations of the Museum and Field Climb in other directions. In conclusion, the speaker emphasized the fact that the way to success in work of this kind depended very largely on securing the interest and sympathy of children and teachers, and in Warrington they were rapidly realizing the value of this policy. In proposing a vote of thanks to the Museum authori- ties, Mr. J. A. WHELDON, F.L.S. president of the Liverpool Botanical Society, remarked that the attitude of the Warrington Museum in fostering and promoting the study of botany and natural history was extremely gratifying, anti it was greatly to be regretted that in this direction Liverpool was so much behind. Dr. J. W. Euats, in seconding the vote (which was carried), said he always regarded the Warrington Museum—the botanical department in particular—as a model of what a museum should be, and the position of botany at the Liverpool Museum, and the attitude of the Museum authorities to teachers and local naturalists, were in comparison matters for regret. An interesting collection of botanical and natural history works of local and historical interest called for special notice. A further treat was provided by Mr. Charles Madeley, the curator, who delivered a short address on the collection, and concluded his remarks with an interesting historical sketch of the develop- ment of Warrington natural history, dealing briefly also with some of the pioneers in-this direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WHITE CITY.</head>
        <p>WHITE CITY.</p>
        <p>The. continued popularity of the White City at Manchester is manifested by the lunge crowds to be found there every afternoon and evening. By lovers of music the presence of high class military bands as afforded this season is especially appreciated, and the action of the manager in giving the afternoon and even- ing concerts under cover whenever the weather is inclement, and in the open air on other days, is a con- cession to the comfort of patrons characteristic of the White City. The younger people find much enjoyment in the huge skating rink and on the various devices that have for so long worked for the popularity of the White City. To-day (Saturday), a great Open Homing Pigeon Race will take place, and some 3,000 birds are expected to be liberated at three o'clock promptly. This will afford an exceptionally good opportunity for the public to witness a most beautiful spectacular flight of these graceful birds. Next Wednesday will he "Treasure Day" at the White City, and 76 cash certificates, vary- ing in value from half a crown to £5 will be distributed about the grounds And in the shows, and finders of these certificates can cash same at the Treasurer's Office of the White City immediately after picking same up. On the last" Treqsure Day" two weeks ago the Cashier paid out nearly £25 for these certificates, and the money that had been set aside for them after the certificates had been cashed SIMS at once turned over to charity.— Admission to the great" Fighting the Flames" Exhibi- tion is given free to children during the afternoon only, when accompanied by parents or guardians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>"A CUP OF COLD WATER."</head>
        <p>"A CUP OF COLD WATER."</p>
        <p>Among the wreaths placed on the grave of Field. Marshal Sir F. Haines, who was laid to rest at Brompton Cemetery on Wednesday, is one bearing the simple inscription "From the bugler at Alma who gave you a drink of water on the battlefield, and whom you remembered forty-four years after, when he was overtaken by mis- fortune."</p>
      </div>
