      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MANCHE,Vc.R</head>
        <p>MANCHE,Vc.R</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday,, June 26, 1909,</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] A- DOMESTIC AGENCY.</p>
        <p>By MARY ANGELA DICKENS,</p>
        <p>Author of "Cross Currents," "A Mere Cypher,' " A Valiant Ignorance.," " Prisoners- of Silence," "Against the Tide," "A Question of Temperament," &amp;c,</p>
        <p>"How she has time to waste over those flowers," said Miss Steward, thoughtfully, "when, as I believe, she has work to do, I must soy I do not understand."</p>
        <p>"The person opposite ? " inquired the you.nger Miss Steward, coining up to the window to help her sister lookout. The advertisement called the houses in St. Lucian's-road " commodi otis dwell ings with elegant bay windows and an excellent garden in front." The chief point of the excellent garden was that you coied if so minded easily open the front gate with the handle of your umbrella while you stood on the door step; this saved trouble, and was doubtless what the advertiAement really meant.. There was also a "spacious back garden. Space is relative, more or less,, and considered from the point of view of hanging out poekethand- kerchiefs to dly-St. Lucian's-road washed its handkerchiefs at home—it was spacious. But moat of the dwellers there had grasped a groat principle, namely, that you cannot have every- thing; and the exceedingly low rental,they frankly owned, was what they found most worth having.</p>
        <p>But the Misses Steward never condescended So far as to allow that they had any reason to think of such sordid causes. They lived in St. Lucian's- road because it was "so quiet and so central." So it was—if you make Hammersmith your centre; not otherwise. They also spoke of it as "so con- venient for the theatres," but they did not speak from experience. For the Misses Steward, though they Would probably have faced torture rather than say so, had no money for theatres or any other superfluities of life. . .</p>
        <p>Their house was No. 45, and that on which their eyes—ken and piercing—were fixed at eight o'clock on this June morning was No. 70, exactly opposite. The house was a fad, simile of every ether in the road in outline, but in detail there were differences, and the differences had attracted and concentrated the attention of the Misses Steward upon this, out of all the other houses of which they could—and •did—make an exhaustive study. Most of the excellent gardens grew chiefly gritty gravel or brownish ivy, but at NO. 70 a strip of real flower-bed ran across the little space, and just now it was filled with geraniums, which made a brave show of scarlet. The Vlindows had each a window-box, and over the window-box in the "elegant bay window" was bending the mistress of the house. "Yes, the person opposite," answered Miss Steward. "Though after all," she continued, "as to the ways of persons who are compelled to work,' thankful tb remember that neither you nor I, Fanny, have had occasion to know anything about them." • Miss Steward did not reflect that if she had known something about work her father, a poor veterinary surgeon might have been able to leave them more than a hundred and twenty pounds a-year between them. • "She has some regular employment, however, I am cOnvinced—indeed, the milkman told Bache"! so; what it is . I shall endeavour to discover." I shall ask the milkman to find out, would have been the plain English of these words —but which of us always speaks plain English? "And she goes somewhere to the moment every day. There, she is going now!" The woman they were watching at this instant pulled off a pair of gardening gloves and then bent once more over the box to give a lingering, loving touch to something in a coiner. The head bent over the flowers had plenty of dark but bright brown hair gathered into a pretty knot towards the top of her head. "Curly and coquettish and no cap," commented Miss Steward. mystically; "it ts to be hoped that it is long since she lost her husband, otherwise she shews little sense of it."</p>
        <p>"If she is a widow—he might be abroad ; or they might not have got on," said Miss Fanny, tentatively and euphemistically. "Fanny, make the coffee—this is an irreproach- able mighbourbood," said Miss Steward, severely, while her eyes were still fixed on No. 70, at the upper window of which she could see "the person opposite "'rutting a hat on her objection- able hair.</p>
        <p>Five minutes later she came down her garden path dressed in a long coat and a pretty bat— these offended Miss Steward as much as did her hair—and,- carrying a small bag, walked briskly down the road. As the road ran into omnibus routes at both ends her direction offered no clue, and Miss Steward, who had once observed to Miss Fanny that the Röntgen rays did not "seem to admit of such :wide or reasonable application as she had expected," would have thought them well used indeed if they could have shown her Mrs. Tressor—that was the name of the opposite person—getting into an omnibus and stopping it half-an-hour later at a side street going off a main thoroughfare in the south-west district.</p>
        <p>She walked a few yards down the side-street, aod stopped at a house where the door stood open and each lintel bore tram plates. The third on the right side was small and modest. "Mrs. Tressor Domestic Agency," were the words inscribed on it. And Mrs. Tressor, key in hand, went up the stairs and let herself into the front room on the second-floor, where the plate repeated its legend in white letters on the windows,, which, 'Ling legible in the street, presented a series of annoying hieroglyphics from the inside surely calculated to promote softening of the brain in the would-be reader. It was very simply, even poorly, furnished. The floor was covered with linoleum of a cheap and ugly pattern, and one or two wicker easy-chairs and some other chairs and.a small table were all the room contained except a large, neatly-arranged writing-table. This was the "somewh,re " of Miss Steward's wonder, and the Domestic Agency was the regular employment as to which she was going to question the milkman. She did question him that very. morning. But as all he could say was that he only knew that Mrs. Tressor "went out reg'lar and paid reg'lar,'' Miss Steward found the result of the inquiry dis- appointing. Mrs. Tressor took 'off the long coat and' the pretty hat and carried them into the back room. Then she stood a looking-glass on the mantel- piece and smoothed her pretty curly hair. The face the glass reflected was pretty too. Most people who came to the Domestic Agency went away saying "that woman has been pretty." i The keener-sighted few said "that woman s pretty." And i was true. Her cheeks were thin; there were lines that were certainly not brought there by her thirty odd years, and the whole outline of the face told of experiences of some sort—and not of a happy sort. But the lines could not spoil a very expressive mouth, and the thin cheeks had still a charming touch of colour, while the dark bide eyes were quite undimmed, and only the more attractive for their testimony to the fact that they had looked sorrow in the face.</p>
        <p>She made herself very neat and trim, put away the glass with care—she had a touch of superstition which shelved itself in trifles, and the thought of breaking a looking-glass was terrible to her Imagination—and 'went back into the front room which had the word "Office" printed in large letters on the door, and estab- lished herself at the large writing-table. On the desk before her was an enormous pile of letters. The Domestic Agency existed for the purpose of providing people with cooks and housemaids and servants generally, it is scarcely necessary to say that Mrs. Tressor's .time was fully taken up in that quest But there exist people everywhere who in their 'egotism are apt to believe that when they need any- thing wriatever all the agencies in the world do but exist to minister to their needs. Thus it happened that every day Mrs. Tressor's letters consisted to a great extent of applications from writers who made requests varying from a demand that she should at once "go to Whiteley's or somewhere where they keep•it ' and procure some fringe "which I cannot get though I have written everywhere," to a desire for a dachshund "which please, get Dog's Home, and despatch passenger train to me, no delay." The fact that this writer seemed to • imagine that she was despOching a telegram made her utterances somewhat cryptic as a whole. To-day them were more than usual of these wholly unreasonable correspondents among the writers of the morning's hitters. and Mrs. Tressor laid down the last of them,. dealing wlth a Unit - culty about the electric lighting of the writer's house in the country and ending in these words, "Asa as you arc au agency please tell me .4</p>
        <p>return who you recommend to see to it imme- diately—l can have no delay; stamped envelope enclosed—for all I have tried are so bad," with a little smile in which humour and weariness struggled together, and sorted the pile in order to answer them. Then she went through those letters dealing more immediately with her own braneo. of domestic arrangements, and laid those also beside her in neat order to answer them. Before beginning to do so she turned to a cOendar for every clay hanging at her side, and tore off the leaf of the day before. The date dig- played as she did so was "Wednesday, June Ist." As she took in the figure she started, the pen she had already dipped in the ink slipped from her fingers and lay unheeded on the floor, while the colour rushed suddenly over her thin face to the very'wavei or her hair, and faded again, leaving her very white. "I didn't remember it was so near," she said to herself, with a little gasp. "The same day of the week too! Mid 1 had forgotten that it was to-day ! "</p>
        <p>.11ei• eyes, which bad suddenly become very bright, M'ere in strong contrast to her very pale face. They were fixed on the shabby paint of the door leading into the back room, but they saw neither paint nor door, nor even the four walls of the Domestic Agency. A minute or two passed, and then .Mrs. Tressor's eyes lost that strange brightness, and she gave her bead a little shake and saw her everyday surroundings again.</p>
        <p>"One doesn't have one's chance twice when one has thrown it away," she said to herself, heavily. And then she threw her energies without a moment's delay ibto the answering of her Aters.</p>
        <p>"Mrs. TresSor, I suppose? I want a cook at once—at once!"</p>
        <p>'lt was an hour later, and a tatl wornan had entered the room with these words, and now s end in tiin middle of the floor looking as if she eepected Mrs. Tressor to produce the cook from the writing-table drawer. "I absolutely require her without a day's delnv," she added. lie'fore Mrs. Tressor, could even -comment on this two stout.wonien, evidently sisters, sailed in. 'You got a fairly satisfactory housemaid for me," said one, in a condescending tone which seemed to imply that it was a great kindness on her part to allow so much. "Now I want a, parlourmaid." "And I want the identical housemaid you got for my slater," struck in the other. This lucid and ungrammatical statement was considered to be enough, for both turned, sat down in the nearest chairs, and glared expectantly at Mrs. Tressor.</p>
        <p>"She must thoroughly understand entr(l,:,," maid the first woman, reverting to the question of her cook with a stately disregard of any other claims than her own.</p>
        <p>"You quite remember that cannot have fringes," said the lady of the parlourmaid, with the like disregard of the tall lady's existence. "My sister's housemaid had decided faults," said the sister of th. 3 padourmaid lady; "mine must have none."</p>
        <p>"I have a dinner-party to-night," went on the first corner, serenely.</p>
        <p>"It must be distinctly understood that there are n young "I stall expect her to be continually finding out, new dishes-"</p>
        <p>"She must never go cut--" "A kitehenmaid I never allow--" "Except between half-past three and four on Sundays:</p>
        <p>"INIOr beer." "A very obliging woman is what I require, of course--"</p>
        <p>"1 do not allow any.thing.---"</p>
        <p>•., As all the three voices made the last remark- able statement at once its conclusion was lest to Mrs. Trcssor, who stood, patiently, waiting until one or the other woman should conic to end of her requirements. The two stout sisters being somewhat breathless after this, she seized the opportunity to assure the tall woman that she could by no means produce a cook in time for the dinner-party of that evening, but would do her best, if necessary, to find a temporary sub- stitute in the course of the next few hours. The tall woman received this statement with an injured air, and after saying forcibly that she considered all agencies of any sort very badly managed, and that this identical one was the very worst in that respect that she had ever known, allowed herself to be 'half satisfied, and began with promptitude to go through the list of those details which were to her mind indis- pensable in the personality of a temporary cook, and then stalked out of the room. The stout sisters at once began a sisterly duel.for Mrs. -Tressor's first attention. While this raged other women came in, in varying moods and manners, sat down to wait with what patience they possessed, and stared wrathfully at the sisters. who were still talking 'both at once at the top of their voices. By about eleven o'clock their voices and their energies had given way a little, Ind they were persuaded to believe that Mrs. •Cressor understood what they severally- required her hands. They left the Agency commanding 'ser to supply them both with faultless ;servants without an hour's delay, and the little waiting zrowd instantly began .to press their claims. 3ehind one of the women composing it, a man 'had come in. He walked quietly .0 a corner of the room—it was dim everywhere, for Mrs. Tressor had let down the sun-blinds to shade the room from the noonday sun, but apparently his eyes could not bear anj, light, for he wore a shade over them —and seated himself there quietly. An hour passed quickly enough for Mrs. Tressor. She was engaged without intermission in attempts to soothe, to please, to satitify, to find the impossible for a succession of exacting and impatient women one after the other. If the time passed more slowly for the quiet man, he did not say so. He sat perfectly still in his corner of the room. .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Tressor had given him a cursory glance at his coming in, but no more, • for she had con- cluded that he was waiting for one of the women of the groupvvith whom he had entered ; and now, as all the rest were gone, she naturally concluded that he belonged to the last individual of it, with whom she was now dealing. She had felt no 'surprise at his entrance; very undecided women not seldom brought with them a tractable husband. Though they never allowed him to say a word, it is to be supposed that they felt a moral support- frcm a masculine presence, and she thought that he was the ehesen support of her present very undecided client. _</p>
        <p>"Slow would you have a nurse-housemaid and a cook-general, and a houge-parlourmaid if you were me ?" queried this victim, appealingly. "You see, 01 have had a cook-housemaid and a nurse-parlourmaid, and a kitchenmaid-gencral. But if I had a nurse-housemaid it would he more convenient when we had dinner parties than a nurse-parlourmaid, don't you think ? Or would you have two house-parlourmaids—equal, you know—and let then, see after the children, and no cook, and the dinner sent in always from one of.these new district—no, distributing kitchens ?</p>
        <p>Mrs. Tressor rather wondered for a moment that. the man in the corner did not make even the protest of a gesture at this proposal, but he was apparently quite content to leave the undecided mistress to sort her - unlucky household, for ,he made no sign. "You see," she continued, plaintively, "it's most annoying, but the servants I've got—they're very bad, but they were someone—they are all going because they say they don't know what they are expected to do. Such nonsense! As if it mattered, when I know quite well what I want them all to do. And the nurse- parlourmaid said besides that she hadn't time to make my things, and I must have them— my blouses for mornin,q—mado at home—d always have—and so I must try a complete change. You must' have experience, so what would you advise ? I've asked all my friends, and they each tell me a different plan, and I don't like any of them, and these are going on Monday. What hadfhotter do?" . Mrs. Tressor found the burden of this client's woes too groat and lengthy to be disposed of while standing, and retreated to her table. From thence she gave her mind thoroughly and most patiently to the task of solving the puzzle of existence for the unfortunate being who invoked her aid in such moving terms. After half-an-hour she seemed to have in some sort succeeded, for the client rose, saying: "Then you think I had better just try to manage with the ordinary sot—cook, and house- maid, and parlourmald, and nurse ? Very well, I'll try. But you:11 get them for me—lr.. Nlonday ? Did I say mine all go on Monday ? When she roc thp oniet Min in the corner rose •co. But to Mrs, Tressor's Liurpr,se no (lid not rollow the distressed woman out of the room, Ie canie instead near to the writing-table.</p>
        <p>_ ... _ "I beg your pardon, madam, he said, !ourteously, "but do I understand that that ;ady's affairs are disposed of At his WQr.ati Airs. Wrotgor looked Up very</p>
        <p>hastily front the notes she had been writing as to the wants of the departing female, and gave a quick mol..,_inent which jerked her pen out of her hand. It rolled over her neat script, blotting ;t hopelessly. The voice of the quiet nrni NM, rather a curious one, with a remarkable inton if on, uneven apparently from ill Lealth. "If so," he ,went on, "I should be grateful In" a 'tittle of your time." _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Tressoi• picked up her pen, and replaced it carefully as she said :</p>
        <p>"But I thought, I certainly thought, you were With her."</p>
        <p>"Oh, no! lam come to consult you on, my own .account."</p>
        <p>A 2 he spoke he looked round rather vaguely, and MrA. Tressor, who had been engrossed on wonder- ing she bad thought so decided]; that he belonged .to her last visitor, suddenly became aware that be could not see •very clearly, sprang up and drew a chair unobtrusively towards him. -Thanks," he sa;d. "l'm • veri stupid I'm afraid. Weak eyes." sat down evidently •thankfully, and she noticed the weary droop of his shoulders.. His hair was grey, but it was impossible to say whether it was prematurely so, for the shade so coneealed his Teatures that no one could have said with certainty whether he was an elderly wan or only broken in health. Mrs. Tressor considered the cluestion for a moment', but only for a moment. She quickly 'dismissed it, and COneelltrated herself to his business.</p>
        <p>"May I ask what I eau do for you ?" she said. He laughed--not very mirthfully. " 5/ou can do ,a great deal, I hope. 1 %vent servants." "Cortainly," she said, vaguely. Her mind was running on a chance which had presented itself to her of satisfying the undecided woman, but she dismissed that train of thought with a litiinesslike determination. She did not expect to have to dismiss it for long. Men had scarcely ever come to her agency, but the wants of the one or two who had done so had been very easily snoplied, as they eschewed the question of followers or fringes and took their stand on chatacter. /This man would doubtless, she Cl:night; act in the same imier.e.r. , "What kind of servau;. ciu you want ? " she LtSirel "All," he said. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"How many ? " she asked, equally briefly. He pulled himself up in his chair. "I'd better tell you the whole story," he said. "It's a poor talc—l mean it seems to prove incapacity on my part, but I don't quite see what I ought to have done. When I came home from Africa---" "Ali!" she said. "You were in Africa!" She spoke as if to herself, and softly. Ho evidently did not hear easily, for he went on quite uncon- scious of her words. -</p>
        <p>"I was .a trifle off colour—and—bothered with private affairs. But after a bit I got a friend's wife to get me a housekeener and a Maid and a boy. I took a flat, and went there as soon as I was all right, and took them and the whole thing in hand—or rather the thing took me in hand. In two weeks the boy told me that the house- keeper went to the theatre, and came in with a latelakey most nights ; and the maid told me that the boy wore my waistcoats and smoked my cigarettes, and I found the -maid's fiance in the dining-room myself one night when I came home unexpectedly to dinner. I confronted the three, and sacked them the same morning. Then I got the seine friend-1 had a great anxiety on my mind, and I couldn't see to the thing myself —to get me some more. She got me a cook- housekeeper, 1 think she called her, and an elderly housemaid and a valet. That was a month ago— April—this is May—no it isn't, it's June—the first of Juno." He spoke those three unimportant words with a curious change of manner. it was hesitatieg and yet emphatic. :Mrs. 'lroner, who had been watching him for game time, started. She grew.- pale, then flushed, then paled again, and her eyes scanned the • figure of the mean before her with intense minuteness. It was very easy to scan him, as his shaded eyes saw nothing of her. He paused; she was quite still, her hands crossed in her lap facing him, and still looking at him. "And last Week," he went on, "the valet gave me warning—said it was too dull for him, because I never entertain. Heaven knows I can't enter- tain ! I mwer shall entertain again. Boer can a man when---" He broke off Mar abruptly. "Pardon," he said. "I am far .from wishing to trouble you with my private affairs." • Mrs. Tressor made no response to this speech, no polite disclaimer. whatever. She scarcely seemed to hear it. She was still in precisely the same attitude, her eyes fixed on the man. In the. silence he -leant slightly forward in a listen- ing ettitude, as if he thought his dulled hearing might have caused him to miss sonic comment. But none came. He raised his hand to smooth back his hair with what was plainly a charac- teristic gesture. Mrs. Tressor leant suddenly forward; he could not see her movement. Her face was very white. On that hand was a ring of envious make ; a sapphire, set in Indian work in the form of a serpent, whose tail was in its mouth. She had ample time to see it clearly, for he let his hand rest on his hair for some moments. She caught her breath with a curious little gasp; he did not hear it. "They are not exactly germane to the matter on hand," he added, apologetically. "It only remains to say that the valet is gone; ha said he felt. for my loneliness, confound him !—I beg your pardon, madam—but he could not in justice to himself remain. 1 told him to go that very hour if he liked, and he went. Then it seemed that the cook felt for my loneliness too. She offered to share it—as my wife. The same clay the housemaid made me the same obliging offer. I do assure you I never felt so humiliated in my life. The fact of being alone is enough, and more than enough—but that 1 should have so menaged matters that they should dare—it was all I could do not to tell the women what I thought of them exactly. That was yesterday; I am left with a el:a avoman to-day. I have had enough of my friend's resources. So I asked the charwoman if she could tell me of a reliable agency to which ladies went for servants. She told me this was quite a reliable agency. gaid. came. Can you help me?" There was a little •pause. "I can trva. Tressor said at last. Her voice was raised a little, and it sounled strange and echoing. "I "What do you think I had better do ? I assure you I don't know one little bit." There was an appeal in the tone which would have gene to any woman's sympathies. Plainly it had touched Mee. Tressor's strangely. Her frame was quivering, and she was holding with both hands to the edge of her table as if to steady herself.</p>
        <p>" You want a housekeeper," she said, vaguely. "I've tried that already," he said, with meaning. .</p>
        <p>"Someone to take care of yon," "Heaven knows I do! The question is, can you find it ? " _ . _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Tressor seemed to take a sudden resolu- tion. She crossed the room, shut the door, and came back to the man. He had risen at her movement and the sound of the closing door. " Will you try me " she said, very distinctly and clearly. • "Good Heavens! Madam!"</p>
        <p>"You've tried me already, too," she said, in Urn same clear voice. "But I'll do better, indeed I will, if you'll try me again."</p>
        <p>"Madam ! "</p>
        <p>Suddenly, and almost with his exclamation, the man seized the arm of the woman with both hands. With one he held to it as if he needed support, with the other he took the left band of the woman in his and raised it close to his eyes. Guarding the wedding ring was a ring the fac of his own, only lighter—a sapphire and a serpent with his tail in his mouth. 'Lucy!" he said, letting it fall suddenly. "Lucy ! " "Say you will try me apin, Jack—say you will try me again! This is unite a reliable rtgency— you said so--say you will try me again!</p>
        <p>The Domestic Agency was closed for the day by means of a large card to that effect fastened outside the door, and it was as well. It might have surprised and pained her clients had they seen Mrs. Tressor sitting on her last client's knee, her arm round his shoulders, and her hand ,3laFped tightly in his. "Why did yen go Lucy? uon c see why." "I'd been so hateful to yoh; so .neglectful, so selfish—no, you shan't stop me—so altogether 'intern] to you before you went to Africa. I blann-fl you for every trouble that I made for myself. I t.ook everything and gave you nothing —nothing. And when you were gone I knew it, and by degrees I began to hope -you'd let me make up for it when you came back, and then— then the pauer said you were--missing. And I went to tln:War Office, and they said you were— alt ! they tatid you were killed. Moreton, they said,. and your initizl and Major. They were vrhey wore wrong, thauk Ileavea _</p>
        <p>”And then I knew—l mean I thought I knel —that I never mild make it up -to yon_neve any more. And, oh! Jack, I couldn't.' bear al_ life for thinking of it. I felt I could never 8" anyone I'd known again. So I just left WTI body and everything, and took a new name on- etime here and did this. And, Jack—oh ! I hardly feel as if it could be true yet—but you will let nUt try to make up for it, won't you ?" "There's nothing to make. up—nothing," 110 said, and .his voice was not steady. "It was.l who. didn't make you happy. I thought of 11, often and often out there, and when I came hack and no one knew anything of you, I thought you'd gone away because you couldn't put up with me any longer. I've moved Heaven ant! earth to find you, and to beg vou to try— thought I never should. _Anti--and—Lucy-30u said you'd come as a howiekeeper—the Domestic Agency is bound to find me that !" He tried to smile with the last' words, but his voice had grown more and more slinky, niid his wife as she looked at him buried her head on his shoulder to hide her tears, as she said, in short, jerky sentences. "It shall, Jack. It has. And shall she cow° to your house—or will you come to hers—tn, dinner? If you've only got a charwoman, you'a better come to me !" And so it came about that the Misses Steward, happening to be looking out o! their front "the window again later in the day, saw opposite person " come home "with an Odor?' man. Is it her husband, do you :suppose' Miss Fanny said, anxiously. "I'll endeavour to discover," said Miss Steward, confidently, for the milkman was at that moment beginning his evening rouneit Irl</p>
        <p>TIIE END.I</p>
        <p>34411" "The Vennycotnequicks," "Mehalah,'" "Red Spider," "The thiscrocks,” ."Richard Cable," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>toAt s.otrie little distance off the bold and precipitous St. at Looe, in Cornwall, lies the island ot TrNicolas, or Looe. It is nearly circular, and ythe most part rises abruptly from the waves. froliep dangerous and troublesome reef runs to it i, the mainland, covered by the waves which t'l t themselves into foam over it. The sea-cliffs °yr sea-cliffsft e mainland rise high above the water, but ;Here are in them rents, down which streams brawl 1:0 little coves. The river that enters the sea at livides the town into two parts, each of in the old dtys of rotten boroughs returned ° Inembers to Parliament. 4The whole of this coast cafe-sea great advantages 16,8',`"lgglers, and at the beginning of last century ti'- Iwo Ismes were notorious nests of those daring rien Will) Carried on a contraband trade with alfee, and the difficulties presented by the coast th 0e it most arduous and discouraging work for it tPreventiie officers to check this "free trading." o nnsuccessful had they been, that at length it ;:.ttwned on the minds of those in authority '..hat fifts as well to plant a station on Looe Island, felt convinced that the two denizens of the 't were in 'Constant communication with the I,ll.::ggAlers, and gave them notice whenever there ;11,3 "anger of their running their goods. They . shrewdly sus ected that on Looe Island these goad were storedPto be shipped ashore at suitable L4te'fients. Yet, however minute the search made I 1 the island, no place could be found where the vi:gB ef brandy and bales of silk were secreted. The roayest auspicious attached to the denizens of the E,l. Old Finn and his daughter, Black Joan, but astif thin could be brought home to the to Zsy them totheir arrest. The coastguard station epete„d on Looe Island was planted there as much Ir, "10 sake of watching the movements of the nos, as of observing the coast. 'fOf Finn was peculiar. For some crime ter other he had -been outlawed, and had taken a rock in Plymouth Sound, where he aol'nt 50111 e twenty-five years, not in absolute hlelnde, for in some unaccountable manner he beentteed a woman to come out to his rock and ate his wife As a result of this wild union, -Itl ;'le . child arrived, whom the couple called Joan. abn„,, ength the woman died, and then Finn *SsC4°lled his rock and came ashore, where he was v4alolested. But life on the mainland was intoler- ise,,t" him, constant association with his felli!w- 11,4",litiendurable and he fled to Looe Island with iPaeghter, there to end his days. bp::: was now an old man, and Joan was a buxom, t% faced, faced, sturdy young woman of three-and- e3.eetY, with very black hair and eyebrows and dark It was not often that she appeared in the itlletN of Lo d then only to furnish herself eeoessar7et an s. She seemed to be flush of money. She's-asked the preventive men—whence came it? by 'd her father ceuld earn it in one way only, WhomA4king themselves serviceable to the daring men wuelled the law. twe'llen the station: was erected on the island, and (11m, le."astguards were placed in it, Finn and his fs, g 'ter looked earth ither in the face, and each e bore the asnect. of dismay. eveicntlev.e,x,tnind,..Toan," said ;he old man; "we'll be the wl em. Providence hey' given us brains by p tc4inee, and to them by the scruple. Don't go 0 look sour lit 'em. Put on your sweetest, „well do 'em yet." 11 do my best, vayther, but it's a sore trial." 41It4an is born to trouble," replied the old man, entiouslv "as the waves is to fret, 'old the t41311,d to dioto water. Whatever you do, don't let NI get a mite of an iuklin' as to where the 41's be " ast me, vayther." • it happened that these cellars were full, :1)1, d('' was necessary to have thm ecl ea red but. ths ~!'" With those coastguardsmen on the alert was eespi sr,,,,(4eie Island is a mass of old red sandstone, with reek!. on top, due to the decomposition of the Whathad been done was to dig down under g,rasts "ed, and tunnel in the friable shale and • ad e