      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WOhIEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOhIEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Pageantry.—The presentation .of plays and pagea?:: goes merrily on, and these iiramatic instincts aro confined to London,and the ancient ecclesiastical ciliej4l bct have even penorrated 10 that churning old-wnr village of Penshurst in Kent, which has the n1)10110(10 of being the prettiest village in England. Mark in one of his serious moments said that "the producti'''' of bygone English life and achievements by pageontrYt is the most heantitul,- most instructive, end I'o°sl impressive way of portraying histqry." }Weever 01 may be, pageantry, after being lapsed ;or many gener.g: Lions, has distincily" caught on." Londtin- has bad Os great Church Pageant, and next month the ancient citl of York %ill be similarly on fete. The Welsh Pnrant, is to be held in the Sonhia. Gardens, Cardiff, the 10„ week in July, and the first week in August, and union,'" the notable people taking part in this will be tul Marchioness of Bute, Lady Ninian Crichton-StuArt, Lord Tredegar.</p>
        <p>Royalty in the Wt.—'h t) West of England is ofte,ll favoured with tbe presence of Rojalty, and this wets" there have been the King's visit to Gloucester for tii!'t opening of the Royal Agricultural Show, and the „ of the Prince and Princess of %Vales to Lord and Lau'', Bath at Longleattlall. Both distrits abound in plsef;', of interest. Gloucester has its ancient Cathedral, its famous stained-glass windows, and of still „woe antiquity is the Abbey of 0 lastuntuiry, visitedlll',", Prince and Princess of Wales this ‘teek, ibonTl, attended the special service in telebration of t'll millenary of the foundation of the See and Cathedrai' ~ at Wells. With one exception Wells is the smallest 91 the Cathed. al towns, end is ecclesiastically yoked Wilitl Bath. Eaily next month will see another itoyal vls,'„ • o the West when the King and Queen will be guests of Lord and Lady Derby, at Knowsley, July sth to 7th, on the occasion of their Majest2est official visit to Manchester. Lady Derby is the young'', - daughter of the seventh Duke of Manchester. She is veil known and popular figure in Societyand has alwaY been a particular favourite of Royalty. London Ballrooms —New dances are introduced 0104 season but the favourites this year in the fashionsb.,, • ballroom are again • the "two-step" and the " Boston waltz, the latter it direct descendant of the "Kt nsieg: ton crawl." With regard to square dances, the Laucers still take first place, and to far hostesses have ben, successful in keeping down anything apl,roBcho°ll horse-play. Voting men who have somewhat over looke.`c good manners and literally "romped " have found the' names erased from desirable visiting lists, and 1•11° result is, better tehaviour generally among dancers. Fearsome Pets.--FaAionabla women, when stockill,g their conservatories this sonuner, have included a 10.9 artily of tree-frogs, little creatures about an inch 11, a half long, and of a vivid green colour. Inde things "crawly" aprear to be in high favour at the moment. One well-known Society woman has 50 affectionate snake, which speeds most of its time the dray) big-room terrifj big • • unsuspecting guests quietly coiling itself about their feet. Anothe "smut t" lady possesses some very t tom lathy alligators 0; while chameleons, lizards and salamanders, are oni‘ popular pets. Soon we shall scarcely be surprised lA' lied lions, and tigers and other fearsome crraturl proVvlieg about cooservatorkss, • drawing-rooms sa boudoirs. The "Lucky Shoe."—The old shoe, sl' conspicuous 11,, weddings in days gone by, is rarely seen now, its plilc.--5 being taken by tiny little. new white satin shoes, minUr heels and quite sott, which are pelted at the happy Pn't as they enter the carcjage or automobile that is to be 4„ them away. It is considered very lucky to pick t!tt one of these little slices, and the scramSla for them must be acknowledged, is often most undignified. B'° is no longer permitted at smart weddings, confetti Vo°'lacks the approval of the "smart set," who just 110:„ prefer to pelt bride and bridegroom with the tiny wlll,- satin slippers described aSave, and handfuls of r°o ' leaves. • Wedding Rings.—The very broad, gipsy-looking riag is giving place to the thick narrow variety, which 16 decidedly a move in the right direction, as the former barely permitted of more than one extra ring bein.g. . worn upon that particular finger. Moreover, there 's just a tinge of vulgarity about the very broad weddil ring, especially when the bridegroom is old-fashioue, enough to insist upon a " wield " being donned I . addition. Names, and the date of the happy event, at"n • invariably inscribed inside the modern wedding riug, together with a motto of some description. Reversible Garments.—Many of the new roversitle coats were seen at Ascot, and though, generally spea,s; ing, these were costly models, the impecunious teiglo with advantage study the idea. For instance, to be , to motor, or otherwise travel in a suitable coat, turn 1 inside out, and appear in &amp;smart wrap, strikes one 35 procedure at once economical and useful, and attil - , moment there is in shopland a very good and inesPel,l; sive selection of reversible fabrics, suitable for cra and even skirls. The cost lies principally in making up, and it is here the clever home worker score' , heavily. Tartans.—As early autumn approaches, we are fa with the customary epidemic of tartans, but this p'ari dressmakers and milliners are more" previous tb usual, and already we see tartan hosiery, tartan be;V::; and tartan bat trimmings, among modish outfits. are now however, so accustomed to the mixing !if' (.11, seasons, that even poplins in December and ermine' June cause us no surprise. The Modern Girl.—Hockey has outlived the °hitt-1; tions raised against it as a game for girls, and tvhat said at the recent conference of Headmistresses., is likely to affect its popularity at the public .schools. is too violent exercise, it is dangerous, and cultivst7, the loud and stringent voice, are some of the °Wei,. tions raised against hockey, but like cricket., 10011 golf,•rowing and swimming, it is a good game for :11 reasonably strong girls, with no physical defects- '„ I these outdoor games help to make girls tall, strong,a;:s straight., and banish the petty vanities and puerillb,,, supposed to be inherent in the sex. Women of o'o older generation can recall very different times whned girl's prided themselves on their delicate physique, 'll4 their general air of helplessness, outside the four within which they were expected to spend •their The change is. in every way a happy one and „to enabled the present day woman to prolong her Ya-00 and freshness )(mg beyond the age which by-garj generations regarded as well advanced on the dowsn''' path of life. w 11() Working Girls Clubs.—Mr. Montagu Williams, ji knew East Loudon as few people know itand who 111#1 keen eye for any really useful practical effort, deserlhol in " Round London a club for girls which he like visted, and asked why there were not many others citli it. Had Mr. Montagu Williams been happily still :•bi- ns, he would have rejoiced to be present at the 001 sr, lion and sale of work which was opened on Mond,113:00 Morley Halt w ith the active co-operation of Prllither Victoria of Schleswig-Holstein, and a number of °,Ol,- • distinguished ladies. Since "Round London was Jr of lished there has been a large increase in the mossier girls' clubs, many of which are affiliated to the Fe, taa tion of Working Girls' Clubs, on behalf of which Ii exhibition and sale of work was held, but 00'0;11. ath room for many more such institutions in Eastaridt'°o:o,;. London and also in some of the great provincial ta oi Helping the Working Girl Class.—lt does 111:4,4,e(1, course follow that any intermittent or half-113 )i.a, ir effort to help the working girl class will succeetl,io the workers have tact and sympathy and are nb',ael° willing to spare some time, any girls' club whicll,ioo certain conditions, must be, as Mr. Montagu dal said, productive of enormous good. One esso.' that the club should be open every night, and if cic be open during the day-time, and afford sleeping arairil); modation for girls who find themselves teinl,s) sti/ without it home, the results will in that case "°,ofer more satisfactory. It is, however, unnecessarY t.,13 clubs to the matter in much detail, because so Las11:1,00 have been established that it is easily Poss'' 00" obtain details concerning the manner of working of those which have been the most successful. 31101</p>
