      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>GARDEN SUBURBS.-THE FIRST HOUSES AT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GARDEN SUBURBS.-THE FIRST HOUSES AT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>"Well, do it now."</p>
        <p>And the girl did eventually recover the oar. But time had been wasted, and way had been lost. For the first time in her life she rowed badly.</p>
        <p>"Joan, shouted Symmonds, "whatever is up You're rowin' like the veriest landlubber!"</p>
        <p>"It's that rubbin' vayther's back for this lumbago has made.me so, and the hartshorn in the embrocation have got up into my head and mt.&amp; me like one drunk. I'm very sorry, Mister Symmonds. Do you ever have the lumbago?" "At times."</p>
        <p>"Well, you get Joel French to spread you face downwards on the kitchen table and go into your back for half-an-hour with flannel and hartshorn and oil."</p>
        <p>"Pull harder._ We are _losing way fast."</p>
        <p>For a 2while she pulled better:</p>
        <p>"But I reckon, "said the girl, "French is that grumpy, you might ax him, but he'd never take the trouble."</p>
        <p>"Ah " said Symmoitds. "If I only had you to rub my back I'd think I wos in Heaven. What's that—ashore ? is your father signalling ? " "He's a wipin' of his blessed nose wi' his pocker- hanker. Pull away, I'm game now." And, indeed, now she handled the oar in very different fashion, and the little creek was soon • reached near where the Finns' cottage stood.</p>
        <p>That evening Joel French returned, very irate. "You're a •fool, Joe!" he said. "Do you know what has been done whilst you've been here watch- ing? They've cleared out everything, run a whole cargo ashore and distributed it."</p>
        <p>"-bash my buttons!" exclaimed Symmonds. "It was Black Jowl had me. But I'll be even with her yet."</p>
        <p>Next, time that the aid encountered Joe on the cliffs there was a sparkle in her eye. But he made no sign. "He is a greenhorn," thought Joan. "He don't know how I drawed him off o' purpose whilst our chaps were clearing out the cellar. It's a pleasure to have to do wi' such a softie. I reckon French would be harder to tackle."</p>
        <p>During the ensuing fortnight it struck Joan that Joe's duty took • him across her pretty frequently,. and that his duty or pleasure brought him to exchange civil words with her. lie had evidently an eye on her, but whether he was watching her movements or fascinated by her, person she could not say. Acting on her father's instructions she continued to be civil to him and to work her eye, but she despised him for the ease with which he had allowed himself to be hoodwinked.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of the fortnight her father called her to him and said: "Joan, gall, there be the Skylark in the otling, come over with cognac from lioscoff. She's shy of coining in, and if we can't menage it she'll run her cargo at Polperro. There is Joel French got his glass on her, and he knows as well as we what's up and she's after. You go smellin' about the ,station and see what's goin' on there. There's Svininonds--" - "I don't think naught o• him. I'll turn his face to the wall and he shall see nothing." "Well, look alive, and tell me what you make out."</p>
        <p>"I'll do my best, vayther." "Set your mind to it! Set your mind to it! There's a lot depends on our getting the goods safe in." _</p>
        <p>During the *afternoon Joan met Joe Symmonds,' and as he made as though inclined for a talk, she afforded him a smile of encouragement.</p>
        <p>u bit lonely to-day, said he, "and, my Word, it's a dull life here on this island and none to fitlk to. "You've got Joel French." "Oh, Joel be blowed ! "replied Symmonds. "He's that crusty and moody, there's hardly any getting a, word out o' him. He's poor company at the best 0' times, and just now he's not here." "Not here ! " echoed Joan. "Where be he to, then?" _ _</p>
        <p>"Well, there's been affliction in his Re's lost his wife's mother." "Didn't know he had a wife."</p>
        <p>"He has, and that's what puts him in the sulks, being hero and away .front her ; and she's a bit flighty, and he don't know what she's up' to when he's shut up in the isle. And now his wife's mother's gone." "Mostwise," retorted Joan, "it be thought a betterment to be rid o' a mother-in-law."</p>
        <p>"Not in Joel's case, for she looked after his wife and kept her in .some decent order when he weren't there."</p>
        <p>"Ah I " said Joan. "No wonder be ain't happy here._ So he's gone to the burying ?</p>
        <p>"Yes, he be:"</p>
        <p>Then, after a pause: "You seem to he in affliction, too, Mister Synunonds, wi' that black band about your arm ?</p>
        <p>"I be. I've lost my sister." "You don't mean to say so. There seems to ha' been an epuldemic among the, women of the preventive service." "Yes, and the lieutenant has lost his wife, and he's off to the funeral to-day. It's coorious, ain't it?"</p>
        <p>"Tremendous," acquiesced Joan. "So we be all sixes and sevens. Me eryin' over my sister, and the lieutenant howlin' over his wife, and Joel in the grumps about his wife's mother. But it's a first-rate thing as it haps just now when all is slack and there's no mischief afloat. Gracious! if the freetraders was to know it—my word!" "NI, hat a muff this fellow is I " thout:ht Joan. "Tellin' me, of all people, that this is so." How- ever, she kept her opinion to herself, and said: "I suppose you was very fond of your sister ?" "Loved her like blazes," replied Symmonds. "Was She older than you ? '</p>
        <p>"No, just about your age—and size; and if you was to chance to be out this evening, I'd shew you a thing or two." "What would you shew me ?" "I've all my sister's things I brought away, and whatever to do with them I don't know." • "What eort o' things ?"</p>
        <p>"Her clothes. There's a pair of sky-blue stock- ines as is new and never put on; and there's ribbons, and there's a hat wi' feathers, real hostrich, but one of them feathers be broke." "You might mend it vvi' a bit of fine wire, I reckon."</p>
        <p>"Can't say. I should like you to see them." "Blue stockings ?" "Sky-blue."</p>
        <p>Vy word ! " "You'd say so if you seed 'em, an never put on her poor dear blessed legs," And Symmonds pulled oat his red cotton handkerchief and wept. "Well, 'tis a pity, never worn. If heed had the chance of wearin'of them just once heed have died more easy. Now I must off 'and peel my pertaties." Then Joan hastened home and told her father that the coast was clear. Death had been among the womankind, and the preventive men were steeped in tears and liquor, engrossed in their domestic afflictions and not likely to be troublesome. "If the lieutenant's wife be dead," observed old Finn, "as a mark of respect the men of the service will attend the funeral.'</p>
        <p>"That's certain," added Joan, "and get fuddled as well."</p>
        <p>"I'll signal," said old Finn. "At nightfall we'll have the Skylark in.,,</p>
        <p>its Joan mem,.</p>
        <p>1/. WOraCU.</p>
        <p>one was ttunxing ot me garments or aoz Symmonds's sister, that he had brought. away to the island sky-blue stockings and a bat with feathers. She looked .at the investure of her lower limbs, old darned, dingy black stockings, thoroughly rusty with age and falling into holes. She had another pair, but not much 'better in con- dition and not superior in colour. "Them legs o' mine," said she, "if they was only skv-blue, Wouldn't be a bad pair o' 'legs at all, and I wouldn't mind liftin' my skirts a bit to shew 'em when I went to Looe market. I wonder, now, if Joe will sell Pein. Why not? They can be no ufe to him. He can't put 'em on ; he'd bust 'eta all the way up and down. I'll go and have a squint at 'em, and we may have a deal. And the 'at. I'd like to see that, too. If the feather's broke he'll dispose of it cheap." Night settled in dark, with a drizzling rain at intervals, but lifting and clearing at. times. "It's just the weather for us," said old Finr: "Now attend to me, Joan. You're to take the lantern, put it under your cloak, and go all about and look hard and see that the preventive boat be not out. The sea is still, and if you can't always see you can hear, and the dip of an oar you can catch four miles away. Look all round and see that .they're not prepared and hidin' somewhere."</p>
        <p>"All iight, •rather." "And if you're quite sure that all is safe then raise the lantern up and down; up and down, mind. But if there is danger wave it from side to side. You understand ? " "I reaon I do. I know my business."</p>
        <p>"'Men be off."</p>
        <p>Joan took the lantern and enveloptd herself in a very full, thick cloak, and strolled along the edge of the island, keeping a sharp look-out on the sea whenever the rain e eased. The li,rhts of Looe made rery streaks on the water, and a break in these caused by a boat was not likely to escape her.</p>
        <p>All at-once she heard in her-ear: "God evening, ;loan." She started. She had been so intentron the water that she had paid no attention to the land, and Joe Symmonde had stolen on her un- awares. "Now, Ido take this kind of you." he continued, "to come out and give me a bit of counsel over them garments and powps and l'anities. or no, poor dear sister." "You haven't got them out here ?" "No," replied Joe, "they'd spoil in th, rein, and the night is dark. I must trouble you to step into the cabin."</p>
        <p>"Ts that quite proper ? What 'ud folks say ? " "Where's the folks to say aught. That growl ing hedgehog, Joel, be away burying of his mother- in-Jaw and drinking himself tight.</p>
        <p>r‘Well," said Joi;rt, "I don', mind so long as we leave the door open. I've been turning over them stockings and that there 'at in my mind, and I daresay I might offer you a trifle for 'em. You see, the stockings Mightn't fit me, and the feather in the 'at, is you say, be broke and no good at</p>
        <p>"You shall judge for yourself," said Symmonds. "But I can't stay long," observed Joan. • "You needn't. I'd rayther You looked at the ha!' o' tricks now whilst Joel is away. it might be unpleasant were he here. He'd be pas.,in' of insulting remarks."</p>
        <p>"That's true," mused Joan. "There's no time like the present, time." "I may say I've had an offer for the lot, but it didn't come up to my figure. Besides," added Symmonds, making his tone soft and insinuating, "I did want that you should have the first pick. seeing as we be neighbours and friendly, and I did notice as your stockings were out of repair and wanted a dash o' paint over 'em." "Well," said the girl, "I don't mindlooking at 'em. Whether we deal Or not is another matter. Lone and lorn I be, and vayther don't give me much pocket-money: Iliad to save up a fortnight to afford myself three-penn'orth of stickjaw." "It's a wonder how you get any money at ail," said the preventive man. "You've nothing t sell." "Well, it is odd. It's Sambo's skull, does it. Gentlefolk; from Lone come's out here picknickin', and vayther he exhibits the skull and gets sixpence or a shillin' by it." JLe Syrnnonds knew the story of Sambo. In the first years that. Finn was on the island there was a big negro there as well. They got on fairly well together. But after a while suspicion was raised that he had sold ltimFelf to the officers of the Crown and would betray secrets. He was found thrown or fallen over the rocks with his neck broken. A coroner and jury sat on him, but nothing could be proved against anyone, and he was buried on the island by old Finn, who, how- ever, dug him up again, possessed himself of the skull and made a show of it. "Here we be," said Joe. ,"We'll leave the door open for the gulls to look in and see that, all is respectable. ' So you have a lantern with you ?" "Yee," replied Joan. "When the rain comes on it is very dark, and I don't want to fall over the edge and come to an end like Sala°, and having old vayther makine exhibition o' my skull and gem tin' sixpences out, of it." "1 see," said Syntnonds. The girl placed the lantern on the floor near the door and threw back her cloak.</p>
        <p>"I be hot," she said. "Take it. off," advised the preventive man. "The cloak,is wet and heavy as lead." "And the IPA, might spoil the ribbins," considered Joan. "I'll just dry my lingers as well if you'll lend me a towel."</p>
        <p>Joe proenced what was asked for and then pro- ceeded to a locker and'drew forth a.bundle.</p>
        <p>"You've a lot o' things there," remarked the girl. "And gay, too," added the man ; see them fine by my cOlza lamp. I'll just screw up the oil and turn the wick up.' Having done as he proposed he untied the knots of the sheet in which were, as he said, the articles that had belonged to his sister. "You see," observed Joe, "if I'd a wife or a sweetheart l'd give 'em to her free, gratis and for not !ling... "</p>
        <p>"But: as you ain't got neither, you'll sell to me. But I'm bad off for money. The skull don't bring in a terlible lot, and when vayther does get some- thing it's precious little he allows to me." "We are not like to quarrel over the price," said Symmonds, sweetly. "There, what do you say to that ?" He exhibited a brightly-coloured silk hand- kerchief. Joan considered it eagerly. It was nearly new. Joe was hardly likely to part with that cheap. "And there !" he said, and unrolled a pair of quite new blue stockings. "My word, they are splendid! I'll deal wi you over them stockings first," she said. "But I don't know if they'll fit me." "Try 'emit on," proposed Spmnonds. "I couldn't have the face to do it," she answered. "They look to me too short in the foot." "They'll stretch," said Joe.</p>
        <p>"But-will they stretch enough ? I've long feet." "Try 'ern on and make sure," again proposed the preventive man. "I shouldn't have the face to do so in here," answered Joan. "But it's not rainin' now, and I'll go outside and sit on your bench and see if I can get my legs into 'em. You bide where you be and don't look out o'. the winder. Sit you there and corer your face wi' your hands as if you was thinkin' of your poor dead sister, and I'll take my lantern outside and try on the stockings and then offer you a twice."</p>
        <p>Very well. "Swear no. peepin'."</p>
        <p>"I swear."</p>
        <p>Then Joan took up her lantern and the blue hose and went forth. She removed her boots and peeled off the miserable black stockings that encased her legs, and proceeded to draw on the articles that so filled her with admiration and desire. It took her some time to get them on. , _</p>
        <p>"My word !," she said. -"They fit the splendid. But I mustn't let him think so. What a pity women wear long petticoats. Wi' stockings such as these they should shew their legs. I wish I could stand opposite myself and see 'em. But I'll have a good lirok at 'ern all the same wi' my lantern."</p>
        <p>Then she took the light and raised and depressed it over each limb from the knee to the tce.</p>
        <p>"I'll have 'em," she exclaimed. "But I'll beat clown that soft-head first." •</p>
        <p>She took them off, put on her old hose and boots, and returned indoors.</p>
        <p>"I said it, they're too short and too tight." , "But in a day or two they'll stretch." "When washed they'll tighten." "l'm sorry they don't suit pru." said Symmonds, sorrowfully. "I was thinking of making you a present of them. "Never!" exclaimed the delighted girl. "I mean it," said Symtnonds. "Now look at tho rest of what I've got." So the remainder of the collection was over- hauled, and Joan bargained for several articles, and after much haggling secured them. • . "What have you in that there little box ? "asked she.</p>
        <p>Symmonds opened it and displayed a ring. "My sister's, "he said. "And as you've been so civil, and bought the shawl and the petticoat and the hat, I'll make you a present of the ring." Joan considered. "I don't know about t hat, " she said, 'reluctantly. "Takin' of a ring from a gentle- man means a lot too much. I think I'll not have the ring, all the same. And now l've been here too long, and my old vayther will swear. I must be off.'</p>
        <p>Snatching up her cloak she threw it over her shoulders, tucked the stockings that were given and her purchases under one arm, and taking the lantern she went forth and strode away to the cottage. The door was open and old Finn was there., "What ha' you been doin', you toad!" rOttred the man. • "Makin' signals as all were right, and the whole crew and cargo have been trapped. I only got off by the merest chance. The preventive men, under the lieutenant and Joel French, have been watchin', and ketched 'em all and took every- thing, and you—you signalled the lantern that all was safe."</p>
        <p>"I!" gasped Joan. And it flashed on her, as the lantern had flashed on her, stockings, that un- wittingly she actually had, in admiring' them, given the token that all was safe. She had, more- over, been lured by Symmonds away from the watch, and thus had allowed the officers of the Crown to steal upon the smugglers unobserved. "Now I'll larrup you," shouted Finn. as he caught up a stick. "by ginger, I'll deal with you as they did with Sambo!' A few minutes later into the • preventive station dashed Joan. "Save me! Save me!. she cried. "Vayther is that mad he'll kill me! And, oh, Joe, I'll have that there ring with all the consekenecs, for I see you ain't such a softie as I thought." "Ali," said Symmonds, "my sister will welcome you into the family." "No, she's dead." _</p>
        <p>Joe winked his eye. "That's all gammon. Neither is my sister dead, nor the wife of the lieutenant, nor Joel's ltot her-in-law. There ain't been no epuldetnic, after all." "Well, now!" exclaimed Joan, and stood back and looked admiringly at the man. "You be a fine fellow and no mistake. I feels as I shall admire und worship you unto my life's end. Give we the ring." . • I Tux nip].</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON CANINE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CANINE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of thiS Society was held on Tuesday at the Lion Hotel, under the presidency of Captain P. N. Avery. There were also present : Messrs. F. G. - Fairhurst, W. A.. Leslie and W. C. Baines (vice-presidents), J. Randall, W. H. Booth, T. E. Baskerville, J. Beswick, F. W. White, J. Sanderson, W. Bunting, F. Turner„ T. B. Berry, H. Tyrer, F. Townley and 'l'. Elwell (committee) and Captains R. Smith and Picton.- -A letter Was read from Colonel Edelsten, the president, regretting his inability to be present owing to the demand upon his time .of the arrangements for the Royal review. He asked the Secretary to put anything he had won into the prize fund, and he also sent, a cheque for 30s. to the Society. (Applause). - Mi.. Fairhurst (treasurer) submitted the balance sheet, which showed that the .society was in a sound condition, and the accounts were passed unanimously. —A. smoking concert was subsequently held, pre- sided over by Captain Avery. An excellent Musical programme was given, including songs by Messrs. Hindley, Ockleston, Wrench, Hannan, Dunn and Captain Avery. Instrumental solos were also con- tributed by Messrs. Petworth and Wood and Captain Smith. Mr. Forster acted ss accompanist. Mr. Moseley also gave a recitation and Captain Pietonttold an amusing story.---During the concert Captain Avery presented the following special prizes :—Silver challenge cup for the best dog in the show, Mr. It. 11. Lord ; silver challenge cup for the best. bitch in the show, Mr. Beswick ; silver challenge cup for the best puppy; bred by exhibitor, Mr. Bunting ; challenge bowl for the best Airedale puppy, bred by exhibitor, Mr. Panting : cup for the best sporting dog or bitch in the show. Mr White; gold medal for the best Great Dane, Mr.• Steel; bronze medal for the best Airdale puppy, Mr. Bunting; bronze medal for the best Irish, terrier, Mr. Sanderson; bronze medal for the best bulldog, Mr. Lord; bronze medal for the best Pomeranian, Mr. Elwell; bronze medal for the best any variety toy, Mr. Townley; bronze medal for the best sporting brace, Mr. White; bronze medal for the best non-sporting brace, Mr. Steel.; Mrs. White's special for the best pug, Mr. Townley.----The Chairman pleaded for increased public support for the society and thanked the officers for their zealous work. The proceedings concluded with i vote of thanks to the Chairman. ppopesed by Captain Smith, seconded by Capain Picton, and carried with musical honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WOLKIAN F,LECTROCuTE D.</head>
        <p>WOLKIAN F,LECTROCuTE D.</p>
        <p>John Smbh, Armly, employed by the Leeds Forge Company, wns on Thursday erecting an electric crane, when he nccidentally put his arm upon a live wire, which ignited his clothing. He was instantly killed.</p>
        <p>Dr. Arthur Somervell, Hat. Inspector, visited the Warrington Training College on Tuesday to conduct the annual examination in music. As usual the collective test was made the occasion of the annual concert which was held in the evening when, at the invitation of the Principal, there was a large gathering of well-known townspeople. The second-year students Were assisted by tenor and bass voices drawn from local choirs, and a small orchestra was also in attendance. The, works chosen for performance were Mendelssohn's " Loreley " and Gade's "The; En-King's Danghter "and it is due to the students to say that their singing evidenced full appreciation of the spirit of these beautiful com- positions, and Was characterised by a taste and expres- sion which moved the audience to cordial applause. The chorus responded intelligently-Jo the baton of the painstaking conductor„ Mr. W. S. Woods, and the principals did justice to their respective parts. In Mendelssohn's " Loreley " the exacting solo passages were sweetly and expressively sung by Miss Emmeline Taylor. The spirited interpretation of" The Vintage Song" by the male voices was well received. In Gade's "The En-King's Daughter" most of the solo work devolved upon Mr. J. Hamilton Gibson, who as Oluf infused much feeling into his singing, and was particularly effective in " When thro' the meadows of tender green." Miss Florence Warrener and Miss Alice •Dmikerley- ,ave sympathetic and tasteful renderings of their respective parts. The choruses were most impressive, the alternating dramatic and fanciful passages being finely realized. The orchestra was composed as follows violin, Mr. R. H. Pearson ; 2nd violin, Mr. A. Knowles ; viola, Mr. G. Johnson ; violoncello, Mr. E. A. Wright; double bass, Mr. J. Sudworth ; pianoforte, Miss Hackett ; harmonium, Mr. W. H. Payton, .Mus. Bac. Dr. SOMERVELL, responding to the invitation of the Principal to address the gathering, sAid be would like to thank the students, in the name of the audience, for the delightful concert they had given, and also the p-ntlemen who had so kindly given their services to make it possible for a college entirely composed of . ladies to perform a work of the calibre which the students there generally rnenaged to do. Many of the colleges had only men or women in them and therefore the kind of music they could pet form was greatly restricted, and he wished to thank the gentlemrn who by their help bad enabled the students to do a very much bigger kind of work than they could do if they were singing alone. Dr.Somervell then addressed him- self to the students. He thought they of..en forgot, when working for an examination—which was perhaps not quite so bad as they imagin-d, when it had come off "—how big a subject might, be. If they went back three hundred years they would find that in those days they put music in a very wonderful position—a much greater position than we put it in to-day. Shakespeare said some of the most extraordinary things about music —so wonderful, so tremendous that we were perhaps apt to think he could not mean what he said. Fur instance :</p>
        <p>The man that bath no music in himself, Nor is not mov'd with concord of sweet sounds, Ts fit for treasons, stratagems, and spoils ; The motions of his spirit are dull as night, And his affections dark as Erebus. Let no such man be trusted.</p>
        <p>That. was a very serious thing to say, if they took it in its literal sense, as meaning a person who had not got a musical ear. He thoteeht Shakespeare meant something bigger than that—that the man who had no imagination, who could not be moved by good and beautiful music, was a bit of a villain. (Laughter). Having pointed out that with the Gresks, music was a necessary factor in education, even Of the very young, Dr. Somervell contended that we started at the wrong end. We did not prepare the soil-at all, but began to educate children without preparation. Occasionally they found a child who by nature was prepared— the preparation generally showed itself in the form of naughtiness. (Laughter.) Dr. Somervell , asked the teachers not to damp the interest, of wideasynkA children. He once went into an infant school and found all the children, aged three and upwards, sitting with their arms folded and their heads drooning. One little boy was standing by a table in front of the class and showed a readiness to answer questions which he (Dr. Somervell) put. He asked. the teacher why the boy had been put there-and the reply was "Oh, be. is sech a chatterbox, and we have to putbim there to keep him quiet." He (Dr.Somervell) remarked "I see what you are doing. Yon are trying to make him as stupid as the others." (Laughter.) That child, in 'his o.pinion, was the one in the whole class who was especially worth educating. Contirming the speaker referred to the high place which the Greeks gave to music as was dvidenced by the legends in which they embodied their ideas about truth. Having alluded to some of the legends, Dr. Somervell said we had developed music to an extraordinary extent in Eng- land during the last sixty years. Ho 'supposed that sixty years ago there was hardly an orchestra or a choral society in England, no-one learnt to sing and play, and the whole nation was what we might cal dumb. Then an awakening in music began. Choral societies and orchestras were f•-•rrned and we had now in London five standrna orchestrasos well as orchestras and choral societies all over the kingdom. Everyone was teaming to sin. and play and we thought we were very musical indeed. But we never had flowers without weeds. Alongside of these beautiful things which had grown up we had the music hall and trashy music, and it was more particularly about the trashy music that he wanted to speak that, night. He always thriugbt the music ball song had one great merit, and that was—and it was perhaps a rather cryptic , saying—that it did not pretend to he anything but what it was. The kind of music which pretended to he good and was bad was the kind we bad got to he careful about. The form that music most usually took,becnuse it was the most easily performed, was the form of song. Last year he spoke about this song, as it was called, and he had the honour of a leading article in one of the Warrington papers. He never had time to reply to it because he was in the country on his inspection, '.ravelling every day for about a month afterwards. He longed to write to the man who wrote the article and tell him that, be had given the whole case away. He said exactly what he (Dr. Somervell) said the English people did say—although he was protesting that they did not say it—and he gave the whole case away, exactly as he (Dr. Somervell) pointed out. However, they would let that pass ; perhaps he would write a better article this time. (Laughter.) There was a kind of sone that pretended to be good, a kind which had an enormous vogue in this country and played unon us to a most, extraordinary extent, because we all had what he could only describe as" a silly spot somewhere "—(laughter)—and it could be played upon by what was sham—sham emotion, sham sentiment, things which looked as if they were the real thing and which were not. There was the song about the children besefoot in the snow, the song about choristers who died prematurely of consumption—the snag which dealt with nothing hut disease, death and destitution. (Laughter). That was the song which we sang and enjoyed. If they were getting up subscrip- tions for a soup kitchen it would be all very 'well to talk about bare feet and snow, to play on people's feel- ings. lint to show how the sentiment of these songs was perfectly sham : if they were going to have a. happy evening with their friends, would they pick out hospital report to read—(laughter)—or take up.the latest statistics about starvation in the East end of London. (Laughter). They knew they would not, and yet people sang songs about that kind of thing and everybody said "I-low lovely! Let us have another of the same kind" (Laughter). That showed that the sentiment. or emotion was perfectly sham or we could not as human beings sing about them, to our own enjoyment. After afew words about "his arch enemy in that camp ''—" Ora Pro Nobis " Dr. Somervell observed that the songs he had been referring to were the kind that the Sirens of old used to sing to lure the sailors to destruction. (Laughter.) They were songs that took every body in. and when they beard them the first time they thought " How lovely ! Later, however, they began to think how stupid they were and threw them ewes-. But what did they do next? They ftked up another of exactly the same kind. almost the same words and tune—sang it for a time and then threw it into the waste paper basket and took up another. (Laughter.) The person who chose deliberately that kind of music for his or her enjoyment must end by committing musical shipwreck, because they could not go on singing music like that and not suffer irretrievably in their taste. If they suffered in their taste and in their appreciation of what was beautiful they must suffer very much more than they could think or see. A man could not go on singing vulgar songs every day and not become more vulgar. Did they think persons could sing, trashy inane songs and not themselves become more trashy, silly and inane ? The children that the students lie was now addressing would have to teach hardly knew any good music—it was the greatest rarity if they did. They heard nothing hilt what was trashy or vulgar or both, and he thought if the teachers remembered to teach the children .really good little songs they would everyone of them be an Orpheus to the children indeed, and fill their esrs with such beauti- ful songs that when they heard the songs of the Sirens they would not want to join in them, (Applause) The proceedings concluded with a verse of the National Anthem.</p>
