        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON. ROYAL VISIT-6Tli JULY, 1909. The RECEPTION commir rEE will he very glad if the OWNERS AND OCCUPIERS OF PROPERPY along the ROYAL ROUTE will, by the DECORATION OF THEIR PREMISES assist in tendering a HEARTY WELCOME to THEIR MAJESTIES THE KING AND QUEEN, and they will also be glad if all employers of labour will arrange for the day to be observed as a balf-holiday alter 1 p.m. A PUBLIC STAND will be erected on the vacant land at MARKET GATE for the preferential use of ratepayers of the Borough. Not more than three tickets will be sold to any person. Charges for seats :—Reserved,ss. and 43.; unreserved,3s. The plan of the Stand will be on view and tickets may be obtained at the Office of the Borough Treasurer at and after 10 a.m. on Wednesday next the 30th instant. (Signed) WM. .1. FORSHAW. Mayor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JUNE 26TH, 1909.</p>
        <p>Sir EDWARD GREY, , SIR E. GREY who is the most suc- AND THE cessful Foreign Secre- BUDGET. tary within living , memory, has a peculi- arity which distinguishes him from the whole of his predecessors. He cannot be induced to make foreign affairs the subject of public speeches. • The National Liberal Club :may have had some subtle intention of drawing him into the making of confidences when it asked him to be its guest at a complimentary dinner on Wednesday night. But in that case it must have been as much disappointed in its expectations as it was delighted with the speech he actually made. Our present FOREIGN SECRETARY has known all through what the GERMAN EMPEROR has only recently learned—that the relations of one country with other countries cannot be made the subject of casual speeches without the danger either of grave complications or at the least of great misunderstandings. And this reminds us that, in his speech at the National Liberal Club, Sir EDWARD GREY actually did make a single allusion to foreign politics. It was, we may remark at once, of a character wholly reassuring. There is, he said, no topic under discussion between England and Germany that can lead to any interruption of cordial relations, and there is, he added, nothing within the purview of the British Government which can lead to anything but friendly :relationships with Germany. There is one point of view from which the speech of Sir EDWARD GREY was highly important,. apart from its extraordinary interest and its unpretending beauty of form. It has been persistently asserted by the enemies of the Government that there are serious dissensions in the Cabinet on the subject of the Budget, and proof has been sought for this assertion in the fact that so few Cabinet Ministers have up to the present time taken part in the Budget debates. The Budget, it has been affirmed, is wholly approved only by Mr. ASQUITH and by M. LLOYD GEORGE. Those who know any- thing of what may be called - " behind-the- scenes politics," are quite well aware that the statement is absolutely untrue. There are Cabinet Ministers who have not risen to defend the Budget, simply because they say that their services were not called for. Sir EDWARD GREY is a case in point. He is, perhaps, the most popular member of the Government, if we consider the estimation in which he is held by both parties. As Sir FRANCIS CARRUTHERS GOULD said he has 'won the affections of his party and the respect of his opponents by the simple, dignified, and straightforward manner in which he has steadily vindicated the highest and noblest traditions of our country. Yet he has not thought it neces- sary to make any speech in the House of Commons on the subject of the Budget. Is this because he disapproves of it? His speech last night was almost throughout a defence of the Budget. "I have a great respect for the Budget," he said, arid he pro- ceeded to defend it throughout all its provi- sions with a force and an eloquence that have not been exceeded by any member of the Government. •</p>
        <p>It happened that, only a few hours before the speech of Sir EDWARD GREY, there had been in the City of London a meeting at which the speakers had explained that they were willing to pay almost any sort of taxes but those which they were actually asked to pay, and to any amount but the amount which is now demanded from them. Com- plaints were made that there are short- comings in our Constitution because the House of Lords is not able to do as it likes with money Bills. To the grievances thus put forward, Sir EDWARD GRAY made quite happy reference. There are short- comings in the Constitution, he admitted. They are of a kind which gives almost unlimited opportunity to obstruction.- The Government,he said, is doing nothing of which ample notice has not been given, especially in regard to Old Age Pensions. Some of the principles of the Budget may be new in their application, but they are not new in. theory Under the last Government, there was actually a measure for the taxation of unearned increment. The Budget, explained Sir EDWARD GREY, takes away no man's possessions.: it proposes merely to tax him to a slight extent on what he has not earned. The . tax on unearned increment and on unworked minerals can, he declared, be taken without oppressing either the interests or the industries of the country. He put the case in a phrase when he said that the land will be taxed to-day at the price at which it can be sold to-morrow. "Tested by general principles from any point of view, the Budget will stand." In fact there has been no finer vindication of the Budget from any Minister or Liberal speaker.</p>
        <p>Here is an interesting example- of unearned increment enriching an individual at the expense of the community.. Mr. J. Costello, who twenty-five years ago was born at Calgary, in Alberta, Canada, and who was the first white child born in that place, was endowed by the village authorities with a 25-foot lot. On June 4th he sold his lot for 18,000dols. (or about £3,6oo)—for Calgary is now a prosperous city and land hasdnereased in value enormously.</p>
        <p>At : the collective</p>
        <p>DR. SOMERVELL examination in music ON TRASHY MUSIC. of the students at the Warrington Training College on Tuesday evening Dr. Somervell, H.M. Inspector, referred to our criticism last year of his speech on a similar occasion. It is a pity that Dr. Somer- vell was, as he now tells us, prevented from replying fully, as he intended to do but for the exigencies of his duties, to what was said in this journal. It would have been interesting and perhaps educational, to have been told how and why our remarks entirely supported his destructive analysis of the words as well as the music of "The Lost Chord." Dr. Somervell's speech on Tuesday, which will be found reported in another column, has one great merit as compared with that of a year ago, in that his illustrations of what is trashy in vocal music are much happier and such as probably no-one will take exception to. The matter on which we ventured to differ from Dr. Somervell may be summed up quite simply in the question whether "The Lost Chord" can be placed in the same category as "Ora pro Nobis." We contend that it cannot and that is why we should like to have heard Dr. Somervell's arguments for classifying them both as musical trash.</p>
        <p>The probabilities point WHITECROSS to an uncontested elec- WARD ELECTION. tion to fill the vacancy in Whitecioss Ward. Mr. Henry Roberts has acceded to a request from many of his old constituents and, after a lapse of nearly seven years, he once more seeks to represent the ratepayers on the Town Coun- cil. His nomination will have special interest in view of the fact that Mr. Roberts, then an Alderman of the Borough, resigned his seat on November 7th, 1902 hi the height of the Non-Trading agitation. We have no desire to review here the circumstances which led to such a step. Time, and a clearly expressed public opinion, have, rendered unnecessary any recapitulation of the differences which divided the Town Council and the electorate at that period of local municipal history. Suffice it to say that certain principles affecting the position of every representative of the ratepayers were thrown into the melting-pot of electoral strife and emerged with a clearness of outline and sharpness of definition which placed them beyond all future doubt or question. In his speech to the Whitecross Ward Committee on Wednesday evening Mr. Roberts volun- tarily explained that his connection with the Warrington Slate Company had changed during the period of his absence from the Town Council. He now held only a nominal interest in the Company, and in view of this fact, and Mr. Robets's expressed desire to deyote himself to the service of the municipality, the Ward Committee unanimously endorsed his candidature. Mr. Roberts also dissociated himself strongly from party politics in Town Council affairs and expressed his determina- tion to stand only as an independent candidate. Indications point to his unopposed return, and we may add that corporate affairs to-day afford scope for the full employment of that knowledge and capacity of which Mr. Roberts has previously proved himself possessed.</p>
        <p>The first question WHAT DOES LORD which at once pres- ROSEBERY MEAN? sented itself to the man in the street, when he read Lord Rosebery's letter upon the Finance Bill, was :—What is he driving at? Is he proriosing that a referendum should, be held upon the Budget, and if so, upon the whole of it, or the landtaxes, the super-tax upon incomes, the spirit and licence duties, or upon what else of its provisions in particular? When Lord Rosebery contends that the Budget creates a revolution, be soars into regions of dazzling rhetoric in which the plain man finds it difficult to follow ,him. Nor, indeed, does the ordinary plain man feel any strong temptation to pursue so vague and misty a track. But what he would like to know is this: Does Lord Rosebery intend to convey the sug- gestion that the Budget is taking by surprise the electorate which returned the Liberal Government to power in 1906, as Mr. Balfour's Education Bill took by surprise the electorate which had returned the Conservative Government to power in 1900 in order to finish off the war, an explicit understanding having been entered into, in the speeches of all the responsible Conserva- tive politicians, that the chief difference,s which divided the two great political parties might be sunk for that object? It is hardly possible that Lord Rosebery should imagine that the Budget commits such a breach of an explicit understanding as that. But does he mean that it is guilty of any analogous breach, in a lesser degree, of any under- standing, explicit or tacit, with the constituencies at the last general election? If so, which are the provisions of the Budget which he accuses? It is upon the land-taxes, but especially upon the separate valuation of sites, that the Opposition has concentrated its hostility. The principle of this separation of site value was affirmed more than once in a Conservative House of Commons. Does Lord Rosebery, then, imagine that the constituencies at the last general election cherished the belief that, .in this matter, a Liberal Government would be less progressive than the Conservative Party? If so, then he has been undeceived. The awakening seems to have affrighted him into conceiving a " revolution" where none exists. Mr. Asquith's retort on Thurs- day was ' crushing and conclusive. Lord Rosebery himself took pride in sowing the seeds of the " revolution ' 15 years ago.</p>
        <p>The Chancellor of the THE CASE OF Exchequer has shown AGRICTILTITRE. throughout the greatest anxiety to safeguard agricultural interests in the framing of the Budget. But he very rightly refused to abandon the defferentiation between land that had a purely agricultural value and land for which a claim might be set up that it was agricultural while its value had been doubled, trebled, and quadrupled, or increased by larger multiples still owing to the action of the community. He has announced the Government's intention of devoting a half- share in the product of the land taxes to the relief of local rates. As he pointed out, there would be no equity in giving such a. relief to agricultural land, which bears no part of the increased burden, if it does not even bear its fair share in taxation when it obtains special advantages from the proximity of great towns and the opening up of new industries. The declara- tions made by Mr. Courtenay Warner "as one who was hit all round by the increased taxes," and by Mr. Soares, "'as• representing an agricultural community," deserve atten- tion. Mr.. Courtenay Warner pointed out that the increment tax was a tax on future profits, and "if he made .E4OO or more per acre on his land he would be quite ready to give twenty per cent. to the Government." Surerr a. reasonable point of view! Mr. iSoaress said. that "'the Chancellor of the</p>
        <p>Exchequer might not be a hero in London drawing rooms, but the working portion of the agricultural community valued the benefits he was conferring upon them' and would support the Bill."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>OTIONS.</head>
        <p>OTIONS.</p>
        <p>The married women teacher question, as raised by the action of the Warrington Education Committee in serving notices upon. certain married ladies in their employ, is to be referred fOr settlement to the Chancery Division of the High Court of Justice, by two of the teachers concerned. Mr. H. Roberts suggested on Monday that the persons aggrieved might have got a decision on the question at the cost of a penny stamp by appealing to the Board of Education. Anyhow, a test case in law seems inevitable I now, and the Education Committee is taking steps to defend its position. * * *</p>
        <p>Considerable satisfaction will be felt in the town at the expressed intention of the King to drive through the streets in an open motor car, weather permitting. on the occa- sion of his visit. It was feared, with reason, that he would travel in a covered and closed vehicle,and in that event the patriotic public ensconsed at considerable expense in elevated shop windows would have a very poor view for their money. The top of a motor brougham is not a very inspiring sight even if it does covei• Royalty. King's weather" is the chief desideratum for ensuring complete success to the Royal visit to the borough. * * *</p>
        <p>The Conservative attack Op the Budget has provoked the party in power to retaliatory measures of a unique character. Liberal Members of Parliament, to the number of 250, have met and organised a Budget League, which in the space of 24 hours has appointed a secretary (Sir Henry Norman), engaged officers and a staff, and .1-napped out a cam- paign of speaking and expounding such as the country has never yet seen. One thousand meetings to be held all over the country, from Land's End to John '0 Groat's, are already projected. What the nation does not yet know about the Budget it will soon be taught. Liberals believe in their politics, and have unlimited faith that, once under- stood, Liberal measures of reform will carry the country with thqm. * * * Lord Egerton of Tatton is determined not to wait in order to have distinction thrust upon him. He made a name for himself by banning Rostherne to the cycling and pedestrian community, but he refrained from attributing his action in this direction to the effects of the Budget. That much abused piece of legislation does not, however, escape from a marked indication of his Lordship's further displeasure. He has caused the following circular letter to be sent to a number of persons who have made applica- tions for annual subscriptions :—" 9, Seamore- place, Mayfair, W.—Lord Egerton of Tatton regrets that, owing to the payments of death duties equivalent to 31 years' income, he cannot for the present entertain any pay- ment of subscriptions." Poor fellow! The lot of the lord seems to be worse than the lot of the policeman immortalised by W. S. Gilbert.</p>
        <p>* * * There has been some delightful debating on the new Tariff schedules, now urider dis- cussion in the American Senate. The speeches are a lesson in the inevitable consequence of Protection. The section dealing with fruits showed the best results. The Senators from 'Florida,'where pineapples are raised, wanted the duty upon imported pineapples increased. On the other hand, the Senators from Mary- land, where the fruit is canned, regarded even the present duty as excessive. Then it was pointed out by the Senators from California, where lemons are grown, that it was plainly in the interest of the nation to have an increased duty on foreign lemons; but they were soberly opposed by the Senator from New York, whose constitu- ents consume, but do not grow lemons, and welcome the competition of Italian lemons. Phis Senator assumed that the effect of a duty was to increase the price of the article; but the Californian explained to him that its real effect was to lower the prices, and that a high tariff was the only thing that could bring cheap lemons to New York. In fact, Protectional arguments seem to be much the same the world over.</p>
        <p>The way in which the workers of War- rington are rallying round the Progressive Labour League in its open-air compaign in defence of Free Trade and the advancement of true progress is one of the most gratifying features of local political life. Interest in the meetings shows no sign of waning and on Monday evening another very large audience listened with keen attention to Mr. Samuel Gleave's closely reasoned,temperate and con- vincing exposition of the case for Free Trade. Mr. Gleave is a clever speaker who has the happy gift of presenting facts in a homely and lucid way and illustrating his points with witty anecdotes. In thanking Mr.Gleave for his splendid address,Mr. J. Howes-recalled the interesting fact that his son and Mr. Gleave were school-mates. They are both now doing good service for the Progressive cause in their respective spheres. MI Gleave said that as a boy he looked up to Mr.Howes sen.as one of the orators of the country and he now felt peculiar pride in his recognition of any- thing he bad been able to do to help forward the principles which Mr. Howes had, so long advocated. * *</p>
        <p>A striking feature of the birthday honours list is the number of distinctions that have been conferred in connection with journalism, literature, science and art. Mr. H. W. Lucy, " Toby," of "Punch," once the Parliamen- tary sketch writer, and later the editor of the "Daily News," has received a knight- hood. He well deserves the honour, and he will receive the warm congratulations of his fellow-journalists. Another new knight, ATI.. John Duncan, is the leading pro- prietor of the "South Wales Daily News," which has done yeoman service to the Liberal cause in Wales, and Dr. Carlow Martin, the editor , of the "Dundee Advertiser," who has received the same honour, is well-known as the chief standard- bearer of Liberalism in the Scottish Press. Another new knight, Mr. G. A. Riddell, is the chairman of the " Western Daily Mail" CO:, but he has 'taken little personal part in politics. Dr. Samuel Dill, the well-known writer on Roman life and history, and Mr. F. Galton, a greaf authority oh anthropology and other' subjects, receive the distinction of knighthood. The drama has been honoured •in the same way in the persons of Mr. Pinero and Mr. Beerbohm Tree. Mk. Hobhouse, the Secretary" to 'the Treasury, Sir Hudson Kearley, M.P., Mr. Russell Rea, M.P., and Mr. James Stewart, M.P.., have been made Privy Councillors. No fewer than six members of Parliament have been made baronets—Mr. E. H. Holden, Mr. Massey, Mr. C. D. Rose, Mr. Talbot Scans- brick, 'Mr. Osmond Williams, and Mr. A. Williamson.</p>
        <p>Lord Rosebery has been lamenting that we have not got the Referendum in this country but it seems to have escaped notice how intimately it is used in Spain, of all coun- tries. "There was a general desire in the Royal Family," says Reuter in telling us of the birth of the new Princess," that the Queen on this occasion should present the nation with a Princess." The Queen accord- ingly, with her innate English Constitution- alism, satisfied the " general desire." After this, (says the " Westminster Gazette ") clearly we ought not to hear that the Referendum is not likely to work well. * * *</p>
        <p>American women would never put up with the divorce laws that Englishwomen tolerate. —Mrs. T. P. O'Connor.</p>
        <p>With the opening of the canal, Panama should be one the richest countries in the world.—Mr. H. Savage Landor. I said to the village umpire at a cricket match, in reference to the weather, "It looks as if it's going to clear up." "Ah !" replied the umpire, "that's what the Devil said to Noah."—The Rev. J. G. McCormick, at a meeting of of the Church Reform League.</p>
        <p>The Government for three years endeavoured to lead in the reduction of armaments. That is a clear, consistent, and right policy of the Liberal Party. Our efforts at reduction, however, were not followed by a reduction abroad, but by an increase.—Mr. McKenna, M.P. * * *</p>
        <p>The following remarkable story is vouched for by • a "Manchester Guardian" cor- respondent who has been staying at a Wirral farmhouse. He writes :—" One of the labourers had gone into a shippon to clean it out one morning, and I followed him. Lying close by the door was a female fox stretched out stiff—to all appearance quite dead. I called my host, and he looked at the animal's brush, which, however, was so poor that he decided it was not worth cutting off (the animal was evidently an old one). The labourer fetched a large four-pronged pykle " such as they use to shift manure, Sand shovelling the fox up with this pitched it on to the midden. The moment the animal fell it sprang up, and bounding over the low stone wall which circled the yard shot across the next field and was out of sight. My host, an old Cheshire man, had never seen anything equal to it." The yarn is certainly a bit foxy. * * *</p>
        <p>Ladies will doubtless be interested in the following collection of verdicts upon the Queen's costume on Gold Cup Day at Ascot: 'The Queen, becomingly dressed in cream voile.'—Morning Leader. 'The Queen . . . in a toilette of pale tan colour.'—Daily Mail. 'The Queen wore a lovely dress of beige lace over saffron yellow.'—Daily Express. 'The Queen wore a deep cream voile dress.' —Daily News. 'The Queen wore a cloak of pale blue over a dress of light cinnamon brown.'—Daily Telegraph. 'The Queen went to the races yesterday in a maize-coloured crêpe . dress.'—Daily Graphic. _ _</p>
        <p>The Queen Jooked very charming indeed in a gown of the palest saffron.'—Daily Mirror.</p>
        <p>'The Queen wore a dress of deep cream ninon:—Times.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS. _</p>
        <p>Warrington Bridge. Mr. J. J. Webster, the engineer, will attend the Bridge Sub-committee on Monday next and produce his designs of the New Warrington Bridge.</p>
        <p>Progressive Labour League. Mr. J. Havelock-Wilson M P,. will be the speaker at the open-air meeting in connection with the Pro- gressive Labour League at. Bridge Foot (near Arpley Cannons) on Monday evening next. Local Success. The Association of Book-keeping Teachers have admitted to their ranks Mr. J. Marsh, F.1.P.5., of 15, Rolleston-street Warrington, as an Associate (A.8.T.) Mr. Marsh was also successful in obtaining the Royal Society of Arts first class certificate in Book-keeping, in the advanced stage, at the recent Examinations for students of the Technical Institute Evening Classes the results of which examinations are published in another column.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guilds Street Collection. As a result of the street collection held a fortnight ago on behalf of the National Childrens Home and Orphanage, an institution open to all sects, the sum of :£lB was raised which considering that there was, unfortunately for both, another street collection the same day is considered very satisfactory. The chief amounts were raised by the Bewsey-road Guild ;£7 13s. Od., Padgate Guild ;e5 3s. 6d. TR.A.VELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES, etc. Special lines at 17s.' 205., 225., and 2.05. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Messrs. Crosfielts New Ferry</p>
        <p>The "London Gazette " on Tues-day, contained the following Mersey Conservancy Act. Mersey Con- servancy, 66, Victoria-street, London, 21st June. I hereby give notice, pursuant to An Act for the better preserving of the navigation of the river Mersey' that I have received notice from Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, of their intention to construct gangways on either side of the river Mersey at War- rington, for the purpose of a proposed ferry for their workpeople in accordance with plans and sections deposited at this office.—Signed G. S. NARES, vice- admiral, acting conservator of Mersey." Schoolboy's Football Medals. On Thursday Alderman Tinnion, accompanied by Mr. 11. Ashton (secretary of the Football Club), visited St. Benedict's school and presented silver medals with gold centres, to the, boys who were the runner's up in the final schoolboys football competition. The scholars were assembled in the main room and witnessed the proceedings with enthusiasm. Alderman TinniOn spoke of the value of games from an educational paint of view and congratulated the boys on their success. The Rev. T. RYlance thanked Alderman Tinnion for his kindness in presenting the medals. Mr. Morris (headmaster) on behalf of the boys, spoke of the pleasure it gave them to be treated sO kindly by the football committee. TENNIS, CROQUET, AND GARDEN FURNITURE.— J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Fiddler's Ferry Sailing Club.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last the Fiddler's Ferry Sailing Club held their 3rd handicap of the season over their usual 21 mile course to New Ferry. Four boats crussed the line in a strong North-Westerly breeze. " Maggie " early on in the race assumed the lead, which she retained throughout, beating " Nina" bY 10 seconds. " Hilda " had a bad start, and " Thelma " ran aground on Halton Marsh Point, and had some difficulty in getting off. The finishing times were :—" Maggie" (S. Meacock), 4 hrs. 24 mins. 55 secs.; " Nina " (J. Whittaker), 4 hrs. 25 mins. 5 secs. ; " Hilda " (J. A. Warburton), 4 hrs. 47 mins. 25 secs.; "Thelma "(H. Warburton), 4 hrs. 48.mins. 15 secs. The sail home on Sunday was .pretty stiff, the boats coining up the river with double-roofed mainsails. Sequel to a .. Railway Fatality.</p>
        <p>_ . At the ' Warrington County "Court. on Thursday, before Deputyjudge A. liyalop Maxwell, Mr. T. S. Steel applied on behalf of Eve Darbyshire, a widow, Longshaw-street, lot the • apportionment and pay- ment of compensation in respect to the death of her husband, Reuben Darbyshire, who was killed at Jockey Bridge whilst following- his employment _as a fireman iri the employ of the London and North- Western Railway Cothpany,. The respondents had paid into court the suin'of £lBO is. id., and he *asked for the imMediate payment of £O, and the balanee to be invested and paid to the ' widO* by ' instalments at per mdiath. The application was granted together with costa; on scale B.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS.—Several godd machines on sale in good condition at prices frona 1.2 s Gd.•-51, Bridge st.,•Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Hurt at Play. A youth of seventeen, named William Hilditch, residing in Slip-street, Frodsham, was playing leap frog with some other boys in the village on Sunday when he sustained a nasty fall. He was in great pain, and was conveyed home. As his condition grew worse he pmoved to the Chester Infirmary on Sunday, where it was found that he had fractured his spine. Warrington P.S.A. Mr. Richardson presided at the weekly, meeting of the Warrington P.S.A. on Sunday, and spoke upon Temperance.and its bearing towards Christianity and worship." The principal address was delivered by Mr. Durant, of Prescot, who spoke at length upon "Religion and its enemies." The lesson was read by the secretary, Mr. Clegg, and prayer offered by Mr. Higginson.</p>
        <p>National Service League The annual meeting of the Warrington division of this League will be held by kind permission of Mr. and Mrs. Lyon, at Appleton Hall, on Saturday, July 24th. The Earl of Stamford will preside and his Honour Judge Hans Hamilton will aJso address the meeting. , A short topical play will be amongst the other attractions. Roller Skating.</p>
        <p>The proprietors of the commodious Palatine Rinks, Wilderspool-road and Bridge Foot, announce special attractions for Walking Day and the King's Visit_ The evening session will extend from 7 to 11.30 each evening. On Walking Day there will be a grana hockey match—Olympia (Manchester) v. White City (Minchester)—and on the evening of the King's Visit some interesting competitions will be held, including two-step (lady and gent), 10 laps race (gent), five laps race backwards (gents), A prize will also be given to the best and most graceful lady skater. THE KING'S VISIT.—FLAGS, STREAMERS, BUNT- ING, etc., for sale or hire. Large stock. —GARLAND, . Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Local Musical Successes. At the recent examination held in Liverpool, June 14th and following days, in connection with the Victoria College of Music, London, the following pupils of Mrs. G. Mayhall, A.V.C.M., 148, Knutsford- road, Warrington, passed very successfully. Names and grades as follows :—lntermediate : Miss Eleanor Shaw, Farrell-road, Stockton Heath, Ist class pass with honours; Miss Beatrice Kate Leonard, Grove- street, Warrington, Ist class pass with honours. Primary: Miss Bessie Newton, Knutsford-road, War- rington, Ist class pass with honours; Miss Beatrice Dora Moorlield, Hewittsstreet, Warrington, Ist class Walking Day. The great children's festival on Friday next is being looked forward to with as much interest and pleasure as ever, and only fine weather is needed to ensure success. There has been a change in the arrange- ments for the procession this year. The school children will not assemble on the lawn but will enter the Park by the East gate, pass in front of the Town Hall, and leave by the West gate. The procession will leave promptly at ten o'clock and traverse the following , route :—Arpley-street, Hanover-street, Palmyra-square and Rylands-street, ' turning into Barbauld-street, .Friers' Green, into Bridge-street, thence to Bridge Foot and back to Market Gate and along Horsemarket-street. Tne .Roman Catholics will. immediately follow the Church of England and Non- conformist procession along the same route, but will not pass in front of the Town Hall, as there would be some difficulty in steering the banners along the drives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., £8 Bs.</head>
        <p>GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., £8 Bs.</p>
        <p>.X,9) 0 OM, and £l2 12s. Others at higher' prices.' Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. H. WOOD, 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt. " Y " Branch N.B.W.T.A. . At Penketh House, on Thursday afternoon the members of the above spent a very enjoyable time, through the kindness of 'Mr. and Mrs. Dodds. The weather conditions were very unfavourable, but the time passed pleasantly in music by Misses L. Turton and Millie Oakley, recitations by Misses A. Taylor and D. Tongue, and songs by the Misses Lloyd-Jones and Miss Marjorie Dodds. After tea Mrs. Moore, in a few well chosen words, presented Miss Dodds with a silver egg and toast stand from several of the members, as an expression of good feeling on her approaching marriage. Aliig Podda and Mrs, Dodds suitably responded. Afterwardi all adjourned to the garden, where mathemAtical races were held, Misir Mills winning the prize. Several guessing com- petitions were held, for which prizes were given; the winners being Misses Taylor, Hawthorn, Robinson and Flemming. After singing" God be with you till we meet again "the return journey was made, all_ having spent a very happy time. • Marriage of Mr G. E. Guest.„ A pretty wedding of considerable local interest tOok place at Trinity Wesleyan Church, Southport, on. Thursday, when Mr. George Edwin Guest, son of Mr. E. J. Guest, 8.A., J.P., Warrington. was married to Miss Jane Helen Boston, daughter of Mr. John Boston, St. Wyburn, Birkdale, partner in :the firm of Messrs. J. Shakerley and Company, tanners, Liverpool, Garston and Northwich. The officiating ministers were the Revs. T. E. Keeble, 3.lcCrae, and Tetley. The hymn" 0 perfect lo\re " was sung and the organist plued Mendelssohn's "Wedding March" and the "Bridal March" from " Lohengrin." The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired. She was attended by the Misses A., P. L., and CI Boston (s;sters of the bride) and Miss G. E. Guest (sister of the bridegroom) as bridesmaids. Mr. H. W. Guest (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a recep- tion was held at St. Wyburn. A large number of guests attended, and. later the happy couple left for London and the South, where the honeymoon will be spent.</p>
        <p>THE KING IS COMING. But the King of Tonics</p>
        <p>is here already. H. M. Ashton's Life 'tonic—the</p>
        <p>great strengthener, s. —H. M. ASHTON, 63, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Advt. independent Order of Rechabites. The Wycliffe Tent No. 3647 held a special meeting on Monday the 14th inst. to again initiate honorary members, namely Mr. John Upson J.P., and Councillor Joseph Hawthorn. The ceremony was conducted by Bro. T. Longshaw, District C.R., assisted by Bro. Joseph Hill, Past Districi, C.R., of Wigan. Several other district officers were present. The meeting was held in the Wycliffe Church Parlour, the Rev. John Yonge and Mr. Joseph Owen (both honorary members) of the Tent were .also present. After the initiation. ceremony • Ind been conducted, Bro. W. Wright, District Trustee presented the new honorary members with a certificate of membership each, at the same ,time giving them a hearty welcome to the Rechabite cause. Several other officers also spoke and the new honorary members suitably replied. A letter was read from Mr. Dodds who was away from town, and expressed how pleased he was that Mr. Upson and Councillor Hawthorn were being made members of the Tent. It was only in April that the same district officers had the pleasure to visit Penketh House to formerly initiate Mr. and Mrs. J. Dodds, hon. members of the Wycliffe Tent. This Tent was the last to be opened in Warrington, only being instituted on November 16th, 1907, with a membership of 10. The present members stand at 21 and include the following as .hon. members, Rev. John Yonge, Mr. Joseph Owen, Mr. and Mrs. James Dodds, Mr. J. Upson, J.P., and Councillor Joseph Hawthorn, which is the record for hon. membership in the Warrington District, N. 4. Death of Mr. Harry Sutton.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Joseph Harry Sutton, which took place at! his residence, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, on Saturday morning. Mr. Sutton, who was forty-two years of age, was a son of the late Alderman Sutton. In his early days he served as an articled clerk to Messrs. Banks and Kendal, solicitors, Liverpool, and ultimately became a partner in the firm of Messrs. Thomas Sutton and Sons, Legh-street, Warrington. For the past twelve months he had been in failing health and had not been able to take his usual active share in. business. Mr. Sutton was a good linguist, a connoisseur of china and coins, and was also keenly interested in political matters, being a member of the Warrington Debating Society. He was accounted one of the finest amateur swimmers in Lancashire and had won numerous prizes, having defeated several of the best known exponents of the natatory art. He leaves a widow and one child with whom much sympathy is felt in their bereavement.—:The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday, the Rev. W. Bracecamp 'officiating. Amongst the mourners were: Messrs. Alfred,and Arthur Sutton (brothers), William Fun and Frank Foulkes (brothers-in-law), Harry Hind, Jack Smith, Oswald Hudson, A. H. 4Clive, and Enoch Lockett; Messrs. Orford Johnson, P. Price, Crompton and Joseph Gerrard (members of the staff of MessrS. Thomas Sutton and Sons). Floral tributes Were sent by Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Sutton, Mr. and Mrs Henry Cookson, Mrs. Mason (Manchester), office staff of Messrs. T. Sutton and Sons, Mrs. John Sutton and family, Mr. Oswald and Mrs. Hudson, Mr. and Mrs. H: Clive; Mi.: and Mrs. E. Lloyd-Jones and family, and Mr. Enoch Lockett.</p>
      </div>
