        <p>.. •. Backache iN:ir fll/4 ; ...,• -- , . .., -,„ 1 ••:k 0., .. It bl'.. ' f. 4,41 *v?„ ~„ 1 ~•, ~ 4, to . s • ..,. xv.., •,....</p>
        <p>WATER POLO.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. WOOLTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington commenced their league programme on Tuesday evening, when they played Wootton at, the Legh-street Baths. The home team came off success- ful after • a keenly contested game. Warrington defended the shallow .end in the first half, and in less than a minute after play began Pemberton scored for Warrington with a stinging shot. Woolton vier() now seen to better advantage and Martin, Richardson and Lott had futile shots at goal. Lott swam bravely for Woolton. but when about to shoot he was cleverly robbed by ‘Voolstencroft. Warrington then pressed and after a foul, Pepper scored from a shot close in. Both teams were playing in determined fashion and there was little to choose between them. Woolton now played in a superior manner and Lott after being left unmarked beat Woods. Play after this was of a rpirited character but no more goals were registered before the, interval. Resuming, the rtme was again vigorously fought, each end being visited in turn. At length lackron sent in a succersful shot for the visitors, placing the teams on level terms. Play at this period of die game vas very rough and fouls were frequent. Pepper added Warrington's third goal from a shot close in. Result :—Warrington, goals;a, Woolton, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SCHOOLBOYS' HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLBOYS' HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>A two-breadth schoolboys' handicap was held with the following result :-1, Taylor ; 2, Makin ; 3, Miller.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>COLLIERY ACCIDENT SEQUEL. Compensation for a Kick From a Pony'</head>
        <p>COLLIERY ACCIDENT SEQUEL. Compensation for a Kick From a Pony'</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday before his Honour Deputy-Judge Hyslop Maxwell, a claim for compensation under the Workmen's Compensation ict was brought by John Dutton, 13, St. John-street, Earlestown, the respondents being the Collins Green Colliery Company. Mr. E. Cooper Burgis, barrister, -.Manchester (instructed by Messrs. Fielding and Ferni- hough, Bolton), appeared for the applicant, and Mr. Peace, of Wigan, for the respondents. . Mr. Burgle said the applicant was employed as a horse-keeper by the respondents, and his average wages were 355. a week. Applicant, in the course of his duties had to attend to sick and injured horses and in March be was attending a pony down the pit, which bad been cut. on the hip, when the animal kicked him on the ain. He did not think much about the injury at the time, but subsequently it becameworse, and he sent a letter claiming compensation, as he was unable to go to his work. A letter was received from the respondents that they had no evidence of any injury to the applicant, or a report of any accident. The point in dispute was' as to whether there was an accident to the applicant on the date in question. Evidence in support was given by the applicant, Aaron Smallshaw and John Burke. Dr. Mouncey, of Earlestown. said that when the applicant came to him he was suffering from a 'contused wound on the outer side of the left leg, whioh appeared to have been inflicted by some blunt instrument. The wound was gaping and looking unhealthy, and he (Dr. 3. founcey) insisted upon the man ceasing work. Ulcera- tion set in and at one time there was a patch the size of the palm of the hand which was riddled with boles. If the wound had been washed immediately it would prob- ably not have taken bad ways. It would certainly be ..several weeks before the applicant would be able to work.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Peace submitted that applicant did not give written notice of the accident, as soon as practicable, as required by the Act, and that respondents had been seriously prejudiced in consequence. He contended that the words" as soon as practie.ible " must be con- strued to mean immediately. Mr. Burgis said his view of the words "as soon as practicable" was that it meant as soon as was reason- able, in view of the circumstances of that particular case. The injury was not one which the ordinary man</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>7 lase</head>
        <p>7 lase</p>
        <p>would expect to he fraught with serious consequences, and notice was given as soon as the applicaot realized the serious nature of his injuries. Obadiah Harrison, sub-menet:Pr of the pit, admitted thatapplicint informed him of the accident in a letter addressed to witness's private address on March 260, but witness did not regard that as a formal communica- .tion to him in his official capacity. His Honour believed that notice was given as soon as practicable and, if he were wrong on that point, he found that respondents had not been prejudiced by want of such not ice.He awarded the applicant 16s. 3d. a week, from the date of the accident, with costs on Scale B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Derelict."</p>
        <p>This new drama from the. pen of Mr. Walter Nixey, has the great merit of originality and the power of compelling interest throughout. It is very cleverly written and contains some powerful situations which afford good scope for artistic acting. "The Derelict" is a man who, through drink and weakness, has become a social outcast, but he has his good points and although it is in his power to blight the career of a successful.rival he restrains any desire to pay off old scores and in the end is acclaimed the hero of the</p>
        <p>piece. Mr. Herbert Skardon, as Reginald Grant, "The Derelict" gives a most realistic study and shows that he is possessed of talents of a high order. Miss Sidney Crowe as Lucie Legardie, the scheming adventuress, grips the audience from the start with her clever portrayal, and the part could not be in better hands. A popular character is that of Pro- fessor Jamieson and Mr. Augustus Hammond acts the absent-minded philosopher as to the. manner born. Mr. Leonard ,Booker is remarkably convincing as Clarke, a financier, and Mr. Robertson Fouliss impersonateds Leon Legardie, the blind ex-convict, with great skill. Mr. Donald Gilbert does well as Ernest Hampden, althoUgh he has been handicapped by indisposition, and Miss Marie Fontaine (Mrs. Hainpden), Miss Effie E. Crawford (Hilda) and Mr. Knott (Musgrave) are seen to advantage in their parts. The scenery and dresses are most elaborate and the production as a whole is a complete success. The orchestra, under Mr. Norman Latham's conductor- ship, has earned hearty rounds of applause for a brisk selection embodying a medley of popular airs, which is admirably performed. . NEXT WEEK'S COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. Herbert Fuller's company will appear in two plays of great interest. On Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and Saturday " A Noble Mother," written by Mr. Fuller, will be presented. Miss Agnes Collier will take the leading part of Rita Lingham, " d noble mother " and Mr. Edward E. Louis will impersonate Harry Gilbert, an English farmer. On Thursday and Friday the attraction will be be the great mystic play " The Sorrows of Satan," adapted from the romance by Marie Corelli. Migs Agnes Collier will appear . as Lady Sybil and Mr. Herbert Fuller as Prince Lucio Rhnanez. "MISS LANCASHIRE LIMITED."</p>
        <p>Miss Florence Baines, who scored a great success in Warrington on a previous visit as "Miss Lancashire Limited," cabled sometime ago to Mr. Loader (the general manager of the Royal Court Theatre) from Australia that she was .returning to England. Our readers will be pleased to hear that arrangements have been made for her to appear at the Court Theatre in " Miss Lancashire, Limited " at an early date. As she has only 14 weeks at liberty, and as she has been inundated with requests for a visit, it is all the more gratifying that she has agreed to devote one of her first weeks to Warrington.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Alderman Smethurst (in the chair), Messrs. W. Roberts, George Pilling, and William Furness.</p>
        <p>THS SKATING RlNK.—Charles Hassan and Bertram Si!cock applied for an extension of the hours tor the skating rink of the Palatine Rinks, Limited. for 2nd July and 6th July by two hours, viz., from TO p.m. to 12 midnight.—Mr. Roberts : It is too late. Eleven o'clock is late enough. That is the time the public- houses close.—The Chairman said the Bench seemed to be rather tired of those applications, but they wefe in the majority that it should be granted.</p>
        <p>SLEFErnai 01:T.—Frank Sw'anton, lodging in Rose and Crown-street, was charged with sleeping in a house in the course of erection in Norwood-street, on Saturday evening and was also charged with not having any visible means of subsistence.—P.C. 57 spoke to arresting prisoner. He gave his name as Frank Simpson. Defendant said he worked at Carton's on Friday, but he did not think they would take him on until his eyes were better.—The Chief Constable said that he would make enquiries and see if the man could start work agtin.—The case was dismissed on condition that prisoner started work. On Tuesday it transpired that that prisoner's statement was untrue and he was sent to gaol for 14 days hard labour. ALLEGED THEFTS FROM RAILWAY COMPANY.— Herbert Rose, Cumberland-street, was charged with stealing, from the London and North-Western ware- house Bank Quay, three pairs of men's boots and two pairs of boy's boots (value El 155.) on June 20th also with steeling a gentleman's waterproof coat value £3 3s. on June 1 • and also with stealing two rolls of satin cloth on the 24th May of the value of £5 is. 2d.—The Chief Constable said thatDetectiveClarkeand another man were on duty at the warehouse at midnight on the 20th June and they heard a. noise inside the warehouse, so they weat in and found the prisoner about 20 yards from the door. They arrested him and brought him to the Police Station. At prisoner's house the police had discovered goods to the value of £4O or £5O, which were the property of the London and North-Westere Railway Company. They hid also found at prisoner's house a bicycle worth something like 14 or 15 guineas, which was stolen from the Cheshire Lines warehouse about 15 months ago.—The Chief Constable asked for a remand until Tuesday week.—The Bench adjourned the case accordingly.</p>
        <p>"HAVE MERCY ON A. POOR OLD MAN."—George Lovatt or Frank Webb of no settled abode ' was charged with being drunk and disorderly on gunday last. Prisoner who was 74 years of age and an old groom at the Patten Arms Hotel, said he had never been locked up before and "he hoped the bench would have mercy upon a poor old man. No doubt he bad driven them in his clh years ago." He pleaded with the bench and in the end they consented to let him go on condi- t ion that he went to Liverpool where he iaid he could find work washing cabs.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS OFFENCE.—James Morgan of no settled abode was charged with indecently exposing himself on Sunday last in a shop in Winwick-road.—The Chief Constable said that such cases were becoming very prevalent, and he hoped prisoner would be made an example of.—Nlorgan maintained his innocence, hut after hearing the evidence the magistrates sent him to goal for three months. _</p>
        <p>- CHARGE AGAINST A WORKMAN —At the Warrington Police Court, on 'londly, Join, M'Lay, Appleton-street, was charged with using obscene language on June 12th. Defendant did not appear. Mr. A. Browne, who prose- cuted, said the offence was in connectiou with the strike in progress at Messrs. Whyman's Foundry. The defon- dant had been before the court previously. On the date in question one of the workmen was met in the street by the defendant, who commenced to abuse him and used bad language. The Chairman (Alderman J. Smethurst) said he wts sure the strike could be settled by arbitration. It was costing a deal of money, and causing anxiety and ill-feeling. He would do his best to bring about a settlement. A warrant was issued for tbe arrest of M`Lay.,</p>
        <p>TURSDAY.—Before Messrs. H. Roberts (in thr3 Rymer Young, W. Richardson, and C. Jackson }Lames. DOLMANS-LANC AGAlN.—Cattherine Davies. or Jones, Back Dolman's-lane, was charged with having used obscene language iw Dolman's-lane on Monday night, and also with wilfully breaking a window in the house 21a, Back Dolman's-lace. The glass was valued at Is. 33.—P.0. Berry stated that he u as called to Dolman's- .l.ine on Monday night and saw the prisoner there who was using very bad language, and asked witness to lock her up. She also broke two windows at the house of her mother.—Prisoner had nothing to say.—The sCtiief Constaole said prisoner had been before the Court 28 times plevionsly.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month with bard labour.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF STficx INC s.—James Hampqon was charged with having stolen from outside the shop of Richard Tebbartt, 115, -Bridge-street, threa pairs of stockings. He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable ss:d that -Mr. William Russell, cab proprietor saw prisoner take the stockings from the shop. Prisoner had given three addresses in Manchester, and he (time Chief Constable) asked for a remand until Saturday in order that his character could be inquired into.—The Bench granted the remand.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair) and other magistrates. TFIEETS FROM THE MARKET.—James Harris, Albert, Bent, 18, George-street, and Frank Stokes, 3, Edward- street, were charged with stealing from a stall in the market place five vests, boy's snit, and boy's jacket, value 18s. 3d , the property of William Itosenburgh. on June sth. Harris and Bent were further charged with stealing from the shop, 166, Lovely-lane, four packs of playing clrds, value 3s. fld., the property of Frederick Adams. Harris was further charged with stealing from the shop, 3, Scotland-road. six packs of playing cards, value 4s. 6d., the property ef Charles Oliver. The Chief Constable said Mr. Rosenburgh occupied a stall in the market place. He left it secure at 10.30 p.m. on Satur- day, June sth. On the following morning P.C. Coombs found the stall out c f order. The locks had been taken away and the stall left insecure. On an examination of the stock it was found that certain articles of clothing were missing. Inquiries were made by the police and it was ascertained that the prisoners were implicated in the theft, one of the vests being pledged by the prisoner Harris for a shilling, while another was bought by Mrs. Siloock, a second-hand dealer, who did not appeir to have experienced caution in accepting the vests from the boy Harris. The lads Stokes and • Bent appe ired to have been led away by the prisoner Harris. In reference to the other charges, on Friday June 17th Harris went into the shop of Mr. Oliver and stole the cards, afterwards selling them in the neighbouthood of • BewSey. So far as Harris was concerned everything had been done for h;ro. For a time he was under the care of Mr. Firth, who found hint employment and he went all right for a time. Now he had broken out again. He had been 3 times previously Lefore the court. —The magistrates hound over Stokes and Bent. Harris was sentenced to gaol for 3 months in each charge, the sentence to run concurrently.</p>
        <p>- - ASSAULT ON A WlFF..—liobert Ferber, Fox-street, was sammoned for assaulting his wife, Emma Forber.— Complainant said that on Monday.bist he came home drunk, and while she was preparing his tea be throw an asbpan, which missed its mark. Later he threw a kettle at her, which struck a neighbour. He continued his violence and rushed her into a corner and hurt her head. —ln reply to the clerk witness admitted that they had been separated once previously some years ago, but only for an hour, as her husband came and apologised and she took him back.—On defendant promising to sign the pledge and to be of good behaviour the magistrates dismissed the case.</p>
        <p>Ii; 1110 --_ - - t 2, RiTs 44 _</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>. et- 0 Ral?</head>
