      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Warrington Occasional Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Occasional Court.</p>
        <p>At the Occasional Court, on Monday, before Mr. James Hepherd, Frank Millington, who lives in a lodeing-bouse, off Butterrnarket-street, was charged with stealing a couple of fowls, valued at 55.. the property of Mr. ;Joseph Hall, Daintith Hall Farm, Appleton. Mary Elizabeth Ward, housekeeper for Mr. Hall, said tint prisoner was on the premises on May 10th, engaged in killing a pig, and he was there on sub- sequent dates. He was also there on June 16th, and after he had left, she missed two fowls. Frederick Bonnell, of the Cattle Market Inn said that prisoner called at his house and asked him if he could do with a couple of fowl. He said they Isere his own. He said he had a little farm at StreLton,but he was overstocked and was killing some of the fowl off. Witness bought one. Detective Clough spoke to arresting prisoner, and said that when questioned prisoner said he had bought them from a man near Wilderspool Brewery. Prisoner was remanded to the Dareshury Sessions, to be held on July 13th.—John O'Neil, of no settled abode, was charged with breaking into the shanty on the farm of Robert Priestner, Acton Grange, and stealing a pair of boots, valued at ss. 6d., the property of Thomas Gibbin, also with breaking into a shanty on Jane 11th, at Appleton, and stealing a vest and neckerchief, the property ot James Murray, and also with stealing from a shanty at Strettori, an overcoat sod vest, the property of Edward Cosgrove. Similar evidence was given in each case to the effect that the prisoner had plwned the articles at different shops. Detective Clough, of the Borough Police, gave evidence to the effect that he arrested prisoner. Prisoner, who had notiiing to say, was committed for trial to the Cheshire Quarter Sessions.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectu3l in most difficult cases. }or slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.-- WRITE S; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw et., opposite top of Bold St., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Alleged Extravagance in Asylum</p>
        <p>Administration.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, the Rev. T. 11. Matthews presiding. There were also present :—Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Simpson, Rev. M. Ryan, Dr. Murray, Messrs. G. Mather, A. J. Jolley, E.L. Bent, J. Billince, W. Gill, J. Evans, A. Brooks, W. Honehton, W. Furness, R. Lee, R. Bolton, M. W. Seton, W. Flanagan, J. Backhouse, A. Bottomley (clerk), R.W. Peacock (assistant clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superiritendent of the Cottage Homes).</p>
        <p>TREATS FOR THE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The Padgate Cottage Homes Committee resolved that the children and officers be allowed to attend Warrington Walking Day, that their railway fares be paid one way, and that the annual treat he given to the children on the same day. It was recommended that £7 10s. be granted for prizes, also that the children be allowed to attend Padgate Walking Day on the 26th Inst. and the Cheshire Lines Engineers Cricket Club Sports on July 10th.</p>
        <p>ORDERS UPON TRADESMEN. At the meeting of the Cottage Homes Committee orders upon tradesmen for the Michaelmas qusrter were made as follows t—drugs, sundries, Mr. Harold Smith ; paint, &amp;c., Mr. Whittle ; plumber's work, Mr. Whittle ; machinery repairs, Mr. C. Cotton; wood and iron work end repairs, Mr. Antrobus ; ironmongery, Mr. H. N. HougUou ; team labour, Mi. Fryer; repairs to clocks, Mr. Fernley; dentistry, Mr. Taylor ; seeds and farm requisites, Mr. Starkey ; • stationery. Messrs. Garside and Jolley ; repairs to spectacles, Mr. J. R. Young. The minutes were approved. • At a meeting of the House Committee on June iSth,it was recommended that tradesmen's orders for the Nlichnelmas reiarter be pliced as follows :—Plumber, Mr. Harold Whittle; blacksmith, Mr. George Allen; building requisites, Mr. J. P. Booth; ironmongery, Mr. H. N. Houghton ; horse and hetrse, Mr. E. Connor ; electrical requisites. Mr. H. Bibby. These minutes were also approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CONFERENCE. REPRESPNTATIVES.</head>
        <p>CONFERENCE. REPRESPNTATIVES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. R. Nlatthews, Messrs. Mather, Rent and the Clerk were appointed delegates to the North Western Poor Law Conference to be held in Manchester on the 23th inst., and Mr. Billinge and,the Clerk were deputed to attend a meeting of the Standing Com- mittee of representatives of Lancashire Union Assess- ment Committees. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RELIEVING OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>RELIEVING OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that he had reeived 95 spplica- tions for the two appointments of relieving officers which had beei advertised. One applicdnt had since withdrawn. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. BILLINGE moved that the applications be referred to the Finance Committee with authority to select .six candidates to appear before the Board.</p>
        <p>M iSS BROADB. N'T scconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. BOLTON stid he did not think such a com- mittee wonld facilitate business. Many of the appli- cants would be unknown to the sub-committee, arm he thought if the letters were at once dealt with by the Board there would be a better selection. He moved that that be done. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Rev. Father. RYAN seconded. It was a duty incumbent upon every Guardian to select the best men to fill these important posts, and they should sll have a yoke in the selection. _</p>
        <p>Ultimstely it was decided to refer the applications to the General Purposes Committee, which consists of the whole Board. _ _</p>
        <p>The CLERK submitted a letter from the Local Govern- ment Board sanctioning the re-arrangement of the relief districts, and the formation of an additional district. ASYLUM ADMINISTRATION—IS "HERE EXTRAVAGANCE? The CLERK read a letter from the Clerk to the Lanca- shire County Council intimating that the Asylums Board hwl resolved that the rate of maintenance for pauper patients received from Unions within the County in the Boards Asylums be increased from 9s. 4d. to 9s. lid. per week per patient, such increase to take place on and after July Ist. - - .</p>
        <p>Mr. HOUGHTON asked if the rate had ever reached that figure before? The CLERK lam not aware of it.. during the past ten years. I remember it reaching 9,;. 4d. but not 9s. lid., although some counties charge over les. Mr. HOUGHTON: Is it worth while sending a gentle letter to say we consider it an exorbitant increase? The CLERK: We ' should have to make inquiries before coming to it resolution of that kind. The Rev. Father RYAN: Couldn't we get other Boards of Guardians to join in protesting? The CLERK: We should want some facts to work on. Mr. MATES ER. : In one case they said it was because of the high assessment whiYh thy Assessment Committee of that particular district had placed on the Asylum. Mr. HOUGHTON : There is gross extravagance in the management of these places in some departments. Mr. MATHER : Perhaps the Press will take notice of that. and no doubt it will get to the authorities. The matter then dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>CLAIMS OF THE INFIRMARY._</head>
        <p>CLAIMS OF THE INFIRMARY._</p>
        <p>_ The CLERK read a letter from the Secretary of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary (Mr. J. If. J. Howson) avkirg the Board to consider an increase of their annual snbscription to the institution. He po:nted out that, this year the Board of Management felt com- pelled, in the interests of the sick poor who could be treated in the hospital in a very much better manner that in their own in many cases insanitary and unsuit- able surroundinus, to open another ward atm' estimated increased annual expenditure of £5OO. It was estimated that at the close or the present. year there would be a debit balance of £3,272. The work of the infirmary greatly assisted to reduce the poor rate and whilst acknowledging the sympathy invariably shown by the Board of Guardians, his Board made an earnest appeal for an increased c,mtribution. - In answer to the Chairman, the CLERK said the Board's present subscription to the Infirmary was £2O. Mr. BENT moved that the appeal be referred to the Finance Committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. BROOKS seconded and asked for the sympathy of the Board because the Assessment Committee were very strong in keening the assessment of the Infirmary at its present level. That committee did not meet as a charitable committee hut to endeavour to do their duty, and if the ratepayers were to subscribe in any form through that Bo4rd, it should be in an open and honest manner such as the Secretary of the Infirmary now suggested. Mr. BENT said he wished the Finance Committee to consider the legal position, as to whether the Board had a right to :give ,more than £2O. The appeal bad his sympathy end support. The CRAIRM AN : It 18 a worthy object. Mr. BROOKS: And undoubtedly an object which saves this institution a very considerable ametmL The Board unanimously expressed itself in favour of an increased subscription, but left the Finance Com- mittee to suggest the amount.</p>
        <p>INCREASED EXPENDITURE. The CLERK repotted the receipt of a return from the Local Government• Board In respect to the expenditure on indoor and outdoor relief (except lunatics) during the half year ended Michaelmas, 1908; such return having been requested by Mr. Masterman, M.P. for presentation to the House of Commons. According to the return the increase in the expenditure in the War- rington Union in 'comnarison with the corresponding half-year of 147, was £B3l or 12 per cent. Mr. A. Boots: Is there any data as to increase of population? The CLERK : No, sir. It gives the population according to the census of 1901. Mr. BROOKS : It is an absolute increase,without any releard to percentaee on population. Mr..IoLLEY : The increase of population would be under 2 per cent. The CLERK : The return states that the increase is due to the increased numbers maintained.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. BROOKS: I think it might be very easily explained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SALE OF THE GASKELL PICTURES Elf, Prices for a Constable and a Turner.</head>
        <p>SALE OF THE GASKELL PICTURES Elf, Prices for a Constable and a Turner.</p>
        <p>The sale of the first portion of the collection of pictures and drawings formed by the late Mr. Holbrook Gaskell, of Woolion, Livernool (fnrmerly of Hill (iliff°, Warrington), took place at Christie's Galleries, London, on Thursday afternoon. They have been" on view" all this week, and have attracted a good deal of attention. the rooms having been visited by large numbers of dealers, collectors and others.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gaskell, like most of the picture collectors in Lancashire and the North of England, had a penchant for the English school of painting, and his collection consisted for the most part of works by recognised masters of the school. Many of them reached him by way of the auction-room, purchases having been made on his behalf at the sales of such well-known collections as the Gillott (1872), Farnworth (1874), Hertel) (1874), Grant (1877), Stone Ellis (1877), Arden (1379), Holdsworth (1881) and M aurrieta. (1892), while others, such as ttre works by Sir Alma Tadema and Sir Luke Fades, were bought from the artists themselves. A great many of the pictures have been exhibited in various parts of the country, Mr. Gaskell always having lent a ready ear to applica- tions for loans to public exhibitions, not only in this country, but also in Scotland and Ireland. The total of the day's sale, which comprised 129 lots, was -£45,467. _ . _</p>
        <p>Turner's celebrated picture "The Burning of the Houses of Lords and Common, October 16th, 1834," was knocked down to Mr. Agnew for the enormous sum of 12,500 guineas, and the last picture painted by Constable, Arundel Mill and Castle," was se3ured by Mr. Knoedler, who bought the picture, it was whispered, for America, for 8,400 guineas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>NEW ZEALAND'S GROWING POPULATION.</head>
        <p>NEW ZEALAND'S GROWING POPULATION.</p>
        <p>The popuiatian of New Zealand increased in 1908 by 31,158, the greatest increase since 1880. The surplus of births over deaths was 18,897, and of arrivals over departures 19,261. The number of marriages, on a population basis, advanced from six to nine per thousand during the period from 187 to 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>twit</head>
        <p>twit</p>
        <p>The anniversary services were held last Sunday in connection with the above, and were attended with success. file Rev. J. B. Turner, of Clougn Fold, Rossendale Valley, specially addressed himself to the children in the morning and in the afternoon nod evening peached to crowded congregations. Special authains and hymns were sung at each service. The collections which were in aid of the Church fauds amounted to £3l 2s. .64. Tea was provided for those who attended the afternoon service and wished to stay for the evening.</p>
