      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday last, presided over by the Secretary. Mr. Nightingale gave the address upon" We have this treasure in earthen vessels Ile referred to the criticism of the Rev. F. R. Dean of Reddish, upon P.S.A.'s and said that if the rev. gentle- man had been there that day and heard the singing of the hymns he would have felt that there was some spiritual power in their midst. He claimed for P.S.A, men the power of God within. They. could not say all the good that they had done, nor could they say all the good they were going to do, because that did not belong to them. It was theirs to do and God would give the success in His own way. The Chairman returned thanks to the brethren tor their vote of sympathy to him on the death of his only sister, also on behalf of Mr. and Irs. Wycherley on the death of their son, who ,was a member of their brotherhood. The chairman moved a vote of sympathy to Mr. Payton and his family on the death of his mother. The Chair- man moved a vote of congratulation to Mr. William Crosskill on this the anniversary of his marriage day and also his birthday, having reached threescore years and ten, and wished him and his wife many happy returns of the day. Miss Jessie Fatrar, A.T.c.L. sang effectively "0 Divine Redeemer" and "Come unto Me." Mr. W. 11. Payton, Mus.' Bac., presided at the organ. Mr. W. Crosskill read the lesson. The Secretary led the prayer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Wesley Guilds. Great Gathering at Knowsley Park.</head>
        <p>Wesley Guilds. Great Gathering at Knowsley Park.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last, by kind permission of the Earl of Derby, a united tete organised by the Liverpool Dis- trict Wesley Guild Council was held in Knowsley Park. Mmy hundreds of members were present from all parts of South Lancashire and Wirral, the Warrington contingent alone nuarbering nearly 200. The visitors were received at the entrance by Rev. N'illirun Collett Superintendent of the St. Helens and Prescot circuit and Dr. Bennett of Warrington vice- president of the Liverpool District Council. Fortu- nately the weather though dull in the afternoon improved later on, and the very beautiful evening in delightful woodland glades was enjoyed by all. A splendid programme of sports, including jumping, races of various kinds, a cricket match, and a tug-of-war competition had teen arranged by the Secretary, Mr. Edney Peters, of *est Kirby, and all were treely indulged in; the tug-of-war particularly causing great excitement.—Tea was served in the park, eight long tables each capable of seating about 50 people, were arranged like the spokes or a wheel with the centre acting as a commissariat department for the whole set. Several sittings down, about 400 each time, had to he a, ranged to accommodate the assembly, hut the whole were satisfactorily got through owing to the number of willing helpers, chiefly from the St. Helens and Prescot Guilds, who had undertaken to conduct this very important and onerous part of the proceedings. After tea, pleasant rambles through the extensive park were much appreciated, great interest being taken in the huge grand stand, even then almost complete, which kiss been erected for the visit of His Majesty on July sth, to review the Territorials, the Royal pavilion particularly being a great object of curiosity. Two very admirable out-door concerts had been arranged, one provided by the Warrington Circuit Wesley Guild Cnoir under that splendid conductor Mr. W. Hipkiss, the other by the Earlestown Guild Spanish Choir, with Miss Watkins at the bead. A Special platform pay with bunting had been erected against a wood, giving a very fine effect to the solos and choruses rendered, whilst the appearance was most picturesque, the gay costumes of the Spanish Choir particularly against the dark green of the woodlands being most pleasing to the eye. Selections of • music were given during the afternoon and evening by the Ntugrove prize band. After the concerts a public meeting was held presided over by Alderman E. Lewis Lloyd, J.P., or Liverpool. Votes of thanks to the Earl of Derby for his great kindness in allowing the free use of the park, and to all who bad helped to make the event so successful were heartily supported. The pro- ceedings concluded about 8.30 p.m., the Warrington party consisting of a long procession of 'times and bicycles arriving back about 10 p.m., the transport arrangements having been carefully arranged by Messrs. W. E. Plinston, F. E. Norman and N Mime Healey with the Rev. P. W. Kenai&amp; in ministerial charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church Anniversary.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road 'Wesleyan Church And was celebrated on Sunday last. The Rev. J. H. Moulton, M.A., D. Lit., of Didsbury College, was the preacher both morning and evening, and his eloquent addresses dealt chiefly with scriptural matters. In the morning the rev, gentleman took as his text "Whist ye not .that I must be about. my Father's business ' (2nd St. 'Luke, part of 49th verse). He commenced his discourse by explaining that he would deal with the word must—the most important word in the English language—in a spiritual way and not generally—general thoughts would ensue from his remarks on things spiritual. The word must ran through all the life of Jesus Christ. It began at the beginning of our Lord's life, and his whole life was determined by that little word must. Continuing, the preacher said that what Christ knew of the Bible he got front the synagogue. In those days it was impossible for Jesus' father (Joseph) to be the possessor of a Bible in his position. Jesus eagerly asked questions of the professors in the religious law of the country, and in turn they listened attentively to the boy. The preacher illustrated how Christ's life was dominated by the word must. For instance. When Christ was left behind in the Temple by his father and mother who turned back for Him, Christ turned to Mary and asked "How could they anxiously seek Him? Surely they must have known that the place to Mad Him was His Father's house." That, was the secret of the whole life of Jesus. Jesus received His strength for His daily labour in the quiet communion with the Father in Heaven, while others sought their strength through sleep. Christ said he must preach—bow that compul- sion drove, Him on from place to place ! The one supreme beauty of His life was that he must, and He felt that he must tell the people of the good news of the Kingdom of God. The 'preacher gave a further instanre of the word must in Christ's travels, and that was when Be said to Zachens, "I must abide at thy house," and also that. He must go from Galiee to Samaria." Why must He go? Was it not that He should spell( to that poor woman by the well, who was to he the means of bring- ing many to him? Jesus maintained that to keep the Sabbath properly was to do the will of God, and by doing that perpetual rest would be obtained. When Jesus said he roust, it was not a must of r signation. He pressed on knowing that the scripture nutst he ful- filled. The great men of the Bible were governed by the word must. Speaking especially to the young people of the congregation, the speaker said that the whole of their success in life depended upon tbe way in which they realised the word must. Life consisted of a great battle between two words,—wilt and must. Everything for them depended upon which won the victory. Concluding Dr. Moulton said that in God's will was our peace, and we should only have the peace, when,duty had become an instinct, and the will of man did for ever the perfect will of God. In the evening Dr. Moulton preached to a large con- gregation basing his sermon on the text" Children,how hard it is to enter the Kingdom of Heaven." (St. Mark 10, part 24th.) During the service the choir sang very effectively, "Send out Thy Light."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Independent_ Methodist Conference.</head>
        <p>Independent_ Methodist Conference.</p>
        <p>Alderman James Evans, of Warrington, presided at the annual conference of the Independent Methodist Churches (opposed to incorporation) which opened at the Elizabeth-street Chapel, Liverpool, on tiaturday. Delegates were present from all parts of Lancashire and Cheshire and surrounding districts. Deputations were received from the Local Free Church Council, the Sunday School Union, and the Christian Endeavour Union.—The Rev. F. W. Nicholson, as representing the Free Church Council, stated that that body had decided to eliminate political questions from their programme, and devote themselves to spiritual work, in consequence of the varied political complexion of the membership of the churches.—Mr. Banks (Liverpool Sunday School Union) spoke of the value of the Sunday school work, and referred particularly to the work of the Union in training the. teachers.— The Conference was resumed on Tuesday, Alderman James Evans being again in the chair.—The President, proposed the adoption of the report of the General Committee, which dealt principally Ns ith the appoint- ment of a sub-committee in regard to the qu,stion of Ministers' Assistance Fund. After careful consideration the sob-committee were of opinion that it was not feasible to approach the other Connexion with a view of continuing membership of the existing funds. There- fore they recommended the establishment of a M inisters' Assistance Fund for the ministers, of the churches opposed to incorporation. So far as could be ascer- tained at present there would be about sixty ministers connected with their churches. It was recommended that the same amount of contribution be made as heretofore, namely, 6‘. per member, and that, in addition, personal subscriptions and a yearly collection in the churches</p>
        <p>Tao for the fund be suggested, the stronger cn_urehes. remembering their •obligation to the weaker ch,9 orlY If considered necessary to provide for possible,otee claims arising,the committee suggested that a gula'`..bich fund of £lOO might he organised, towards Ni'd church premises and personal guarantees sh" foe&amp; requested. It was further recommended that the should be placed to a separate account, and sh°uti.uhore • disbursed on lines and by means similar to once hitherto followed, and that a Ministers' Assisld Fund Committee be appointed, whose duty sh," to carry carry out these objects and report to eacn lug of the General Committee.—Mr. seconded the resolution, which adoPt was ont f A summary of reports from churches was Pressi,,,iog and these showed that steady earnest work w4s, —toed carried on. The tottl membership, which in )90 shoot at 1,139, was increased to 1,191, and the SundiY,',chtlY scholars, who in 1908 "ere 3,527, were also sill!, os• increased in number. The value of church propertYsig aver £31,000.—A resolution was passed esl,_,)rl for thankfulness for the goodness of Almighty many evidences of His presence during the Past ble and urging that the Churches shonld l'u—toar themselves before God that He might greater - greater power upon them to turn men fromna,, ness to light and from the power of Satan to " On the proposition of Mr. Brimelow' seconded bY, the James Dodd, a resolution was carried approving °Lies formation of a Ministers' Assistance Fund,and Prillurossi or guarantees amounting to L'66 were received tolvo:ttee guarantee fund of £lOO. The formation of a c"."til to give effect to the resolution was left over, in to-day:—ln the evening there was a public 111,3"",fte5t the church, when Alderman James Evans (Warrinb,er,' spoke on " Vhe Neglected Secret of the Church and Mr. William Brimelow (Bolton) gave AO apdreSBo:o 'Methods of Service in the Church." Toe chair call occupiod by Mr. R. Pritchard, and both speikers - banked for their-addresses, t the The conference came to a close on Tuesday,3-„niea Elizabeth-street Church, the chair being again by Alderman Evans. Reesolutions were passed esPl'e'd big thankfulness for the Children Act, urging the %tit for licensing reform, and calling upon theGoveruuldal to use its influence with the European Powers 0" the iniquities practised in the Congo. The President, in thecourse of an address, said thatbliPod to now theyhad followed a course of action whichwit)l not only commended itself to those who wereA b4ea them on the matter of the incorporation, but it ha% to a course which had given no occasion for offence- its those who differed from them (hear, hear). beZio very anxious thr.t between now and the time cr,..'A te review of the circumstances and conditions wou'oe taken, that the course they might pursue would bth which they might in days to come look back uP" -ticn pleasure and with no reason for regret in anY they had taken, nor in any attitude they had as"l""tbe The President was unanimously re-elected of proposition of Mr. W. Brimelow, seconded by Worthington, and and supported by Mr. Isterling president.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
