      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ROMANCE OF A FAMOUS PICTURE.</head>
        <p>THE ROMANCE OF A FAMOUS PICTURE.</p>
        <p>MILLET'S "ANGELUS."</p>
        <p>Just fifty years ago in a small cottage in the French village of Barbizon h peasant was. working with • loving care .upon a small picture. In the mornings he was busy upon his land cultivating potatoes and cabbages, but at the hour of his mid-day meal the soil was washed from his hands and he took up brushes to follow a more congenial task. The picture was of the simplest kind imits subject and treatment. Two French peasants such as the ainter worked with morning by morning,,a wheelbarrow and a rlbtato sack filled the foreground ; a distant spire on the horizon and that was all. But the painter worked at it with loving and .almost passionate care. This was Jean Francois Millet and the picture was the famous "Angelus." A few days ago France was stirred to the highest pitch of interest at the intimation that by the will of M. Chauchard this bit of canvas would become the property of the nation to be open to the public gaze in the Louvre. Its history is so full of interest that we propose to give it in outline to our readers, putting first a few facts of the no less interesting life of the painter. Up to the time of Millet, art had been a flatterer. It is true that Oliver Cromwell when being copied insisted that the artist should give the Warts and .wrinkles, but I there were few art-patrons like him. Millet was born to look hard facts in the face. He</p>
        <p>Photo, Bates. REV. JOHN GUNNELL..</p>
        <p>Pciazeth.</p>
        <p>Editors of newspapers are supposed to be infallible judges of readable copy. To my great surprise, the Editor of the "Examiner" seems to be under the impression that a paper from me under the above heading will have interest for his readers. Likely enough an editorial reading of the manuscript may modify this impression. How- ever, the responsibility is with that mysterious personage, and not with me.</p>
        <p>I have a strong a-version to speaking and writing about myself. Happily there is no need in this case to give much prominence to the personal element. It will suffice to say that I was born in Braintree, an ordinary market town in the county of Essex, with a population of about 5,009, and within forty miles of London: similar to hundreds of others in the land. Still it has its features of interest for those who claim it as their birthplace. Cod gives all men all earth to love ; But since man's heart is small Ordains for each one spot shall prove Belovi.d over all.</p>
        <p>So sings Rudyard Kipling. My ancestors on both sides, for several generations, were sturdy nonconformists, when nonconformity Meant legal disability, and social disgrace. Both my parents were members of one of the Congregational Churches of the town from their youth to the day of their death. There lies before me while I write a small book of memoranda made by my father touching the leading incidents in his life. Quite early in the records, I read this :—" I was admitted into the Church of Christ under the pastoral care of the Rev. ,John Carter, October 12th, 1826." He was then in rlis 19th year.. A similar entry records my mother's admission to membership of the same church, on June JAC 1827, in her 20th year. Two years after they were married. This is not the place in which to speak of their natural gifts, and Christian virtues. Still NI must claim the privilege of paying their precious weinory a passing tribute. Their Christian 7haracter was of the purest and noblest type, and their example, training, and influence such as should. have made. of me a much better man than I am. True, the discipline of the home, looked at in the light of present domestic laxity, was somewhat severe; but we children had not travelled far in life's journey before we discovered how wise and needful the restraints had been. There were eleven of us in all; and to the four still surviving no spot on earth is so clustered and crowned with,beautiful memories as the home of our childhood.</p>
        <p>Braintree, as I knew it nearly seventy years ago, possessed a fine old church of the 12th century, several mansions, and many less inetentious'buildings, dating back to the 16th and 17th centuries—the latter being unspoiled by modern restorations. I cannot honestly say that as a lad, Thad any special reverence for those old buildings. As a rule it takes many years to make of the average English boy an antiquarian. Probably the introduction of a brand new plateglass window in some old-world shop front would excite vastly more interest. Through a good part of the 18th century the town was under the government of the Four and Twenty" with their self-imposed constitution and rules, their monthly dinners, -their great pew in the parish church, and, if the whole truth must be told, the angry contentions of their wives on the burning question of precedence therein. An excellently kept minute book of the meetings of this body is preserved, which shows that according to their lights they governed the town well, and were held in great respect by their neighbours. It was a singular form of Local'Ooverni-ndnt, and by what charter, royal or otherwise, they were empowered to rule the town I have never been able to find out.</p>
        <p>The year of my birth was the year of Queen Victoria's accession to the throne- -1837; it ,also witnessed the abolition of punishment by the Pillory, and the opening of Father Matthews far-reaching Temperance Mission. It is not an easy thing, even for me,to realize that at that time no steamships , had crossed the Atlantic, no telegraph ' existed, the stage-coach and the stage- waggon were still running, the stocks, as an instrument of punishment, in frequent use, penny postage unknown, letters being charged according to distance and weight, while the all-but forgotten Chaftist Movement was at its height. I have a very vivid recollection of a huge gathering in con- nection with this movement held on a piece of waste land just outside the town, and the consternation it occasioned in the place. A hundred county police had been drafted into the town, were marched into the Corn Exchange and kept there for many hours, to be called out in case of riot. There was much passionate protest against political and social injustice; and earnest advocacy of the five points of the People's Charter as the only remedy, but nothing approaching riot. I confess that my youthful sympathies were on the side of the Chartists. It was a time pf alarming discontent for which there was ample justification. Trade was depressed, work was scarce, and poverty deep and wide- spread. The Corn Laws were in full force. There are very few advocates of Protection whose memory goes back to those hard times. Bread, of only moderate quality, was cloven- pence the four pound loaf, and for a time, I believe, stood at a shilling. A skilled mechanic who received ,E 1 a week was con- sidered well paid—many had less. A farm labourer, if single, had 95., if married 125., and a horseman who had charge of a team, 14s. How they managed to exist on such wages ' it is difficult to imagine. It is, in part, explained by the fact that the staple trade of the neighbourhood for many genera- tions had been cloth weaving—serge, woollen</p>
        <p>textures, and druggets or baize being the chief products of the looms. Much of this weaving was done by the labourers' wives and daughters in their cottages, thus adding to the family income. But with the general movement of trade northwards in the early part of the 18th century, hard times set in, and the working people were' reduced to sore distresS. When things were at their worst the . silk trade came to the rescue. Several large mills were built, landlooms gave place to machinery, and a thriving industry</p>
        <p>, employing many hundreds was established. This was the condition of things in my youth : though oven then the click of the shuttle was heard in many a town and village cottage. The town is famous for its hand-made silk embroideries. The corona- tion robes of our present King and Queen were made there; and much costly and beautiful work of a like character has been done by the same firm for foreign courts. Other tradeg have been added in more recent years, and have made of this quiet market town a little hive of industry. _ _</p>
        <p>The educational facilities- of Braintree 70 years ago were neither better nor worse than those of a hundred other towns of like size, remote from the seats of learning. For infants there was the old Dame School of which we had several, all about equally inefficient ; the Parish, or Charity School as it was called; a British School for boys, and another for girls, supported by voluntary contribution and the penny fees of the scholars. The Boys. British School was by far the best attended. The master wa:s a retired shoemaker, who would be remem- bered by. the lads more for the punishment he inflicted than for the knowledge he imparted. Not infrequently the school was in open rebellion against him and hi tyrannous discipline, merciless severity being used to suppress the outbreak, ending as it sometimes did in the expulsion of the ringleaders. In every case the teacher was untrained and incompetent. There was also an" Academy" for the sons of tradesmen and the professions, where a really good commercial education was given. This Academy and the British school were in the same road, and between them there was per- petual and deadly strife. Many a pitched battle was fought—the Britishers usually holding the field by sheer weight of num- bers. But woe betide the British stragglers who fell • into the enemies hands. Torn jackets, bleeding noses, and black eyes were the chief casualties, to be followed alas ! by home chastisements much more hard to bear. In my later boyhood, a marked improvement took place in the elementary schools. The Courtauld brothers—founders of the silk trade, rendered splendid service to the cause of education. One of them built at his own expense, a beautiful school and class rooms for boys and girls, with an infant school adjoining. Both were thoroughly equipped, the staff trained, certificated and in every way qualified. Very soon the old British schools were supplanted by modern buildings and modern methods, and, for the past 50 years few small towns in England have had more satisfactory day school pro- vision. We were far enough from "equality of opportunity" in educational matters ih my early days. But it by no means followed that the lad with the poorest opportunities was less able, or, in the end, less successful than the boy who had the best of chances. Boys of real grit, with force of character,and sound moral principles, whether trained in the " Academy " • or the humble British school made there way in the world and proved themselves good citizens. Many of these rise before me as I write. There were of course some unhappy failures—lads who made nothing out, and were a discredit to everybody that had to do with them ; but these were by no means confined to the humbler class of school. Then—as now— the two vital factors were the character of the child and the moral atmosphere and training of the home. For more than two hundred years Brain- tree and the adjacent towns and villages have been a stronghold of Nonconformity. Many of the ejected clergymen of 1662 settled in the neighbourhood and founded Free Churches, which have grown in power and usefulness from that day to this. The Five Mile Act—more cruel than the Act of Uniformity—which soon followed had a like effect. By its provisions a Nonconformist minister or *teacher was forbidden under heavy penalties to live within five miles of any town where he had preached before his ejectment. The result was that many of these brave men retired into the villages and formed congregations in remote places in cottages, faimsteads and country houses. A large proportion of the village chapels of Essex to-day—and there is hardly a consider- able village in the county without one—were built by small churches gathered together under the pastorate of these ejected ministers: the Vicar of Braintree himself being one of them. It was my privilege to conduct services in many of these chapels in my lay-preaching days. With scarcely an exception they were Congregational in their form of Church government, Calvinistic in their creed, and preachers and people fearless advocates of civil and religious liberty. The preserved records of one of these Churches in Braintree itself go back to the year 1700, when the members were meeting in a barn, with an ejected minister of the Established Church as their Pastor. The congregation grew apace, for in 1'707 the large chapel now standing was emoted. In 1716 the record shows a congregation of 800, of whom 110 had voted for the county, and that at a time when the franchise was a .£5O rental or a freehold. So that it must have been a Church of considerable social prominence. In my youth the town had two large Congregational Churches, two smaller Baptist Churches, and a Friends' Meeting House, while the :only Wesleyan Chapel within six miles of the place was a small building, a mile from the town, and the property of a brewer. Now we have a handsome building in the centre of the town.</p>
        <p>As some of the older readers of this paper will remember, it was -in Braintree the celebrated struggle was made against com- pulsory Church Rates. Formerly money was raised for the maintenance of the Church fabric by a rate levied by the parishioners at a Vestry Meeting. The struggle began in the year of my birth, when a rate was refused at a somewhat stormy meeting held in the body of the Church. Three shillings in the pound was the rate which the church- waidens sought to impose, and a resolution to that effect' was submitted to the meeting. It was defeated by a large majority. A poll Was demanded by the minority. To this the Nonconformists consented, with the proviso that the voting should be by ballot. Strong objection was taken to this mode of voting, indeed the Vicar refused to put the resolution, and vacated the chair. Another chairman was appointed and the resolution put and carried. Then followed the strangest incident. Both parties carried out the poll in their own way. The Vicar and Wardens sat at one table in the Church and recorded votes openly; the opponents of the rate at another presided over the ballot box. It says much for the sturdy character of the Nonconformists of the time that almost</p>
        <p>to a man the voters went first to the Vicar's table and voted openly and then to the other and voted by ballot. I assume that the return would be on the open voting. The figures were 70 ' for the rate, and 207 against. In spite of this the rate was levied, and determined efforts made to collect it which failed completely; Then followed a long legal battle of fifteen years. A strong Anti-Church Rate Committee was formed. The question raised by the numer- ous lawsuits was "the power of the minority in the vestry to make a rate," many decisions were given, some for, others against. The case was carried through all the law courts, and at last, after years of bitter con- tention, reached the final Court of Appeal. The judgment of the House of Lords was given on August 12th, 1853. It was this: That a rate muAt be made by a majority, and that no other rate is valid. I have a vivid recollectioniof the day when the decision reached the town ; it was a.time of great rejoicing, for the Braintree case had been the test case throughout the conflict. This settled the matter for ever. Practically, church rates were abolished from that day, but it was not until 1868 that a law was passed formally abolishing them. During the unhappy strife hardly anything was done to keep the Church in good repair, and in the end it was in a deplorable condition. Repairs and restoration speedily followed, , 'and some of the most generous contributors ' to the restoration fund were those who had most stoutly opposed the rate. It will be seen from what I have before said, that by birth and education I was a Congregationalist, or Independent, as we then called them. I have never lost sight of my indebtedness to the Congregational churches for my early _religious associations. This is ' not the place to give My reasons for leaving them, and seeking a religious home and sphere of service among the Wesleyans. This step was taken when I was 18. In the ISunday school, the ,choir, and the Mutual Improvement Society, I found many pleasant companions and much happy employment. Openings for brief exhortations in village chapels followed, which issued in my accept- ance as a local preacher, and just as I was being urged to take the usual steps to enter the ministry my father sickened and died. His removal rendered necessary a change in my plans. Business claimed my attention two years longer, then the way opened and I became a candidate for the Wesleyan ministry. I was accepted by the conference of 1862. It is a singular fact that of the five •young men who entered the ministry from that circuit in 1861 and 1862 the three still living have been stationed in Warrington—the Revs. E. 0. Coleman, Alfred Freeman and myself. For nearly 30 years, with scarcely a break occasioned by ill-health, I was endeavouring to do my duty as a Wesleyan Minister, and chiefly in the counties of York and Lancaster. In 1889 I was appointed superintendent of the War- rington Circuit and for a year or more I was in vigorous health, and had great enjoyment of my work. Then a sudden and rather serious illness laid me aside for six months. I can never forget the skill and kindness of the late Dr. S.tarkey Smith during that try- ing time. In the end my general health suffered very little, but the attack left me with hearing so much impaired that I was placed at a great disadvantage in the conduct of the many official meetings over which the Superintendent has to preside. By the kindly help of my good colleagues—the Revs. Alfred Freeman and Thomas Ross, and the ind.ulgence of the circuit, I was able to complete my term of 3 years and then sought a year's rest, in the hope that by skilful treatment my hearing might be restored, and that I might resume the full work of the . ministry elsewhere. The specialists consulted gave me 'little, if any encouragement. Before the year ended, the unwelcome fact had to be faced that I was disqualified for the service required of a Circuit Minister, in Methodist phraseology I had not " competent abilities for the work." Permission was given me to retire,. and I became a Supernumerary in 1893, con- tinuing my residence in Penketb, where the year of retirement had been spent. Here I have lived nearly 17 years rendering such service as oppoiiunity offered, and my dis- ability would allow. My keenest regret all through these years has been that the sphere of service was so restricted, and my share in the religious and philanthropic work of the district reduced to such narrow limits by my deafness. The good town of Warrington has always treated me with a kindly respect that has never failed to touch my heart; while the Methodists— my own people—of all ranks and ages throughout the circuit, and especially at Penketh, have overwhelmed me and my good wife again and again with their warm and generous regard. But my best friend and most efficient helper through all these years of disablement—the brightness of my home, the glad sharer of all my joys and , sorrows; whose active interest in Christian and Social Service has gone a long way toward supplying my manifold deficiencies; the one on -whose sympathy and co-operation I could always rely with confidence, is my good wife.</p>
        <p>- The 20 years have brought many changes, a lengthy paragraph would hardly suffice to record the bare names of those who have joined the great majority. Of the prominent officials of the circuit when I came compara- tively few are left. Happily, in many cases, instead of the fathers we have the children, and where this is not so, successors of ability and devotion have been raised up to fill the gaps. I question if the Warrington Circuit was ever better staffed ministerially, and officially than it is now. lam no pessimist. Not for a moment can I subscribe to the dismal creed that the former times were better than these. There are a few things, I sometimes think, in which we 'night imitate our fathers more closely with no small advantage to ourselves and the nation; but speaking generally in matters political, social and religious there has been steady progress these 70 years past, and the outlook td-day is full of promise. While old men are dreaming 4lrea,ms, more or less hazy and confused, the young men are seeing visions, with increasing clearness, catching their inspiration, and translating them into history.</p>
