      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>'ANGELUS."</head>
        <p>THE 'ANGELUS."</p>
        <p>toiled in the fields till his hands were horny. (" No less beautiful were they for that" he used to say). A son of the soil and a descendant of generations of , peasants, he digged the soil during his most impression- able years and to the end of his days never desired to be otherwise regarded. He was one of the wooden-shoed millions who make up France, a liver of the simple life and a frugal poverty-stricken agriculturist. 1--e was born at Gruchy, near Cherbourg in 1814 and, as generally happens to French peasant children, was cared for by his grand- mother. This tender soul used to waken him in the morning by saying "Wake up, my little Francois; the birds have long been singing to the glory of God." His father, too, was poetic and religious, and as they gathered up their tools at sunset would look at the crimson sky and say "My son, it is God." From such teachers Millet learned the duties of life and to him there was ever a halo of glory over the simple toilers of the</p>
        <p>At the age of 18, though he had never seen a picture, he sketched the figure of an old man so strikingly that his father sent him to Cherbourg to study art. From this congenial labour he was, 'however, soon recalled by the death of the elder Millet and he took his father's place at the management of the farm. Two years later by the kindness of his brothers, he was able to go to Paris to enter the studio of Delaroche, but, Millet could not bring himself to the ideals then held, and his efforts brought little satisfaction. For ten years he endeavoured to support himself by his brush. Art he felt to be his calling and he endeavoured to be true to it, but he followed the examples sot by the painters of the day, and they ill accorded with his genius. The story of those years makes sad reading. The trial of illness, and the loss of his wife weighed upon him ; he took round his draw- ings to exchange them for bread. In the Revolution,of 1848 he had to shoulder a rifle, but relinquished it as quickly as he could. Driven from Paris by the cholera in 1849 he made his home in Barbizon and began once more to live the life of a peasant. Here, after the morning's soil had been washed from his hands he painted the pictures which have made him famous for ever. Free from the influence of schools, he put on canvas the scenes of• his daily life and painted "The Sower," "The Shepherdess," " The Gleaners"' and "The Angelus." Beset with debt he *sold them for trifling sums' to his friends, little realizing that within a few years they would be regarded as priceless. What is there in the " Angelus," it may be asked, to make it famous. It is a small canvas only 25 inches by 21, and its figures are the simplest that ean be imagined. The answer is that it is a great picture because it is supremely real. The man and woman in the -picturc have toiled till the sack is filled. The bell of the distant church rings the Angelus for evening prayer and they bend in the open and unconstrained worship of the French peasantry. Labour and prayer, thus joined, form the subject,and the painter has given us a picture, peaceful and dignified, which speaks as perhaps no other does of the dignity of labour. The simple pictures of Millet were labours of love. They hung upon his easel for months, and though hard pressed by debt he would not allow any of them to' go until he had put his whole meaning into them. Pressed to sell his "Sheep fold " which had been under his care for months he said "No, you can't hear the dog bark yet." "The Gleaners" could not be sold until visitors felt sure they could see the women rise and stoop at their work. The " Angelus " was only considered complete when M. Sensier entered the studio and, without knowing the title, said that he could hear the bells. Painting with a heart full of the memories and ideals of his youth, Millet created pictures that were instinct with devotion and life. In his eyes the peasant was as great as the statesman and as deserving of honour, and the glories he saw in them he transferred to canvas. What he did had never been done before, and will never need to be done again. He was doubtless a puzzle to his neighbours, but to posterity he is a prophet. Of the history of the canvas we have only space to speak in the briefest outline. After weeks of bargaining it was sold through a</p>
        <p>purchase it at any cost. The picture was placed on the easel amid .scenes of intense excitement, and bidding quickly ran to 30,000 francs. At this point the Government representative entered the contest, along with those of two American museums. These last retired when 450,000 francs had been called, but two others from America arriving by special train from Havre took up the contest. Amid great excitement the hammer fell at 504,000 francs, and when it was known that it was the Government bid the audience shrieked itself hoarse with cries of "Vivo la France." An objection was raised, and the struggle was resumed until the Government silenced its opponents by a bid of 553,000 francs.At this the company of excit- able Frenchmen went wild. Hats were flung into the air and the women sobbed as though• relieved from a terrible suspense. From some reason which remains still unexplained the Government failed to ratify the purchase. The next bidder raised his price and after the picture had been exhibited a day or two in Paris carried off his prize to America. The Custom House told him that 1£7,000 was due to them as duty, but this he was unwilling to pay. As a compromise it I was arranged that the picture might remain six months in the United States without payment of duty. During the period allowed the owner exhibited the picture and it then re-crossed the Atlantic to France. It was then that M. Chauchard made a bid. After negotiation the art world was startled by the 'news that he had paid .£32,000, a sum never excelled for a modern picture. The French 'nation now receives the canvas as a gift and will carry it in triumph to the National Gallery in the Louvre where also rests the "Gleaners."</p>
        <p>On a bleak January day in 1875 Millet's body was carried to his resting place in the village cemetery. He had died as he had lived a poverty-touched peasant. We recognize to-day what his friends had failed to see, that by his brush he had spoken to the universal heart. Whil e men and women labour and pray they will love the simple pictures of Jean Francois Millet. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WOMAN'S TORTURE FROM PILES.</head>
        <p>WOMAN'S TORTURE FROM PILES.</p>
        <p>Hot Weather Made Her Worse.</p>
        <p>• Mrs. Florence Llewellyn, of 2, Bedford Place, Zion Rd., Clarence Rd., Bristol, related to a Bristol reporter a story • of terrible suffering from piles, happily now completely banished by Zam-Buk. " Early last .summer I began to suffer from piles," said Mrs. Llewellyn, " and the pain rapidly became SO terrible that I was soon quite ill. The hot weather brought on an almost.unbearbale itching, and I would collapse from. sheer exhaustion. I went to the infirmary three times a week, but I was not a bit better for all their kind attention and treatment. I was trying to foip,et my sufferings by reading,when I came across the account of a Zam-Buk cure in a case of severe piles. This gave me a little hope, and I got a box of the balm.</p>
        <p>" The ease and comfort Zam-Buk brought me are almost beyond expression. I found myself able to walk about and sit again, while that awful burning and itching sensation gradually passed away. I con- tinued using Zam-Buk until at last all the irritation and pain had gone, and the terrible piles were com- pletely banished."</p>
        <p>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD AGRICULTURAL</p>
        <p>SILO"-.</p>
        <p>As announced in our advertising columns, all entries for the forthcoming Show, which will be held on the Society's beautifully equipped and permanently built show ground (the finest and most np-to-date in the kingdom) at Bebington, Birkenhead, on the 13th, 11th, and 15th July, will close at 10 a.m. on Thursday next, the Ist July, or by letter bearing a previous postmark, andtunder no circumstances whatever can any entries be accepted after the above mentioned time. There will be a grand naval and military display in the large judging ring on both Wednesday and Thursday evenings,and on the last named there will also be a horse leaping competition for leaders which have never won a prize of £lO or more, confined to residents within 25 miles from the Show Yard. Hence there will be 12 hours continuous entertainment for visitors on each day. The various Railway Companies are issuing cheap tickets for each day of the show. Any intending exhibitor who has net received a prize schedule should apply at once, stating for which section same is required to the Secretary, 6, Hamilton-square, Birkenhead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>friend of the artist to Baron de Papeleu. From him it passed to the Belgian minister in. Paris, M. von Praet, who soon after exchanged it for another picture by Millet to M. Tesse. Later it was bought by M. Durand-Ruel, who seem.; to have been impressed by its beauty, for £1,250. When Millet heard of this he was amazed and regarded the price as fabulous. Other people seem to have thought so too for during the Franco-Prussian War the picture travelled to England and was on offer in Bond-street for £l,OOO. The highest bid, however, was only £BOO. Oil its return it found a buyer in Mr. J. W. Wilson, who gave £2,000 for it. It was upon his death in 1881 that a .chapter of unusual interest opened. The " Angelus " for the first time was put up for auction. M. Secretan had a desire to secure it and instructed a firm of dealers named Petit to bid £6,400. Mr. Vanderbilt also gave a similar instruction and to the same firm. It was purchased and the two bidders decided to draw lots. M. Secretan won, and at once sold the picture to .the dealers for £B,OOO. A few years after, repenting of his decision, he bought it for £12,000. Mr. Vanderbilt offered 'him £20,000, but the offer was resisted. The picture hung in Mr. Secretan's gallery until his death in 1889 and was then offered for sale by auction. -:‘.The world was now thoroughly alive to the charm of Millet's picture, and it became known that the French nation meant to</p>
      </div>
