      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>LAWN TENNIS</head>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. CROMELD'S.—Warrington Cricket ground, Saturday, June 19tb. Results : Mixed doubles :—H. Hands and Miss Rose (W) beat A. S. Percival and Miss A. Jackson (0), 6-3, 6-1, 6-0. J. Murphy and Mrs. Davenport (W) beat H. Clayton and Miss Marsh (C), 6-1, 6-0, 6-1. William Murray and Miss A. Warren (W) beat Knowles and Miss Mellor (C) 6-3, 6-1, 3-6. F. Richmond and Miss Wilson (W)' beat J. Plant And Miss Pickston (C), 6-2, 6-1, 6-1. T. C. Murphy and Miss Murphy (W) beat W. Barlow and Miss Smith (C), 6-4,6-5, 5-6. E. Hill and Miss F. Wallington (W) bent H. Sandy and Miss E. Jackson (C) 6-2, 6-2, 6-2. Gentlemen's Doubles :—Hands and J. Murphy (W) beat Percival and Knowles (C) 6-3, 6-3, 3-6. Richmond and T. C. Murphy (W) beat Barlow and Plant (C) 5-6, 6-3, 6-2. Murray and Hill (W) lost to Sandy and Lythgoe (C) 1-6, 3-6, 6-3. Lalies Doubles :—Miss Rose and Miss Wilson (W) beat Miss A. Jackson and Miss E. Jackson (C) 6-0, 6-0, 6-1. Mrs. Davenport and Miss Warren (W) beat Miss Mellor and Miss Smith (C), 6-0, 6-1, 6-0. Miss F. Wallington and Miss Murphy (W) beat Miss Marsh and Miss Pickstpn (C) 6-1, 64, 6-2. Warrington won by 30 sets to 6 sets, 200 games to 84 game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARNING TO BEE-KEEPERS.</head>
        <p>WARNING TO BEE-KEEPERS.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agricultural and Fisheries desire to ,warn all bee-keepers that an ontbreak of disease believed to be identical with the Isle of Wight bee disease has occured in several hives in Buckingham- shire. This disease which has destroyed almost all the .bees in the island from which it takes its Dame is due to a bacillus cllisely resemblihg the bacillus of plague, and no remedy for it is known. It is of the utmost importance therefore that bee-keepers should take every precaution to prevent the disease spreading, and they are strongly advised to keep a careful watch for any signs of its appearance. A full description of the disease vas published in the Journal of the Board of Agriculture for Febrmary, 1909, and bee-keepers who find symptoms of disease corresponding to the description there given should communicate with the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4, Whitehall Place, I.4ondov,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER SUCCESSFUL MEETING AT BRIDGE FOOT.</p>
        <p>Tariff Reform Fallacies Exposed.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Progressive Labour League is to be congratulated upon the splendid Success which is attending its open-air campaign. At the meeting at Bridge Foot (near Arpley Cannons) on. Mond'ay even- ing there was again a very large and enthusiastic gathering and the speeches were followed with keeii interest. Mr. E. England presided and the principal speaker was Mr. Samuel Weave, of the Free Trade Union. Amongst others present were :--Messrs. C. E. Parker, G. Wallington, J. Howes, J. Elwell, S. Wright ; J. Morton and J. Howes jun. (hon. secs).</p>
        <p>THE CHAIRMAN ON POLITICAL AND LOCAL AFFAIRS. SHOULD WARRINGTON .HAVE A DOCK?</p>
        <p>The CtrAiitAtAN, referring to the meetings of the Tariff Reform League, said that a few weeks ago the Tories had a speaker from Burnley—a Mr. Tom Smith. (Mr. Weave t They all come from Burnley.) He not only told them some very harrowing details but fairy tales. (Laughter).- They found he syss tripping, as was revealed in the Press. Their friend Mr. Parker exposed him in regard to leather. Mr. Smith made most extraordinary statements in connection with the leather • trade, about hides going to America. And yet they were to •be found at Penketh. 'Mr. Parker bought no less than 28,000 of those hides that Mr. Smith said had been taken to America. (War, hear). Take their irrepressible friend Councillor Broadhurst, who seemed to be like a bottle cork—you no sooner knocked him down than he rose again. (Laughter). When Mr. Broadhurst could not combat Mr. Elovves' statements about mining royalties, he said they were only &amp;trifle. \Vas over seven millions a year only a trifle ? the speaker asked. That was the money • derived from those royalties. Why seven millions a year would settle the unemployment question. It would more than pay thetas on sugar. It would more than take off the whole duty on tea, because it. aid not come to six millions a year,land in fact the whole of the duties on food only amounted to 14 millions a year. Therefore they saw that when Mr. Broadhurst stated that seven millions a year was only a trifle, he was very much off the mark. Let them see if they were only a trifle. He had with him a statement from Mr. Percy, a member of the Institute of Mining Engineers.' and also one of the best authorities on royalties in the country. The speaker thought that Councillor Broadhurst would agree that Mr. Percy knew more about mining royalties than he (Councillor Broadhurst). Mr. Percy said that the effect of royalties on iron and coal on the cost per ton of manufactured iron producted was as follows :—Pig iron, England Is. fid.•; France. Bd. and Germany fid. ; plates for ship-building, England, ss. 9d.; France, IS. Olds and Germany is. ; steel rails England ss. 6d.; France 11d. and Germany 81d. Continuing, the speaker said that any of the audience who knew anything at all about contracts, and the question of manufaAtire must admit that a few shillings per ton prevented tenders being accept el in this country. But, never- theless, we were able to ii‘xport from this country, in spite of the mining royalties, mora than any other country in the world. There would be far more employment for us, if it were not for those mining royalties. (Applause). Councillor Broadhurst was very fond of quoting Germany to us. It was a very singular thing that during the last few months some commercial gentlemen, who could be relied upon, had been making an inspection of Germany and they told us some very astonishing things about Germany. They compared the municipal taxes of Dusseldorf with Salford. The Mayor of Dusseldorf received £1,050 a year end extras, and their twelve aldermen, who were elected for twelve years, received from £3OO to £B5O a y'ear. Of course- they had to work all the time, but he thought they had a pretty nice job. Those salaries and the carrying On of the town had to he paid for by the people. Every wan who earned £45 a year had to pay income tax,and then there was a house tax. They had also a land tax, a dog tax, a tax on entertainments, a tax on churches, a tax on the business turnover, a tax on property sales, and an increase of value tax. In Dusseldorf the taxes amounted to £4 per head, whilst in Salford only to £.2 lie. 9d. He might say at ouce that the rates and taxes at Salford were a little higher than they were in Warrington. However, we could copy parts of the German system with considerable advantage. Proceeding, the speaker said that life had changed a great deal in Warrington during the last 20 years. The conditions of labour were better. We had 'shorter hours and more money. ((lear, bear.) Fifty-two years azo, when he was born, one million pounds would have &amp;tight all the works and undertakings in the town. To-day their profits might be estimated at £1,000,030 per year.Tvvo years ago one firm alone male a quarter of a million of money. Therefore in spite of our old system of Free Trade there were great sums of money being made, only unfortunately the labourers who assisted in getting i did not get their fair share. (He ir, hear). That; was the great difference. Free Trade bad helped us to build up our vast undertakings. If it had not been for certain landowners in Warrington, we should have had one of the finest industrial towns in the world. (Applause.) If it had not been for certain landowners we should have had Port Sunlight here. That would have meant ' 10,000 more people—(applause)—and they would have been under wonderful conditions of living. We would have had some of the mills of Preston, but for the landlord. We knew also that the London and North- Western Railway wished to have their works here, but they could not come because of the landlord. (Hear, hear.) Really that question was at the bottom of most of the mischief in this country. There was still one great hope for Warring- ton, if we would only make the best use of our geographical position. He referred to one of the finest waterways in the world—the Manchester Ship Canal— which was not more than a mile away. Goods could be brought to Warrington along the canal at one third less cost than they could be taken to Manchester.We, in War- rington, were not having the full advantage that we ought to have of our geograPhical position, because it seemed to Mtn that if we were to make a dock in War- rington it woubicertainly develop the trade of War- rington. (Applause). By making the dock it would relieve unemployment. If instead of casting our eyes on the far ends ofthe earth, and thinking so much about the foreigner who was alway supposed lobe injuring us, we would only do what we could in the district in which we lived we should solve those great problems. They could be solved by the energy and enterprise of the inhabitants. (Loud applause). ME. GLEAVE AND TARIFF REFORM FALLACIES. Mr. S. GLEAVE delivered a racy and comprehensive speech which was listened to with close attention from beginning to end. Taking up the chairman's reference to the Manchester Ship Canal, he said that now when the ratepayers of Manchester were beginning to look forward to such a volume of traffic up the canal as not only to relieve them of further rating for the under- taking but to pay them interest On the capital tbey had subscribed, the tariff reformers came along and said '` You have been rating yourselves to make it easier for goods to come in. Yon must tiow tax yourselves in order to prevent them coming in." (Laughter). Referring to the improved conditions of life in this country, he tabour tbat according to the "Board of Trade 'Labour Gazette," the average daily number of poor relieved by Boards of Guardians in 1850 in England and Wales was 56.5 in every 1,000 of the population. In 1908 it had dropped to 22. In London alone in 1850 the average number receiving poor relief was 45 per 1,000 ; in 1918 it had sunk to 21. Mr. Gleave then referred to the woollen and worsted trades and pointed out that while the Tariff Com- mission's report on those industries contained very doleful tales as to tbepresent conditions,it bad none of the customary suggestions to offer for an improvement. The reason was that they could not make a proposal in regard to one branch of that industry which would not have been stoutly resisted by other sections of the trade concerned. He would ask the tariff reformers how it was that during the last twenty years tbEiwages of men engaged in the woollen and worsted trades bad advanced by 13 per cent. and those of the women by 21 per cent. (Applause.) Mr. Gleave also dealt with our motor car industry, which tariff reformers contended required Protection against foreign competition, and quoted an extract from the " Midland Daily Telegraph "—a tariff reform newspaper—which- statAl that the slate of affairs at Coventry was not so black as bad been painted and giving the result of inquiries of the various firms which showed that extensions of the works were being made and that over-time was being worked to cope with-the increased trade. We heard a great deal of the decline of ths glass trade, but he would like to point out that during the last five years our yearly imports of glass bad decreased by £1,000,000 worth whereas our exports had increased by £250,000. He asked the tariff reformers whether it was not a fact—he said it was a fact and he was prepared to prove it—that there were more people .employed in the glass trade of this country now than ever there had been since We had bad any glass trade, that the rate of , wages paid was higher than ever it was in the history of the glass trade here, and further that the volume of wages paid was greNer than ever it was. I Tariff reformers were fond of pointing to the number I of people who emigrated from our own shores, and they told us that if we got tariff reform all that emigration, or the greater part of it, would stop. During the last ten years the population of Norway, which was a Pro- tectionist country, had increased by 12) per cent. and emigration had increased by 400 per cent.; the popula- tion of Sweden had increased by 10 per cent., and emigration by 200 per cent.; the population of Spain had increased by 3 per cent., and emigration by 200 per cent. ; the population of Italy had increased by 61 per cent. and emigration by 250 per cent. ; the population of Austrit bad increased by 7 per cent., and emigration</p>
        <p>by 800 per cent. If Proiection stopPed the tide of emigration, why did it not have that effect in the countries mentioned ? It was often pointed out that the German trade union returns of unemployment were smaller than ours, but there W.IS a perfectly good reason. The figures in Germany did not apply to the same tritee;. They left out the great bulk of the workers in the building and textile trades, and therefore the returns could not be considered an index to the state of unem- ployment in the sense that our trade union figures were.</p>
        <p>In 1907 and 1908 the trade union unemployment in New York State averaged over 30 per cent. for the year. It was not honest for the tariff reformers always to quote the German figures—which the" Board of Trade Labour Gazette" declared could not be used for that purpose-1-- and not to give the American statistics. The first reason why emigration was greater from this country than from Germany was that we had got Colonies for our people to go to and Germany had not. Not only that, but our Colonies did all they could to induce people from this country to emigrate to them and our owe authorities backed them up. If tariff reform would stop emigration from this country to the Colonies, what was going to become of the Empire which its advocates were so fond of shouting about? Did they want Canada, Australia and New Zealand to be filled with foreigners? They some- times told us we were in danger of losing the loyalty of the Colonies unless we taxed our food. What would the position he if we stopped British people from going there? Tariff Reformers said we wanted Free Trade within the Empire but he thought they would be the first te recognise that they could not get it. Why ? Because the self-governieg Colonies were standing out. As regarded 350,000,000 of the population of the Empire we had now got Free Trade, and it was only in regard to 10,000,000 or 15,000,000 that we had not. Tariff reformers proposed to take those 350,000,000 Free Traders and make them into Protectionists and to turn the 15,000,000, Protectionists into Free Traders, and thus, they said, we would get Free Trade within the Empire. (Laughter.) Germany had copied our example in as much as she had now got a wide Free Trade Empire and America furnished us with the example of the largest Free Trade area in the 'world. Sir Robert Giffen had stated that as a result of the adoption o! Free Trade by Great Britain more than one half of the total trade of the world was now done under Free Trade conditions. (Applause.) Our ship continued Mr. Gleave, had gone up by leaps and hounds, ond had reached such a point that we actually carried on foreign account from foreign port to foreign port even more merchandise than we difl for ourselves. America adopted Protection and killed her ship-building trade. Germany had copied our example by applying Free Trade conditions to her ship-building industry, and practically every leading nation had done the same. Even America had come forward and con- fessed her mistake' and she had started on the line or giving Free Trade to her ship-building. But the vested interests there were so strong that what she had given in freedom with one hand she had taken away with the other. She had imposed such conditions, qualifications and restrictions that up to the present there had been a great deal of heiit ition on the part. of the ship build- ing people in availing themselves Of the new conditions. England had always le I the way and been the wisest in everything that pertained to ffeedorns whether it was freedom of the Press, freedom of speech, the right of workingmen to combine, or Abe freedom of every man to have a voice and part in the management of the affairs of his OWI) cthtntry and locality. If foreigners had not copied our example more completely it was not because the mass of the people in those countries had not tried their utmost. Ile asked the tariff reformers to name one count ry whtre ,Protection had satisfied not the work- ingmen, not tke mass of the industries, but the Pro- tectionists themselves. ,The largest political party in Gerinany to-day was the Socialist party, and anybody who did not like tariff reform but who was going to vote for it as the only alternative to Socialism, should ponder that fact. It was no alternative to Socialism in Germany, but as a matter of fact it had increased and accentuated the distress in which Socialism would thrive. At the last general election the Socialists gave the first place on their manifesto to Free Trade, and at nine simultaneous mass meetings of unemployed held in the " show "city of GerniauSr a resplution was passed demanding the abolition or taxes on the necessaries of life. In America at the last Presidential Election coven political p irties appealed for the votes of the electorate, and everyone of them came to the conclusion that it was advisable and even necessary to put a reduction of the tariff as the first item on their programme. Why had not the mass of the people been able to get what they had agitated for? Because the vested interests which had grown up mid entrenched themselves behind the tariff walls in Protectionist countries had been too strong, and up to the present, time they had not dislodged them. Th I t was the chief reason why he (the speaker) urged his audience to resist the first step in the direction of tariff reform, because every step would mean a now vested interest. The party , identified with tariff reform recognized that vested interests were their "pals " and supporters, and the present Government had had.to deal with the vested interest - which Mr. Balfour so wantonly created in the licensing trade. Spsaking at Manchester, Mr. Bonn- Law said `"Tariff reform will not have a tendency to raish wages." It was only fair to state that he was addressing a meeting which was largely composed of employers of labour. (Laughter.) A little later on, when he went to Newcastle, addressing workingmen he said " If it did not raise wages it would be their own fault." (Laughter.) That seemed like a way of saying " Yon will have to fight for it." (Laughter.) Dealing with the question of unemploy- ment, Mr. Gleave said he remembered how when the unemployment problem Vsaa not pressing, when trade was good, the tariff reformers announced their. intention of waiting for unemployment to increase in (niter that they might use it for their own party political purposes. That made Jilin very much doubt their sincerity. (Applause.) Until recent times Protection had never been put forward as a xemedy for unemployment. It had always been recog- nized that it would cause unemployment here and there but its advocates contended that it was justified on other grounds. Ile suggested it was only because the present Government got in and started an epoch of social reform, and the attention of the people was drawn from the ends of the earth to things at home and they began to recognize the unemployment problem, that tariff reformers came along and said" Tariff Reform will cure unemployment." When the Trade Union unemployment rate readied its height it was 95 per pent. and it was going down now as everybody knew it would. That 9.5 was made up chiefly because of the large percentages of unemployment shown in cert .in trades. For instance the ship building trade had 27 per cent. and he thought that even the Protec- tionists themselves could not claim that tariff reform would do anything for them. The wildest of them never suggested thatwe suffered from foreign competi- tion in the ship building trade. The building trades showed 19 per cent. of Unemployment and the engineer- ing trade 17 per cent. Tariff reformers could not do anything for the engineering trade, because ths great part of the unemployment in that industry was due to a reduced export trade and the tariff reformers proposed, in effect, to stop the export trade altogether. They said we ought not to send so much machinery abroad to assist our rivals to compete with us. It was worthy of note that the more we had exported textile machinery the more we had gone on exporting textile goods. Mr. Gleave showed how tariff reform, far from bene- fiting the building trade, would have an injurious effect noon it, as a tax would be imposed upon almost every- thing the builder used. The speaker concluded by again advising his bearers not to pay any additional taxation until the wealthier daises contributed some- thing like their fair share. (Applause). Mr. J. HOWES in proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman and Mr. Weave, announced that on Monday evening next Mr. Havelock Wilson, M.P., would speak on "The Labour policy of the present Government." The meetings so far had been most successful and they would be continued until the time came for them to go indoors. Alluding • to the Tariff Reform League meetings, Mr. Howes remarked: "There —you get the fairy tales, and here you get the common- sense." (Laughter.) He annouoced his intention of continuing to talk upon the subjects • which vexed their opponents so much;letween now and the next election—mining royalties, ground rents and the monopoly of the good things of this world by the land- lords—because be still insisted that/tariff reform was the red herring that Was drawn across the path in order to make them forget the land question. At next week's meeting they would put a resolution which had been sent from the House of Commons by a body of earnest reformers who were insisting that the Chancellor of the Exchequer should keep to the land clauses of the Budget. (Applause). They would be ,glad to know if that the member for Warrington Was one, of those members. (Applause). I Mr. WALLIN(' TON seconded and the vote beartilyipassed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>CZAR'S visit</head>
        <p>THE CZAR'S visit</p>
        <p>Questions were invited and one asked was: "Is it consistent with the traditions of the Liberal party that the Liberal Government should take the responsibility of inviting to our shores a political despot like the Czar of Russia?" Mr.HowEs said that no-one could be more melancholy than he should be if be thought the Czar of Russia wtl,3 coming to this town to receive a public recognition from the people. The question as worded was, unfor- tunately, untrue. It was not the Government who had invited the Czar here; the Government never attempted to make such an invitation. The visit was part of the scheme—an excellent scheme in itself which had already been productive of immense good—whereby oar monarch met the other monarchs of the world. He understood the Czar was not really coming to this country, but that King Edward and be were to meet on the sea. The • Government, however, was not respon- sible. They might, A f course, have vetoed the visit, but that would hive been a very ungracious thing to do if our King desired it as part of his scheme. Britishers would show their detestation, whenever they got the chance, 'of the tyranny that was being carried on in ,Russia. The one reason be (Hr. Howes) would not like to give the Czar recognition as au acceptable visitor to our shores was because of the terrible system over which he presided. If he pleaded that ho was not responsible he (the speaker) would not take that plea as worthy of very much credence, while the misery went on which he could certainly prevent if he had the courage or the will to do so. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
