      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Czar's Visit.</head>
        <p>The Czar's Visit.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At a special meeting of the Warrington Branch of the Independent Labour Party, a resolution was passed that we strongly protest against an official reception being given to the Czar of Russia, whilst such gross mis-government exists in the Russian Empire.—Trusting you will let this appear in your next issue, I am, yours truly, J. A. HEYWOOD (Hon. Sec.) \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Walking Day Suggestion.</head>
        <p>A Walking Day Suggestion.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Seeing that the stands erected in front of the Town Hall for use during the King's visit are com- pleted 1 should like to suggest that the public may utilize them to view the procession on "walking Day' by paying a small fee. As "walking day " is the acknowledged children's day I should suggest that the whole of the proceeds be devoted to the Poor Children's Seaside Camp Fund.—Tours respectfully, WILLIAM SHAW.</p>
        <p>Eustace Street, Warrington. July 22nd, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Territorials and Patriotism.</head>
        <p>Territorials and Patriotism.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. .T. Sr, W. DUTTON'S SUMMER SALE.</p>
        <p>The enterprise of ,Ilessrs. J. Sr, W. Dutton. 16,18. and 20, Sankey-street, Warrington, is a household word, hut their summer sale, .whicti opens on Monday next, and will be continued throughout the following month, promises to establish a record in completeness and attractiveness. The firm hold two sales every year, and they do not buy any of the low quality goods that, are specially produced to look cheapest at sales, but 'name substantial reductions from,their regular stock. Ladies and gentlemen will be splendidly catered for at the forthcoming gale. For the fair, sex there is a fine variety of millinery, including stylish London and Parisian models, a large stook of the smartest of this season's blouses, a grand displaY of costumes, coats and skit ts, as well as a choice assortment of hot:Se. bold linen, dress materials, silks and neckwear. A splendid opportunity is afforded to proctire real bargains in lace curtains, carpets and rugs' &amp;c. Lines which specially appeal to gentlemen are afine range of flannel suits, suitable for business and holiday wear, from the remarkably low price of 19s. lid., and an up-to-date stock of hats, caps and collars. Those who are con- templating holidays can equip themselves with 'bags and trunks at prices to suit all purses. For full details of the sale we would refer our readers to Messrs. J. and W. Dutton's. advertisement on the front page of this issue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
