      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>Obstacle Race.-1, H. Champ; 2, N.r. Smith; 3, D Holt.</p>
        <p>Girls' Composition Ilace.-1, Mary Plumb; 2, Violet Collins. House Challenge Cups.—Football, Domville House; cricket, Doraville House; , sports, Doraville House; tug-of-war. Doraville House. During the afternoon an excellent and interestino, dun,b-bell and gymnastic squad display was given by the boys under the supervision of Instructor Leigh, of Oughtrington, Gymnasium. Hearty cheers were then given for Colonel and Mrs. Legh, the visitors, and the staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>GUILD TRIP.—On Saturday the members and friends ot the Penlteth Wesleyan Wesleyan Guild vent for a trip to Knovsley Park. Some drove and others cycled. The party entered the lark by the Eccleston entrance, arriving there about 3 o'clock, and soon, found many friends among the united guilds. Good vocal music was rfnciered by the Earlestown Spanish choir and Bewsey-road Wesleyan choir. An excellent tea was enjoyed, each guild sitting at their own tahle. Sports were also indulged in and were much enjoyed. The return home was made at 7.30, Penketh being reached about 9 o'clock when all were of the one mind—that a very pleasant afternoon had been spent. Praise is due to Mr. Murnhy, the guild secretary, who had been in charge of the arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>PADGATE WESLEY GUILD.—The annual guild picnic took place on Saturday last when about 50 members journeyed to Knowsley Park, Prescot (kindly lent for the occasion by the Earl of Derby) and took part in a garden party arranged by the Committee of the Liver- pool Wesley Guild Council. Sports. ecc., were indulged in,competitors taking part from all the different guilds, the second prize in .the.ladies egg and spoon race being awarded to Miss Emily Ball of Padgate. Tea was partaken of in the park after which walking narties were arranged to view the hall, lake and park. During the evening selections were ciien .by a select choir (which included several of the Padgate members) under the conductorship of Mr. Hipkiss of Warrington. . The weather during the early part of the afternoon was very cloudy but later on the sun shone brightly and a very enjoyable afternoon was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Lowton</head>
        <p>Lowton</p>
        <p>CHURCH MEMORIALS. , At the Liverpool Consistory Church on Wednesday the Rev. Francis Smith (vicar) and the wardens of St. Mary's, Lowton, were vranted permission to erect a stained-glass window, containing figures of David and Johnatban, in the central window of the eastern wall of the nave, in memory of James William Smart Simpson, first vicar of the parish, and of his wife. The expense is to be defrayed by voluntary Contribution.—At the same court .an application was made by the Rev. Webster Hall, rector, and wardens of St. Luke's Lowton, to introduce an oaken lectern and to erect a tablet on the south side ot the chancel as a memorial to the late Rev. Richard Smith, who was rector of the parish for thirty- three years. The cost, about .£l4O will be defrayed by volontary contributions. The faculty was decreed. The court sits again on July 28th. Daresbury.</p>
        <p>THE RIFLE CLUB.—Much interest is being taken of this new venture, and not a day has passed since it was opened without shots having been fired. At the present time there are about 113 male members, and 27 lady members, a result highly gratify- ing to the indefatigable secretary and treasurer, Mr. Shepherd-Cross.- it is intended to give a prize each month to the memt er viho makes the highest average score. For the first month this trophy was gained by Mr. John Grosvenor, who received w biscuit barrel at the hands of Mr. SLepherd-Cross. The twelve highest averages for the month were :—Messrs. J. Grosvenor, Charles Ryder. Stephen Whitlow, John Tickle, George Walworth, John Wright, R. P. Booth' C. Shepherd. Cross, F. Woods, D. Done, homas Speed, and D. Gordon, while Mrs. Shepherd-Cross and Mrs. Burder proved the best shots amongst the lady members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS SCHOOL SIVIMMI'Y G COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>The annual competition in connection with the Cowley School Sports w..s held in the swimming baths on Monday. 0: tiilvray won the school championship, with J. Appleton second. H. Lomax won the novices' race, end one length open race was non by L. V. Moxon with W. T. Hill second, and.H./P. Laycock third. TWO LIVES LOST IN PIT ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>About ten minntes past nine on Tuesday morniing.a most unfortunate accident occurred in the shaft of the Sutton Heath Collieries, St. Helens, whereby John Lincoln Jones, 44, Parr Stocks-road, and Hugh Griffiths Jones, of 9, Croppers Hill, lost their lives. It appears that the men were working in the shaft making some alterations when a plank on which they were standing tipped up, and they were shot down the mine, falling a distance of nearly sixty yards, into a considerable depth of water at the bottom of the shaft. Information was at once sent to the officials at the top, and a search was commenced for the bodies of the two men. RAILWAY FATALITY AT PEASLEY CROSS.</p>
        <p>Louie Smart, aged)fourteen, of Lunt's Heath, Fern- wortii, met with a terrible death en the railway at Peasley Cross, St. Helens, on Saturday night. The girl's brother, John Thomas Smart, aged twenty-eight, had for some time worked in St. Helens, and lociged at 14, Sutton-road, Peasley Cross. On Saturday Louie paid a visit to her brother, and they spent the evening in St. Helens, returning to the lodgings about nine o'clock at night. At ten minutes to eles en they left the house and went to Peasley Cross Station so that the girl might go by the 10.58 train to Farnworth, on the Widnes branch. They were seen .by a porter named Joseph Leather to detcend the steps from the road on to the up-platform. At this time the Widnes train was enter- ing the station on the opposite side. The porter saw the girl and her brother walking towards the end of the platform with the intention of going across the line to the train, and then he lost sight of them. Just at that time the train from Widnes to St. Herons ran into the station, suddenly appearing from under the bridge as the two were preparing to cross the line. The driver of the train saw the girl jump o)! the platform on to the line in front of his engine. It was impossible 'for him to prevent the engine striking her, and he could not pull up until two or three coaches had passed over her. When he stopped the train the driver heard someone shout, "Ease off my foot," and on examination he found the brother, John Thomas Smart, under the first coach, suffering from severe injuries. His right leg was crushed, so as to be practically amputated at the knee, and his left foot and ankle were mangled in a way that rendered them useless. Further along the train the body of the unfortunate girl ‘‘as found under the wheels having been run over at the waist and cut in two. Assistance was at once rendered to the brother, and the girl's body was removed to the mortuary. Mr. Brighouse county coroner, opened the inquest at St. Helens on Monday. Formal evidence having been given the inquiry was adjourned until Wednesday next.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that M r.Sloane, of Moore, bad written the Council re an alleged nuisance proceeding from a ditch at Moore. He (Mr. Sloane) bad since communi- cated with the County Medical Officer* of Health, and that gentleman had met the Council's Medical Officer of Health and the Inspector of. Nuisances on the spot. Dr. Adams said that he and the Inspector of Nuis- ances bad met Dr. Meredith Young, Mr. C. L.Evans and other gentlemen on the spot. They went over the ground, and Dr. Young had since written him on the matter. Dr. Young said that the ditch from which it was contended that an effluvium arose received the sewage of 28 honses. He (the doctor) thought that the complaint was well founded, and one likely to be repeated. The cleaning out of the ditch would not adequately remedy the nuisance. Apart from the question of effluvium, cattle occasionally grazed in a field 'close by and drank from the ditch. A verbal com- plaint had also been made to him by Mr. C. L. Evans, agent for Sir Richard Brooke, that further down the ditch the water was drunk by the cattle and a contanfin- sited ditch might prove detrimental to such cattle. He was of opinion that it was the duty of the Council to take steps to deal with any effinvium nuisance. The Council Would doubtlessly consider this matter in con- junction with a sewage scheme for the whole or a con- siderable Pection of the locality. Mr. Woodward said that he still adhered to the opinion that he expressed at the last meeting, that on the evidence before the committee .who visited the ditch the effluvium arose, not from the ditch of which complaint was made but from the ditch receiving the sewage from Mr. Sloane's own house. As to cattle drinking the water he agreed that it was not suitable water for any cattle to drink. After some discussion the matter. together with a letter of complaint from Mr. C. L. Evans was referred t the Moore sewerage sub-committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>VIOLENT RUNCORN BEGGARS. At Runcorn, on Monday, two young men named John Lee and John Spencer pleaded guilty to begging in Cburch-street, Runcorn, on Saturday night. The prisoners had behaved very violently when arrested bY Constables Davies add .Lidgett, and Spencer took a running kick at Lidgett's bead. At the police station Lee lay on the ground as though dead, and Super- intendent Ennion applied restoratives. When Lee was placed in the cell he commenced to bite ferociously, and the superintendent placed a stick in his mouth. After the cell door was closed he appeared quite sane. Les was sentenced to a month's and Spencer to twenty- one days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The fortnightly meeting of the above was held last Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse. when there were present :—Messrs. J. Baxter (chairman), j. Spruce (vice-chairman), C. Roylance, G. Pilling, G. Egerton Warburton, Admiral Jones-Parry, .1. Hallows, R. Collins, H. INinwdsley, P. Booth, J. H. Davies, C. E. Linaker, jun., .1. L. Jones, William Morris, S. Woodward, T. Wright, C. E. Linnker, sen. Edwin Britch, NV. Harrison, S. Horton, E. Bretberton, J. Mynekyrne, NV. Winstanley, Antwiss, clerk (Mr. G. F. Ashton) ; the deputy clerk (Mr. W. E. Hough); the Master (Mr. Rollinson) and the relieving officers (Messrs. T. W. Cooke, W. De Prez and. J. Pearson).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>COUNTY RATE.</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY RATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling asked—the-tiiiirence in the County rate compared with the corresponding period last year. Mr. Woodward replied that the call was £10,490 13s. 2d. a thousand pounds more than the corresponding half-year. The actual increase was £1,203 16s. 10d. Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen. : We sit here to administer relief, hut we don't get much relief. The Clerk observed that a great portion of the increase was through special elementary education expenses thrown upon those parishes that had built new schools or had had expensive alterations. These accounted for about £5OO of an increase in the County rate.</p>
        <p>.-„. Mr. Linaker: They will be special expenses on those townships. The Clerk : Yes. • Mr. Woodward: But they have to appear on the general rate. The Clerk: Quite so. THE WORKHOUSE. Mr. Spruce said that there were 170 inmates in the Workhouse as compared with last year, 152. • Vagrants relieved during the last fotrnight, 253. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The fortnightly meeting of the above was held on Tuesday last, at the Dutton Workhouse. Mr. Thomas Wright. presided and there were present Messrs. C. H. Linaker seer. (vice-chairman), 3, Baxter, J. Spruce. C. Roylance, G. Pilling, G. Egerton-Warburton, Admiral Jones Parry, j. Hallows, P. Collins, H. Mawdsley, P. Booth, J. H. Davies, C. E. Linaker, jun., j. L. Jones, S. Woodward, T. Wright, Edwin • Britch, W. Harrison, S. Horton, E. Bretherton, C. Horton, W. Winstanley, T. Antwiss, Clerk (Mr. G. F. Ashton), Deputy Clerk (Mr. W. E. Hough), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health). Inspector of Nuisances (Mr. James Farrington), Surveyors (Messrs. Edwin Digg le and John Swinton.)</p>
        <p>LETTER BOX AT ANTROBUS. The Clerk read a letter from the Postmaster, North- wich, stating that he was prepared to fix a letter box in the neighbourhood of Autrubus Chapel, at the Cross .roads. Mr. Swinton said it was a very suitable place for a box. It was agreed that the box be fixed at the place above mentioned. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>MOORE DITCH.</head>
        <p>A MOORE DITCH.</p>
        <p>DR. ADAMS' SALARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. WoodwardTin—res-pe—er7t-O--an- increase in Dr. Adams' salary, said that the Clerk had been instrrcted to write and see what the salaries of gentlemen in Dr. Adams' position were and also enquire as to the duties.</p>
        <p>HEALTH. Dr. Adams, in presenting his report, said that there were only a few cases of infectious diseases, which were of the nature of scarlet fever.</p>
        <p>ILLNESS OF MR. TURNER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows moved tiraia letter be sent to Mr. Turner sympathising with him in his serious illness. This was agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>FOOTI3ALL CLUB AND TRAINING.</p>
        <p>The managers of the Earlestown Football Club have recently out of six applications for the post of trainer, elected Mr. John Powell, who .has previously done duty in the same capacity, It is intended thst a more stringent mode of preparation be adopted, taking two nights per week.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN RECOGNITION SERVICE. EARLESTOVvN WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A very brieht and impressive meeting was held in the Earlestown Wesleyan Church last Thursday evening,for the purpose of publicly recognising new members added to the Church during the past year. rbis recognition service is now an annual one, and the meeting was fairly well attended. Rev. S. C. Hall expressed great pleasure that he was able to present so many young people, who, he said,had given themselves rip voluntarily to service in the Church. Ile earnestly urged them to seek after truth, and be faithful to their God and Church. At the end of a three years ministry it was well to look back, and in doing so now be could say he had never been associated with any church in which there had been so much change, by the rerboval of a number of influential members; but. it was a matter of great thankfulness th,rt there were other men willing to take their places. It was strange to notice, however, how few had come amongst them, considering the large number who -bad left.' Mr. Hall spoke in a hopeful spirit of the future, and stated that he had every confidence in the members welcomed now. The sacrament of the Lord's supper was administered at the close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>• EDUCATION COMMITTEE. Examinii-w7Results.</head>
        <p>• EDUCATION COMMITTEE. Examinii-w7Results.</p>
        <p>INCORPORATED PHONOGRAPHIC SOCIETY. 320 words per minute: Pass, Eric Barnett and Elsie Norbury ; 90 words per minute : Pass, .Beatrice Dale, William T. Eccles and Florence Townley: 80 words per minute: Pass, Percy Clarke and Frank Hobin.</p>
