      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>CERTIFICATE EXAMINATION.</head>
        <p>CERTIFICATE EXAMINATION.</p>
        <p>The following candidates have been si c2essfril in passing the Preliminary Examination for the Certificate. 1909:— Thomas Ball Wilfred M. Barton Geography. James Brown • Bernard J. Kelly Thomas Stirrup Geography. Oswald White Margaret J. Amos Ada M. Baker Minnie Dilliway • • Ada Egerton 13ess1e P. England Hilda Johnson Dorothy M. Johnson English. History, Mathe- matics, Geop raptly, Science and F•ench.</p>
        <p>Vera C. O'Brien Annie M. Roberts Elsie N. Swaine Fnglish and Geogriphy. Anni eUpson .• English and French. Florrie Moores Winifred A. Brock . Ada J. West</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>SENIOR SCHOLARSHIPS.</head>
        <p>SENIOR SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>The following is a list, arranged in order of merit, of the candidates who have passed the examination for</p>
        <p>Sir,—Alderman Bennett's appeal in your last. issue asking for more " Territorials ' is of interest to me. It directly affects me, because I am of that age and class fel- which there is a demand to join. The appeal points out that it is the duty of all eligible to join and save the town from "the shadow of deep. and permanent disgrace." Being a patriot and of a sensitive nature I at first felt a degree of guilt attached to me,. feeling , ashamed for my delinquency, if any. My cogitation on the subject brought me to the horns of dilemma. lam asked to join a body of, men who are trained to repel an attacking force or vice versa: I do not like the idea of taking part in buch work, the, work of learning how to massacre my fellow beings. I do pot like the idea of making myself a unit of a human =Chine which is expected to work with infallible precision, and I do not like the domineering, coercive actions of the officers. Stripped of its seductive picturesque- ness, it is -a horrible machine used for the carnage of flesh and blood and tome it is repulsive.. But Ipm shirking a duty. A duty to what or whom? The duty Alderman Bennett would .have us to recognise is defence of country and the" good old town,". which is the work of the Territorial Army, and I suppose the implication is that the individual's relationship to the • country necessitates him taking some share in its defence. There we have the crux of die whole question, but; to tell the individual he has certain responsibilities to the State necessarily implies that the State has certain, responsibilities to the individual. Duty savours of something which is reciprocal, and what does " our country ' and the "good old town "do in return for the demand Made upon the working class to whom the appeal is directed. For instance, when we say our country does that mean that the people own. England? Personally the extent of the land which I shall ever own will be ift. x 21t. in the cemetery. England does not belong to the English hut a few of them. It is estimated that ten-elevenths of the British Isles is owned by 176,520 persons—please -emphasise—the population of Britain being 43 million people. Perhaps some of our so-called patriots will remember these figures. So to ask me to defend something which someone else possesses seems to me absurd. The following anecdote, which is true, sums up the situation. A man asks a friend to join the National Service League., • "Why should I join the N.5.L.," was the inquiry. "To defend your country," replies' the other. The man undergoing cross-examiration had previously been tramping the country looking for work and was then in lodging.' "To defend my country" was the astonished exclamation. " Why "he continued, "All I possess is an old clothes box and I would'nt risk my life defending it." The logic of these remarks is emphasized when we see that during the year 1906-7 some 81,121 persons died leaving estates valued at £299,330,000. • Of this huge fortune £218.252,000 was left by 4,662 persons, while the amassed wealth of the other 77,949' only totalled £81,081,000. Every year about 700,000 die and out of this total- 020,000' leave so little behind them that it relieves them of the ardous labour of arranging a will. if I were to die now my possessions are so, little that they would relieve my necessity for making out a Will. So 'Alderman Bennett's plea on this score. exonerates me from defending property. The country and town concedes 'so little to the Worker that it seems to me he has no &amp;Hy to perform in return. The parson and politician are so busy lecturing him about his duties that they have become oblivious to the duties owing to the worker. And when he does demand the duty of others towards him, in the form of an eight-hour day, the right to work and, better wages, these patriots 'assume an astonished air at the worker's effrontery and talk about 'capital leaving the country and the doing of other " patriotic "'actions. Patriotism does not end with shouldering a rifle or flag wagging. The true patriot is the man or woman whose energies are directed to elevating society. The true patriot would not tolerate slums, alow _standard of living, long hours of labour, adulterated food or anything else which lowered the people's physique. Degeneracy in the people means the deterioration of the Empire. After repeated • reductions in the standard of physical efficiency we find that in the year 1907 oat of 34.808 men offering to enlist 10,297 were rejeeted, and in. Manchester the percentage of rejection went as 'high as 72 per cent, The facts prove that the people, especially the workingelass are being neglected, myself amongst the number. So these are some of the reasobs why I refuse to join the Territorial Army. T may be called unpatriotic,and may seem so from some people's point of vie*; but my patriotism is of a different kind from theirs. My .patriotism seeks to elevate the class to .which.' I belong and by their elevation the whole of Society will be the better.—l am, yours for humanity, LABOR OMNIA</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>No need to boil the clothes</head>
        <p>No need to boil the clothes</p>
        <p>Some women can't imagine that clothes can be made pure without boiling. So they wash them the Fels-Naptha way, and boil them afterwards.</p>
        <p>There's no reason why they shouldn't, if it helps their peace of mind. But there is absolutely no need for it.</p>
        <p>If you want to boil the clothes, by all means boil them. But don't do it when you are using Fels-Naptha soap. Keep the two separate.</p>
        <p>Try it next washing day, the simple Fels- Naptha way. Money back if it isn't the better way. So there! Silks</p>
        <p>Use warm or cold water; not hot. Soak and rinse; don't rub or wring. Iron. when quite dry under a damp cloth. That's all !</p>
        <p>Fels - Naptha</p>
        <p>The soap with a Way of its own. 8.11.13.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>INSTANT RELIEF, RAPID CURE of GOUT AND RHEUMATISM by EADE'S PILLS.</p>
        <p>APPROVED BY DOCTORS ! ! Durham Villa, Park-road, Hythe, Kent, June 12th, 1908. Dear Sir,—For the paßt years I have been troubled with Gout in my right big toe joint, and suffered great pain until I was told of your Gout Pills by a friend of mine, and determined to try them. But I cannot allow this opportunity to pass without writing you to express my admiration at their quick and efficient treatment, and I SHALL NEVER BE WITHOUT THEM.</p>
        <p>lam a dispenser of 31 years' standing and in all my dispensing these many years I have/ailed to find anything do me good, so quickly. The senior , partner of my firm of doctors, I may say,approves of them. You are at liberty (fully) to make any use you like of this letter. Yours faithfully, EDWARD D. CULL, Dispenser. EADE'S GOUT PILLS Are perfectly safe in their action, eliminate all injurious matter, and are highly restorative. Sold everywhere in bottle is. lid. and 2s. 9d. or sent post tree for stamps or P.O.by the proprietor, GEORGE EADE, 232, Goswell road London.</p>
        <p>EUMATIC PILLS.</p>
