      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>PEOPLE'S BUDGET. Premier Refutes Lord Rosebery's</head>
        <p>THE PEOPLE'S BUDGET. Premier Refutes Lord Rosebery's</p>
        <p>Epithet.</p>
        <p>The unanimity, enthusiasm, and determination of tbe Liberal Government in the cause of land reform and social progress found full expression on Thursday when a large assembly at the Holborn Restaurant listened to the speeches of the Prime Minister and the Chancellor of the Exchequer on these particular aspects of the Finance Bill. Although not the actual beginning of the great campaign in favour of the people's Budget, which will soon be in full swing, the meeting showed that all sections of the Progressive party are united in support- ing the proposals of the Government. Both Ministers were the guests of the Land and Housing Reform Joint Committee at a special luncheon, and the speeches were a/convincing reply to the City's protest meeting of the previous day and to Lord Rose- bery's recent alarmist note. They recalled also the shrill clarion call for land reform sounded two years ago by the late Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman. Sir John Brunner. M.P., presided over a large company, 'which included the following members of the Govern- ment :—Mr. Lloyd-George, Sir Samuel Evans, Lord Pentland, Mr. Runciman, Mr. A. Ure, Mr. McKinnon Wood, Mr. A. Dewar, Dr. Macnamara, and Mr. C. F. G. Masterman. A large number of M.P.'s, including the Member for Warrington, were also present. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>MR. ASQUITH'S SPEECH. Lord Rosebery Convicted of Being a Land</head>
        <p>MR. ASQUITH'S SPEECH. Lord Rosebery Convicted of Being a Land</p>
        <p>Tax Advocate.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith, who wasloudly cheered, opened with references to the Small Holdings Act, and the Housing and Town Planning Bill. tinder the former he said, if we add together the acreage of land voluntarily acquired and to be acquired by compulsion we get a total of nearly 42,000 acres of which practically half has been acquired in the present year. Forty-two thousand acres. The total amount acquired by all the County Council's in fifteen years under the Small Holdings Act of 1892 was under 900 acres. (Cheers). A very good contrast, gentlemen—(cheers)—not between platform and plat- torm, but between Liberal and Tory land legislation. (Hear, hear.). LORD ROSEBERY'S "REVOLUTION. I pass now to say a few Words in regard to the land proposals which are contained in the Budget. (Cheers), We all saw and read with interest a letter from a statesman to whom all owe a great debt of obligation— Lord Rosebery—in which he describes the present Budget as of a revolutionary character. (Laughter). Gentlemen, what does that epithet mean? A revolu- tion, remember, is something more than an innovation. A revolution, if we are to attach anything like the exact meaning to the terms we employ, is only fit to describe a change which in principle has never been approved by or even adequately discussed before the people, and which is being carried into effect, if not by the actual violation of the Constitution, at least by some process which transcends and sets at defiance its normal operations. What part of the Budget, I want to know, corresponds to that definition? Nine-tenths of the Budget—l am not sure I am not understating it—nine-tenths of the Budget, regarded as a revenue-producing instrument, consists simply in readjustments and increases of old /miler taxes. There is nothing revolutionary in that; and when Lord Rosebery describes the Budget as revolutionary I think we may construe the term in the light of the 'flamboyant embroideries of language with which it was described in the City yesterday. (Laugh- ter). I presume be refers' and refers only, to the land proposals it contained. Gentlemen, are they revolu- tionary? remember very well some fifteen years ago going in the company of any noble friend Lord Rosebery to a meeting in St. James' Hall—(cheers)—summoned, I think, in the interest of the Progressive party of the County Council of London, at which we both made speeches, and I have been refreshing my memory as to what was said upon that occasion. 1 find that my noble friend said, amongst other things, in a very fine and memorable speech : " The London County Council has made some great Parliamentary exertions, and it has laid down some principles which will not be allowed to die until they have been carried into effect. I will take only two or three, but they are familiar. The firrt is the taxation of ground values." (Cheers). He goes on to say that it is not a very easy principle to carry into effect, but be bad very little doubt that with the keen brains they had now amongst them they would very soon arrive at a satisfactory result. (Laughter and cheers). Gentlemen, that was fifteen years ago. But this revolutionary proposal has bad a considerable Parlia- mentary history since that date. In the last Parliament it formed the subject of legislative proposals during no fewer than four successive sessions,land was carried In the last by a majority of 90. These measures may have been right, or they may have been wrong; they may or may not have embodied the principles I declared some time ago; but the main principle has been before the public for more than fifty years, and in the late Tory House of Commons, after being rejected, was ultimately carried by large and growing majorities. (Cheers). To speak of that as revolutionary, as a proposal that has never been sub- mitted to the public, is altogether an abuse of our ancient vocabulary. NOT TAXES ON LAND. What are these land taxes? Let me, in the first place, say, that though, by a convenient and popular use of language, they are called land taxes, that is not a correct description. They are not taxes upon land in -the sense in which, for instance the tax upon tea, or the tax upon spirits is a tax upon that commodity. These land tax proposals by my learned friend are a dole levied by the community only on the added values which accrue to land, or, rather, to the owner of land, not from his own effort, enterprise, or expenditure, but from social causes for which the community, and the community alone, is responsible. They are not, there- fore, taxes upon land • they are taxes upon the com- munal value which has been added to land by the exertions of the State. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>Swimming Warrington are to play Toxteth at water tool° on liionday and Everton on Wednesday away. The teams are to be selected from Cie following:—C. Woods, S. lace, R. Pepper, R. Beaman, C. Turner, T.Pembertonf W. Dunn and H. Wolstencroft.—The following entries have been received for the Works Squadron Competi- tion :—Co-operative Stores (holders), Rylands Bros., Pearson and Knowles, P. Walker and Son, Ltd., Warrington Corporation, Post Office, Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd., and Crosfield's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD VACANCY -- Mr. H. Roberts as an Independen</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD VACANCY -- Mr. H. Roberts as an Independen</p>
        <p>Candidate.</p>
        <p>The only candidate for the Whitecross Ward vacancy is, so far, Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P., who has decided to seek the suffrages of the electors as an inde- pendent candidate..., ..</p>
        <p>- Our readers will recollect that the Whitecross Ward Committee last week considered the vacanCy and decided to approach certain gentlemen with a view to obtaining a canaidate, and Mr. Roberts Was among the number.</p>
        <p>A further meeting of the Ward Committee was held on Wednesday in Thewlis-street Schools, to receive the report of the deputation. There was a good attendance, the chair being occupied by Alder- man James Evans. Mr. Roberts attended the meeting and gave an explanation of his position in view of once more seeking municipal honours. He expressed himself very strongly against the association of politics with municipal elections, and claimed absolute freedom and independence for himself if he once more entered the Council. Alluding to his connection with the Warrington Slate Company, Mr Roberts said that he had some time since disposed of his. ordinary shares in the company anti now had only a nominal interest in the concern. He had no paid position under the company at all. In conclusion he said he was willing to stand as an independent candidate if the committee would give him their support._</p>
        <p>The Committee unanimously passed a resolution supporting Mr. Roberts as an independent candidate. The meeting was then adjourned to Monday even- ing next, in order that the situation might be con- sidered 'after the nominations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>DATE OF ELECTION.</head>
        <p>DATE OF ELECTION.</p>
        <p>The date of the election has been fixed for Monday, July sth—the day preceding the Royal visit. Five o'clock on Monday afternoon next is the latest hour at which nominations can be handed in at the Town Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>MR. ROBERTS'S NOMINATIONS.</head>
        <p>MR. ROBERTS'S NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Roberts, soap and chemical manufacturer, Kingsley, Hill Cliffe, is proposed by James Evans, seconded by Thomas Barton, and supported by Walter Unsworth, Samuel Faulkner, Francis Charles Dixon, John Edward Edwards, James Ash worth, David Beswick, Thomas Brough, Samuel Newell. In another paper Mr. Roberts is proposed by Edwin Ambrose Jones, seconded by Thomas Dale, and supported by Robert aWirliam Dutton Cook, Richard Gorst, John Apple- ton Edward George Williams, James Evans, jun., John Barker, James Nock, John Elliss Jones. In a third paper Mr. Roberts's nominators are Aquila Cook and John Longsbaw Hawthorn, and the assenting burgesses are Frederick Henry Todd, Henry Tranter, Alfred Worrell, Joseph Jeavons, Charles Heywood Holt, Samuel Hazlehurst Hankinson, Thomas Shipley, Alfred Rogers. Mr. Roberts is also proposed by Joseph Hawthorn seconded by John Henry Edwards and supported by William Littlefair, John Joseph Ashworth, Michael Farrington, Sydney Parker, William Hill, Arthur Allcock, William George Robinson, Albert , Edward Lowe; proposed by James Corradine, seconded by Frederick Hardy and supported by Richard Highley, John Atherton. James Gorst, William pitErton, James Rigby, John Rossiter. Alfred Ernest Austin, John Toft ; proposed by the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingwortl3 seconded by Joseph Owen and supported by Edwin Elijah Pester, Richard Bertram Harrison, Joseph Bailey, Samuel Bowyer, William Houghton, William Pearson James, John Bowers' Henry Woolley; proposed by Walter Brough seconded by Wm. Albert Seymour Clatworthy, and supported by Peter Lawton jun, Thos. Weave, Edward Kelly,Peter Traynor, Wm. Woodcock, John Henry Well,Albert Edward Dean, Ernest Ball; proposed by Alfred Long, seconded by Isaac Owen Roberts, and supported by Andrew Fleming, Henry Jones, Fergus Coyne, Peter Howard, Samuel Clayton, David Clayton, John Corkin, David Walker, proposed by John Henry Knowles, seconded by Wm. Wright, supported by Harry Woolley, Samuel Alfred Leech, John Jones, James Knowles, John Randles, George Leech, Daniel Gregory Shaw, Thomas Morrell ; proposed by ,Linnamts Greening, seconded by William Webster, and supported by Edward Gilbert, ,David Percival, Thomas Howard Monks, Albert Mansell, William Stringer', James Stowell, Abel Webster, Thomas Pownall ' • proposed by Martha Whitfield, seconded by Mary Ashton, and supported by Ann Back-, house, Rosannah Cope, Emma Newell, Harriet Hewitt Dutton, Maria Hewitt Dutton, Agnes Lee, Clara Goy Hall, Elizabeth Williams. MR. ROBERTS'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Roberts's address will be found in our advertising columns. He promises if elected to do his utmost to secure for the town economical and efficient management in each department while at the same time he will support such a policy ns will offer every reasonable inducement to manu- facturers to extend their operations and so pro- vide remunerative nod additional employment for our rapidly increasing population. He lays great stress upon tbe question of the public health, pointing out that the last report of the Medical Officer of Health discloses such a condition of affairs in regard to the general health of the town, and especially as to the excessively high infantile death-rate, as calls for prompt and effective treatment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>STRIKE SCENES.</head>
        <p>STRIKE SCENES.</p>
        <p>Another Warrant Issued.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court yesterday, before the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) and other magistrates, James Seto!), 13, Garner-street, was summoned tor assaulting Henry Harding, 47, West-street, on Saturday night. Defendant did not appear. Mr. Arthur Browne said the case arose out of the unfortunate strjke at Messrs. Whyman's Foundry, and the complainant was a labourer at Messrs. Whyman's works. On Saturday night he was with his brother and brother-in-law, when the defendant came up and told him he ought not to be working. After Harding answered that he had to work, the defendant, it was alleged, struck him on the nose with his fist and also on the left jaw. A crowd assembled, and as at the end of Tanners, s-lane the defendant was waiting the complain- ant had to seek police protection, Beton then bolted, and had since left the town. He now applied for a warrant, with an intimation that defendant would not be sent for if he kept out of the town. A warrant was issued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>Impressive scenes were witnessed at the funeral which took place at St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, on Tuesday afternoon, with full military honours. Notwithstanding the heavy rain, hundreds of people assembled along the line of route from the</p>
