      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 11)</p>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>PRISON GATE MissioN.—A meeting was held in the Church House, Padgate, on Thursday afternoon on behalf ofthe . Police Court and Prison Gate Mission and Juvenile Temper- ance work. It was intended that the meeting should be in the garden, but owing to the inclement weather the promoters were forced to have it indoors. Alderman Evans spoke of the help that the Police Conrt Missson had been to the magistrates and said that it enabled them sometimes to give defendants another chance instead of putting them into prison. The Rev. W. P. Phessey (diocesan secretary) and the Rev. R. Postance (chairman of the Diocesan Church of England Temperance Society) also spoke. Mr. J. J. Firth (Warrington Police Court Missionary) gave some of his experiences. The Rev. G. Vale Owen was also present. Tea was provided at the Vicarage by the Rev. G. W. Dickenson, who presided at the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>WALKING DAY.—Owing to the persistent rain throughout the greater part of yesterday, the usual festivities attending upon Stockton Heath Walking day had to be postponed until Thursday next. However, yesterday WO day and Sunday school scholars of St. Thomas' assembled in the Church at 2 o'clock, where service was held, and afterwards they adjourned to the school where they partook of tea. The tables were presided over by the teachers and the district visitors. Amongst those present were the Vicar (Rev. James Collins), Rev. H. A. Lester, Messrs. Fred Rose, Sberratt, Mclntyre and A. Moss. On Thursday the children will assemble at 4.30 in the afternoon and join in a procession, afterwards repairing to a field at Wilderspool, where the sports will take place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Grappenhall.</head>
        <p>Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>JUMBLE SALE.—A very successful jumble sale was held in the large room at Grappenhall on Thursday. The proceeds which were in aid of the Parish Church Organ Fund amounted to £l6 10s. Od. Mrs. Lister- Kaye and Mrs. Aylme Frost, Of" Grappenhall Hall," were responsible for the oreanization of the sale. They were very ably assisted by Mesdames Pigot, R. Wilde, Kerfoot, W. A. Wood, Hutchings and others. The pro- ceedings were considered very satisfactory indeed. SUDDEN DEATH.—The death occurred' with painful suddenness at Messrs. Long's Tannery on Monday of Mr. George Johnson, an old and respected employee. Deceased, who was 57 years of age and resided in Cliff. lane, was following his usual employment, when the foreman saw him swoon and fall. He was just in time to save him from falling into a pit, but the prompt assistance which he rendered was of no avail,death being almost instantaneous. Deceased had been in the firm's service upwards of 30 years.—An inquest was held in the large room at Grappenhall by Mr. H. C. Yates (coroner) when a verdict of "Death from natural causes" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>WARRINGTON HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>Where to go and Where to Stay.</p>
        <p>With the near approach of the Warrington holidays many of our readers will be considering the un- important question—where to go and where to stay. A perusal of the advertisement columns of the " Examiner " should assist them in coming to a decision, for under the now familiar heading of ." Coast and Country—Where to Stay" we publish by far the largest and most representative list of hotels, board- ing establishments and apartments, to be found in any paper in the district. Accommodation is offered in all parts of the country, giving the holidky-maker a practically unrivalled selection.</p>
        <p>- The LOndon and North-Western Railway Company announce that on July Ist, 2nd and 3rd cheap excursions for various periods will be run from Warrington (Bank Quay) to Blackpool, North Wales Coast, Lancaster, Morecambe, Windermere, Penrith, Patterdale,. Keswick, Manchester, Liverpool, and New Brighton and Douglas, 'via Liverpool. There will be a special through train to Blackpool on Walking Day.</p>
        <p>Neglected Baby At the Warrington Police Court, yesterday. Emma Jane Handlon, Back Everton-street, was charged with neglecting her baby in a manner likely to cause unnecessary suffering. Mr. Arthur Browne prosecuted on behalf of the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, and said that the case arose out. of an inquest held on the. child, when the jury returned a verdict that de2th was due to congestion of the liver and mal-nutrition, and expressed the opinion that it was a case where defendant should be brought before the magistrates. The child, when born, was quite healthy, but when the society's inspector visited the house on June 2nd, he found it in an emaciated con- dition. It then appeared to be starved. The woman told him she was feeding the child on bread and milk, but such food would not assimilate on the child's stomach. It was practically deprived of food, and it was alleged that the woman was often found drunk, and as a result the home and child were neglected. A fine of 20s. and costs were imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>GOLF' CLUB ROBBED.</head>
        <p>GOLF' CLUB ROBBED.</p>
        <p>Burglars, on Wednesday night, effected an entrance to the Hanger Hill Golf Club and stole a large number of trophies, including two which had been presented to the club by Mrs. Leopold de Rothschild and the Marchioness of Cranby.</p>
        <p>Mr. Justice Lawrence, as president c r the Railway and Canal Commission on Wednesday, gave judgment in favour of Messrs. Brunner, Mond and Co., on applica- tion to have certain railway rebates continued, the railway companies concerned having some time ago given notice ,of the discontinuance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>DEATH OF DR, COLLINS.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF DR, COLLINS.</p>
        <p>Deep and widespread is the regret occasioned by the death of Dr. W. J. Collins, who passed away at his residence, Wilderspool Causeway, Warrington, at four o'clock 6n Saturday morning. Although the doctor had only been connected with the town for a comparatively short period,his invariable kindnegs and geniality had gained for him the esteem of a large circle of friends, by. whom he will be sorely missed. A short time ago Dr. Collins was called out to attend a patient in the early hours of theonorning, aud he contracted a chill which developed into pneumonia and pleurisy. His condition soon became exceedingly critical, and he received the unremitting attention of Dr. Robinson, of Latchford, Dr. Reginald Smith and Sir James Barr, of Liverpool, but all efforts were unavailing. Dr. Collins, who was only thirty-nine years of age, was a son of Colonel Collins, of County Cork, and he was born at Curmgh Camp, Ireland. For practically the whole of his life he was identified with the army, and he served in the Zulu and South African wars as an army surgeon. During the latter campaign he sustained a severe bullet wound in the abdomen. He was placed in hospital and the surgeons contemplated an operation, but Sir Frederick Treves, whose attention was called to the case, disapproved of that course. The operation did not take place, and Dr. Collins slowly became convalescent. His labours in the South African war were characterised by devo- tion, sell-sacrifice and courage. On returning to England he came to Warrington and soon established a successful practice and gained a warm place in the hearts of the townspeople. He was a surgeon-lieutenant in the 4th Battalion (Prince of Wales's Volunteers) South Landashire Regiment and a fitting tribute was paid to his memory by the members of the battalion at the funeral. He was deeply interested in the work of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade and as one of the hon. surgeons frequently 'gave lectures at the various classes. Dr. Collins was the W.ll. of the Birkenhead Lodge of Freemasons and his brethren heard with great sorrow of his death. He was a Clfurchmau and worshipped at St. James' Church, Latchford. The deepest sympathy is felt with the widow in her sad bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>TARY FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>TARY FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>deceased gentleman's residence in Wilderspool Cause- way to the church, and also in the churchyard and the The blinds of most of the houses in the . neighbourhood were drawn. The cortege was headed by the band of the 4th Battalion (P.W.V.) S. Lancs. Regt., under the leader- ship of Bandmaster Richardson, playing the solemn and moving strains of the Dead March in "Saul." A detachment of Territorials, including a firing party under the command of Dr. Reginald Smith, followed. The service in the church was of the simplest char- acter, there being no music, and it was impressively conducted by the Revs. James Collins, H. A. Lester, H. B. Firth, and E. M. Swift. The coffin, which was covered with the Union Jack, and bore a wreath from the widow, and Dr. Collins's military cap, sword and belt, was carried to the grave by a detachment from the 4th Battalion (P.W.V.) S. Lancs. Regt. The last rites of the Church having been performed by the Rev. H. B. Firth, the firing party fired .three volleys and the bugles sounded" The Last Post." The Masonic burial service was then read by the Rev. H. A. Lester. The chief mourners present were Mr. Frank Collins (brother), Mr. A. Linnell (father-in-law). Mr. Thomas ' Linnell and Dr. Clarence Robinson (brothers-in-law), Mr. It Stretch, Mr. Lowe and Dr. Baker. The officers present were Lieut.-Col. J. D. Fairclough, Major Appleton, Captains Dr. Reginald Smith, Gordon Roberts, Williamson, Owen, P. N. Avery, Picton (A. t.C.), Lieutenants Fairclougb, Max Woods, McClure and Monks, and Captain and Adjutant Mott. The following medical men were present :—Doctors Langdale, Murray, Peacocke, Bowden, Jan-o, Hibbert, Binns, Hutt, Bennett, Willett, Parker and gowers. The Warrington Medico Ethical Society was repre- sented by Dr. Langdale (president) and Dr. Smith (secretary), and the British Medical Association (War- rington Division) by Dr. Murray (secretary): The Masonic representatives in attendance were : Bros. John Lee, P.M., 2826, P.J.G.W.: Alexander Rutherford, P.M., 2826, P.P.G.W. ; Owen Jones, P.M., 2826, P.P.G. Treas.; E. R. Lathom, P.NI., 2826, P.P.G. Treas.; Joseph Clarke, P.M., 2926, P.P.G.D.; W. B. Roberts. P.M., 2826, P.P.G.D. ; Alexander Saunders, P.M., 477, P.P.G.S.B. ' • David A. Legg, P.M., 2826; Sidney Croft, P.M., 2826; Geo. Hulse, P.M.' Sec., 2926; H. F. Bull, 1.P.M., 2826; Mark Baxter, SM., 2826; William Railton, organist, 2826' W. Makinson, S., 2826 and 2963 ; and A. C. Perry, S. 2826 ; Colour Set geant F. , Taylor, Equity Lodge, (1384), Widnes. • The Independent Order of Rechabites (" Sunbeam" • Tent), of which deceased was medical officer, were represented by Messrs. Joseph Whitfield, George Whit- field, T. Fleming, T. Nelson, W. Ridgway, and J. Moss. The Hearts of Oak Medical Aid Society was repre- sented by Bros. A. C. Binks (president), Hatton, Coningsby and Jowett. The representatives of St. John Ambulance Brigade , were Superintendent Baldry, Sergeant Fell, Sergeant Williamson, and Privates Kirby, Spann and Broorahall. Amongst others present were :—The Rev.Jobn Yonge, Councillors Rogers, Singleton and Steel, Messrs. T. J. Lee, H. Ashton, W. Mclntyre, E. Lockett, W. Strange, L J. Strange. J. Llewellyn, P. Maginn, Cooper, A. J. Willett., F. Warburton, Forster and Rogerson. ' Floral tributes were sent by the following :—The 1 widow, Mr. and Mrs. Linnell, Mr.T.Linuell, Dr.and Mrs. % Robinson, Master Jackie and Dorothy Robinson and - nurse' Mr. and Mrs. Rutter, Colonel Reid Walker,- Mrs. Forshaw, Dr. and Mrs. Bennett (Padgate), Freddie 'L Browne, Mr. and Mrs. H. J. Stretch (Little Sutton), Rev. H. B. Firth, Mr. and Mrs. William Strange," Jack and Baby Sis," Mr. and Mrs. John Strange, Mrs; Thornton, Mrs. Graham (Margate), Mr. and Mrs. J. B. Starkie, Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Pretty, Mr. and Mrs. Heesom, children at Rylands-street surgery, Mr. and Mrs. E. Rose and family, Mrs. Cbesters and family, Mrs. Hooper, Councillor and Mrs. W. H. Roberts, Dr. and Mrs. Bowden, Mr. and Mrs. W. Coninsby (Stockton Heath), Mr. , and Mrs. W. H. Cain Mr. and Mrs. Davenport Middle- burst Farm, Grappenllll), Mr. and Mrs. Lowe (Ashley Hall),Warrington Medico Ethical Society, Hearts of Oak Medical Aid Society, Independent Order of Rechabites (Stockton Heath), brethren of the "Lodge of Friend- : ship " (2863), from the Worshipful Masters, officers and brethren of the "Birkenhead ' Lodge (2826), Colonel . J. D. Fairclough and officers of the 4th Battalion, Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment), Sergeant-Major and staff of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, officers and men at headquarters of the Waarington Corps of the St. John Ambulance L Brigade, members of the mothers meeting of St. James', , Latchford, members of the nursing division of the St. John Ambulance Society. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr. % Ralph Johnson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
