      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-26_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>CORN.</head>
        <p>CORN.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER, THUit-SDT;Y-.—ln flour there was little doing, and wheat, both English and foreign' was on the easy side, also at late rates. Wheat: Fine Russian (Azima), Os 6d to 9s 7d; No. 2 Northern Manitoba, Os Old to Os 7d ; No 3 ditto, 9s. 4ld to 9s sd; No. 4 ditto, due, Os id to 9s Ild ' • No. 1 Durum, 9s to 9s Old; Northern River Plate,9s 4d to 9s sd; Australian, 9s 6d to Os Old per 100 lb. Oats: Cheshire' prime, 3s OA to 3s 6d-; ditto medium, es 2d to 3s 3d; foreign 2s 9d to 2s 91d per 451 b. Barley : Foreign, 3s sid to 9s 6d per 601 b. Arrivals : Wheat, 181,964 bushels ; Indian corn, 125,194 bushels; barley, 17,511 bushels; oats, 22,515 bushels; peas, 888cwts. LIVERPOOL, THURSDAY.—WbOftt : Futures are steady. Trading: Graded July Os to Os 02d, and sellers; September, 8s qej to 8s 61d, and sellers ; December, 8s 4-Id, to 8s 4gd, and sellers. Maize : Futures are steady. Trading: Plata, September 5s 7141 to 5s 7i:d, and value ; October, 5s 72d, and value, per oental.</p>
        <p>The Blue Coat School.</p>
        <p>The warm place which the Blue Coat School holds in the affections of old scholars was evidenced by the fact that ninety of them attended the annual Re-union Service for" Old Blues" which was held in the chapel attached to the institution on Sunday. Mr. and Mrs. Rose cordially welcomed the gathering which included :—The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Forshaw), Mr. W. Forshaw, and the Misses Forshaw, Mr. T. Armstrong,Messrs. J. Armstrong and S. Millett (former ushers in the school), Messrs. Rowe, Rutter, Smallwood, ,Povey, Essenhigh, &amp;c. The service was excellently rendered throughout and was thoroughly appreciated by a large congregation. Precisely at 6.30 the children entered the Chapel 6hanting as a processional Psalm lxxii. "I was glad when they said unto me, we will go into the house of the Lord. This was followed by the hymn "Children of the Heavenly King," after which the prayers were read by T. A. Kendrick. Then followed the portions of Scripture selected for recitation, that for the boy, Ernest Smith, being, selected from Judges iv., and that for the girl, Beatrice Beckett, being the Gospel for the day. The hymn, "Thy life was given for me," was then sung, after which followed the Catechism, the subject selected being "Prayer." The anthem, "Behold, how good and joyful a thing it is, brethren, to dwell together in unity," was creditably sung, the soloist being j. Wood. The remaining prayers were read by W. Rourthley,•and the service concluded with a rendering of the " Nunc Dimittis " and the ,hymn ," Lord, dismiss us with Thy blessing." Mr. J. L. 34illett, one of the teachers of, the institution, was at the organ.</p>
        <p>Children's Summer Camp. The pretty grounds of Darlington Lodge,Manchester- road, the residence of Mrs. Brookes, were the scene of a successful sale of work and garden fête in aid of the Warrington Poor Children's Summer Camp on Saturday afternoon. Fortunately the weather was favourable and enabled the large number of visitors to spend a very enjoyable afternoon. The following were the stall-holders :—Fancy and plain work stall : Mrs. Percival, Mrs. Harold Smith and Misses Edith Woods and Harding. Flower stall: Misses Sanke37, Whittle, Clarke and Millie Lowe. Photographic stall: Mrs. Lowe. Refreshment stall: Mrs. Proud, and the Misses Brookes, Smethurst, Trixie Sankey, Plinston and Woods. Ice-cream Stall : Mrs. Brookes (of Oughtrington), and Misses Woods and Edith and Greta Howard. Palmistry tent: Miss Gordon. "Aunt Sallie " stall: Mr. F. M. Darby. Rifle range: Mr. Frank Kertland and Mr. Isaac Sankey.— The fete was opened by the Hon. Mrs.. Willis, of Halsnead Park, amongst those present being the Rev. Canon and Mrs. Willis. Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst, Mrs. Forshaw (the Mayoress) and Miss Forshaw, the Rev. G. W. Dickenson, Mr. C. S. Burgess, Mr. Ransom° (treasurer of the camp) and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins (secretary).—The Hon. Mrs. Willis spoke very highly of the work of the summer camp, and a hearty vote of thanks was passed to her on the proposition of Mr. Jenkins seconded by Mr. Burgess.— Miss Barbara Brookes, daughter of Mrs. Brookes, of Oughtrington, presented Mrs. Willis with a handsome bouquet of sweet peas.—The attractions for the visitors included numerous garden games and com- petitions and two concerts arranged by Mr. T. Woods, were held in the drawing-room. The St. Barnabas' Orchestral Society, conducted by Mr. H. J. Iddon, gave some pleasing selectibns.—A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs. Brookes, for kindly lend- ing her grounds, on the proposition of Alderman Smethurst, seconded by Mr. Jenkins. A vote of thanks was also accorded to the Ladies' Committee.— A special word of praise is due to Miss Florence Lowe, Manchester-road, who as hon. secretary worked heart and soul for the success of the fete. Excellent work was done by a Ladies Committee consisting of Miss Brookes (Darling- ton Lodge), Miss A. Harding (Eustace-street), Miss Lowe (188, Manchester-road), Miss Perciyal (St. Peter's Vicarage), Misses Plinston (Stafford-road), Miss Sankey (Church-street), Misses Smethurst (Bewsey-place), Mrs. H. Smith (Bridge-street) and the Misses Woods (Winmarleigh-street).—The pkoceeds amounted to about £4O.</p>
